 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T11:09:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Buckley and Alan Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/492906f3-aeb0-5a72-9877-0e2effca7dd2', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/492906f3-aeb0-5a72-9877-0e2effca7dd2'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Manhattan socialite weds for the third time in 1940.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/1940-mabel-boll-queen-of-diamonds-weds-no-3/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Manhattan socialite weds for the third time in 1940.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fort Lauderdale, Fla. — Mabel Boll, one-time sales girl in a cigar store, who became known as ‘‘Queen of Diamonds,’’ has married again and husband No. 3 is Theodore Cella, thirty-nine-year-old harpist and assistant conductor of the New York Philharmonic Orchestra. From her humble beginning in Rochester, N.Y., as a dispenser of stogies and Colorado Claros the blue-eyed blonde Mibsey Bach, the daughter of a bartender, as she was then known, suddenly found herself in luxury as the wife of a wealthy Colombian. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/blogs/01iht-old02/01iht-old02-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 476, 'width': 366, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/blogs/01iht-old02/01iht-old02-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 476, 'width': 366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/blogs/01iht-old02/01iht-old02-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/blogs/01iht-old02/01iht-old02-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/blogs/01iht-old02/01iht-old02-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '1940: Mabel Boll, “Queen of Diamonds,” Weds No. 3', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diamonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T11:54:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/187eae41-7095-5881-9393-93ba3e473208', 'word_count': 190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/187eae41-7095-5881-9393-93ba3e473208'}, {'abstract': 'Q-Tip, from A Tribe Called Quest, shared the stage with Mr. White, who refuses to be a throwback.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/02/arts/music/jack-white-links-hip-hop-and-blues-at-madison-square-garden.html', 'snippet': 'Q-Tip, from A Tribe Called Quest, shared the stage with Mr. White, who refuses to be a throwback.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Feedback erupted time and again as Jack White headlined Madison Square Garden on Friday night. It was no accident; Mr. White’s music hangs on the edge of overload, and the feedback only proves it. The occasional electronic screeches were part of his joyful assault; so were the quivering whoop in his voice and the way his guitar solos rushed way up high and stayed there, to zoom around or wriggle and wail. And so was the flat-out bashing of the band’s drummer, Daru Jones, who often stood up to provide extra wallop.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/arts/02white/02white-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/02/arts/02white/02white-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/arts/02white/02white-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/arts/02white/02white-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/arts/02white/02white-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/02/arts/02white/02white-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/arts/02white/02white-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Blues and Hip-Hop Meet at the Hands of a Power Guitarist ', 'kicker': 'Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Blues and Hip-Hop Meet at the Hands of a Power Guitarist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Jack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Madison Square Garden', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blues Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Q-Tip (Rapper)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T11:54:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce5fbcd5-771d-5a95-b28c-5aad7b2766c1', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce5fbcd5-771d-5a95-b28c-5aad7b2766c1'}, {'abstract': 'Chateau Rhianfa in Wales is offering guests courses taught by a local language expert.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/learning-the-language-in-wales/', 'snippet': 'Chateau Rhianfa in Wales is offering guests courses taught by a local language expert.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chateau Rhianfa, on the Isle of Anglesey in Wales, is offering guests the chance to test their linguistic abilities by learning how to pronounce Llanfairpwllgwyngyllgogerychwyrndrobwllllantysiliogogogoch — the longest place name in Europe — among other words and phrases.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/29/travel/29wales/29wales-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/29/travel/29wales/29wales-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/29/travel/29wales/29wales-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/29/travel/29wales/29wales-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1282, 'width': 1930, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/29/travel/29wales/29wales-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/29/travel/29wales/29wales-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/29/travel/29wales/29wales-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Learning the Language in Wales', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wales', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T12:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Lieberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lieberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d59532f6-c6f5-5575-b0b2-13cb7153c1b4', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d59532f6-c6f5-5575-b0b2-13cb7153c1b4'}, {'abstract': 'After a scintillating start to the men’s final, Djokovic cruised in the final two sets to defeat Murray and claim a fifth title in Melbourne.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/02/sports/tennis/novak-djokovic-defeats-andy-murray-to-win-australian-open.html', 'snippet': 'After a scintillating start to the men’s final, Djokovic cruised in the final two sets to defeat Murray and claim a fifth title in Melbourne.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia — Huffing and stretching with full-throttle effort, ripping forehands from inside the baseline, Andy Murray for quite some time seemed to be a player on a mission to impose a new ending for himself at the Australian Open.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/sports/02TENNIS/02TENNIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/01/sports/02TENNIS/02TENNIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/sports/02TENNIS/02TENNIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/sports/02TENNIS/02TENNIS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1329, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/sports/02TENNIS/02TENNIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/01/sports/02TENNIS/02TENNIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/01/sports/02TENNIS/02TENNIS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Novak Djokovic Leaves Andy Murray Crushed in Reprise at Australian Open', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Reprise on Melbourne’s Stage, Djokovic Leaves Murray Crushed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clarey, Christopher', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Djokovic, Novak', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Andy (1987- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Australian Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T12:32:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2ece17d-18ed-566f-8727-95e45538d4d8', 'word_count': 1330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2ece17d-18ed-566f-8727-95e45538d4d8'}, {'abstract': 'After the Islamic State posted a video claiming to show the killing of the journalist Kenji Goto, the public has remained supportive of Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s stand against Mr. Goto’s kidnappers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/02/world/asia/japanese-hostage-beheading.html', 'snippet': 'After the Islamic State posted a video claiming to show the killing of the journalist Kenji Goto, the public has remained supportive of Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s stand against Mr. Goto’s kidnappers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Japan reacted with sorrow and outrage on Sunday to the posting by the Islamic State of a video purporting to show the grisly killing of the journalist Kenji Goto, bringing an end to a hostage standoff that has horrified this usually tranquil nation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/world/02hostages/02hostages-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/02/world/02hostages/02hostages-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 417}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/world/02hostages/02hostages-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/world/02hostages/02hostages-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1424, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/world/02hostages/02hostages-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/02/world/02hostages/02hostages-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/world/02hostages/02hostages-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hostage’s Apparent Beheading by ISIS Stirs Outrage in Japan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kidnapping and Hostages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goto, Kenji (1967- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T12:56:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler and Rod Nordland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rod', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nordland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc825f69-d098-551d-bb3c-28eb41bc9780', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc825f69-d098-551d-bb3c-28eb41bc9780'}, {'abstract': 'In school, nothing our son did was seen as typical. At home, I’m able to see him as more than a diagnosis, and he’s able to learn.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/home-schooling-and-teaching-a-child-with-down-syndrome/', 'snippet': 'In school, nothing our son did was seen as typical. At home, I’m able to see him as more than a diagnosis, and he’s able to learn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This past September we started home schooling. Before that our son, who has Down syndrome, attended public school in a regular classroom setting — often referred to as inclusion — at our request. After two years we realized school was hindering his education.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/us/motherlode-words/motherlode-words-articleLarge.png', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/01/30/us/motherlode-words/motherlode-words-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/us/motherlode-words/motherlode-words-jumbo.png', 'height': 449, 'width': 680, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/us/motherlode-words/motherlode-words-superJumbo.png', 'height': 449, 'width': 680, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/us/motherlode-words/motherlode-words-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/01/30/us/motherlode-words/motherlode-words-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/30/us/motherlode-words/motherlode-words-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Home Schooling, and Teaching, a Child with Down Syndrome', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Down Syndrome', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T13:09:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kari Wagner-Peck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wagner-Peck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4820e7a-5f7e-5711-ac42-69ee92fde7e3', 'word_count': 846, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4820e7a-5f7e-5711-ac42-69ee92fde7e3'}, {'abstract': 'A bus route in Silicon Valley has become a well-known overnight shelter for the homeless. The video journalist Elizabeth Lo rode Line 22, documenting its many patrons throughout the night.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/an-overnight-stay-at-hotel-22/', 'snippet': 'A bus route in Silicon Valley has become a well-known overnight shelter for the homeless. The video journalist Elizabeth Lo rode Line 22, documenting its many patrons throughout the night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Silicon Valley, one of the most expensive places to live in America, is also one of the toughest places for the homeless to survive. As the tech boom prompted property values to skyrocket, some of the city’s poorest residents have been displaced, with limited options for affordable housing. In this Op-Doc video, Elizabeth Lo documents scenes from a bus route that has become a well-known overnight shelter for the area’s homeless.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Overnight Stay at Hotel 22', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sundance Institute', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Jose (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T14:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52297a1a-1988-5e99-86d7-c6f8e91adab7', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52297a1a-1988-5e99-86d7-c6f8e91adab7'}, {'abstract': 'Welcome to the February 2015 Times Insider crossword puzzle pack.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/a-month-of-puzzle-love/', 'snippet': 'Welcome to the February 2015 Times Insider crossword puzzle pack.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TIMES INSIDER — Even in the coldest, darkest part of winter, we have cause to celebrate. The Super Bowl, President’s Day, Valentine’s Day, the Chinese New Year and many more holidays help us shake off the winter doldrums.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Month of Puzzle Love', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T14:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c0b4752-c2cd-50ce-9e4c-c4bd313db174', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c0b4752-c2cd-50ce-9e4c-c4bd313db174'}, {'abstract': 'Blogging is dead, long live blogging.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/floor-waxes-dessert-toppings-and-blogging/', 'snippet': 'Blogging is dead, long live blogging.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I see from Ezra Klein that Andrew Sullivan says that he’s stopping blogging; Klein and others are offering various encomiums. You’ll pardon me if I don’t join in. You see, I remember Sullivan declaring that the “decadent left” was poised to become a fifth column in the war on terror — and of course I remember the campaign of character assassination he waged against yours truly for daring to criticize his then-beloved George W. Bush and his wars. If he ever apologized for any of that, I never heard about it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Floor Waxes, Dessert Toppings, and Blogging', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T14:15:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2333235c-2357-5fee-b1b8-1218e9abc6f3', 'word_count': 839, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2333235c-2357-5fee-b1b8-1218e9abc6f3'}, {'abstract': 'Bowles what?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/the-disappearance-of-alan-simpson/', 'snippet': 'Bowles what?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not that long ago Alan Simpson bestrode Washington like a colossus — a rambling, cranky, potty-mouthed colossus. As Ezra Klein noted, reporters didn’t treat him as an ordinary public figure, but rather as the voice of obvious responsibility, on whose behalf the ordinary rules of press neutrality didn’t apply. And his pronouncements — like his early 2011 declaration that we faced fiscal crisis within two years — received breathless coverage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Disappearance of Alan Simpson', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-01T14:25:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15a69720-cf1b-584d-8ef1-00345f8d00c4', 'word_count': 87, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15a69720-cf1b-584d-8ef1-00345f8d00c4'}, {'abstract': 'In just a few days Ben Brantley sampled a rainbow spectrum of comedies, “My Night With Reg,” “The Play That Goes Wrong,” “The Ruling Class” and “Golem.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/london-theater-journal-laughing-wall-to-wall/', 'snippet': 'In just a few days Ben Brantley sampled a rainbow spectrum of comedies, “My Night With Reg,” “The Play That Goes Wrong,” “The Ruling Class” and “Golem.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Comedy comes in many shades, from jet black to shocking pink. In a rare moment of giddy confluence, the theaters here seem to have the whole rainbow on display.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/01/31/arts/31LAUGHING1/31LAUGHING1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'widt