_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Pennington', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pennington', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/519cbeef-9b49-5e47-b4e8-a36cf74d273a', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/519cbeef-9b49-5e47-b4e8-a36cf74d273a'}, {'abstract': 'This year’s highlights included the weepy teen dramedy “Me and Earl and the Dying Girl” and the David Foster Wallace biopic “The End of the Tour.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/02/t-magazine/highlights-sundance-festival-films.html', 'snippet': 'This year’s highlights included the weepy teen dramedy “Me and Earl and the Dying Girl” and the David Foster Wallace biopic “The End of the Tour.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A significant part of the fun of attending the Sundance Film Festival lies in trying to guess the dominating narratives beforehand. Will a star emerge from nowhere, à la Kevin Smith in “Clerks” or Quvenzhané Wallis in “Beasts of the Southern Wild”? Will we see anything as heartbreaking as “Blue Valentine” or as compelling as “Hoop Dreams”? What, if anything, will be this year’s “Boyhood”? There’s no real indicator for where the hits will come from at Sundance — and when they do, there’s a good chance you’ll still miss them, thanks to the festival’s breadth of programming.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/t-magazine/02sundance-oloizia-1/02sundance-oloizia-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/02/t-magazine/02sundance-oloizia-1/02sundance-oloizia-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit'