is walking back that claim, Ben Protess writes in DealBook. As part of a $1.37 billion settlement deal expected this week, S.&P. has agreed to acknowledge that it never found evidence supporting its accusations of retaliation, people briefed on the matter said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: S.&P. Walks Back ‘Retaliation’ Claims', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-02T12:26:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14b5fa25-aa35-527c-a267-27499bf95931', 'word_count': 2120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14b5fa25-aa35-527c-a267-27499bf95931'}, {'abstract': 'The science editor considers the problem of links that are a ‘dead end’', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/02/perfectly-reasonable-question-pay-walled-links/', 'snippet': 'The science editor considers the problem of links that are a ‘dead end’', 'lead_paragraph': 'A reader, Rich Dempsey, writes that he is “sick of articles that reference professional journals that hide behind a pay wall.” For example, he notes, in a recent Op-Ed on ageism, a link leads to an article from the Journal of Social Sciences that cannot be accessed without paying a hefty fee. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Perfectly Reasonable Question: Pay-Walled Links', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-02T13:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', '