id': 'nyt://article/5f07874e-de26-51bf-a291-058cea5289d5', 'word_count': 541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f07874e-de26-51bf-a291-058cea5289d5'}, {'abstract': 'Jody Lee Lipes’s “Ballet 422″ follows the rising choreographer Justin Peck as he prepares his piece “Paz de La Jolla” for the New York City Ballet.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/02/ballet-422-new-york-city-ballet-documentary-exclusive-video-clip/', 'snippet': 'Jody Lee Lipes’s “Ballet 422″ follows the rising choreographer Justin Peck as he prepares his piece “Paz de La Jolla” for the New York City Ballet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The uninitiated might see ballet as a sort of 19th-century relic, conjured to the stage by stern dance masters and ethereal waifs too beautiful to break a sweat. “Ballet 422,” a new documentary by the cinematographer-cum-filmmaker Jody Lee Lipes, which premieres tomorrow night at BAM, is an antidote to such illusions. Lipes, whose varied credits include “Martha Marcy May Marlene,” several episodes of “Girls,” a Jay Z video and the dance film “NY Export: Opus Jazz,” follows the rising choreographer Justin Peck as he creates an original work for the New York City Ballet (the company’s 422nd): “Paz de La Jolla,” a critically-lauded reverie set on the beaches of his native Southern California. Lipes captures the young dance-maker and his collaborators during every step of the two-month process, from the first choreographic sketches through costume and lighting design to the premiere. The result is a vivid, multifaceted depiction of the hard and necessary work of making art for the 21st century.\xa0In the exclusive clip above, Peck observes and corrects several of the company’s dazzlingly fast dancers during a rehearsal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Exclusive Clip From a New Dance Documentary by a “Girls” Director', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}