ank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-02T18:12:04+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/6b54d8c8-570e-5ded-91a9-0228c1224fbf', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/6b54d8c8-570e-5ded-91a9-0228c1224fbf'}, {'abstract': 'An appeals court ruling that narrows the grounds for insider trading cases may not be the cataclysm that the government contends it will be, Peter J. Henning writes in the White Collar Watch column.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2015/02/02/court-sets-up-a-hurdle-not-a-barrier-for-insider-trading-cases/', 'snippet': 'An appeals court ruling that narrows the grounds for insider trading cases may not be the cataclysm that the government contends it will be, Peter J. Henning writes in the White Collar Watch column.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is interesting to see how a few words in a judicial opinion can mean so much. One sentence in the recent appeals court decision in United States v. Newman that overturned the convictions of two hedge fund traders has caused the Justice Department and the Securities and Exchange Commission to cry foul and ask for its removal, contending the statement will imperil the government’s efforts to crack down on insider trading.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/02/business/dealbook/dbpix-collar/dbpix-collar-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/02/business/dealbook/dbpix-collar/dbpix-collar-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xla