3f-1f74e3cee321', 'word_count': 916, 'uri': 'nyt://article/505b3495-276e-5ba3-be3f-1f74e3cee321'}, {'abstract': '“The Human Symphony,” Dylan Marron’s new play, is performed by six audience members taking instruction from MP3 tracks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/03/theater/the-human-symphony-from-the-new-york-neo-futurists.html', 'snippet': '“The Human Symphony,” Dylan Marron’s new play, is performed by six audience members taking instruction from MP3 tracks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You don’t have to participate in “The Human Symphony,” a playful, patchy and compassionate show conceived by Dylan Marron, a member of the New York Neo-Futurists. When you check in, you can pretend to be under 18, or you can invent a medical condition (“I will break out in hives,” I heard one woman threaten), or you can simply decline. But six willing spectators will be plucked from the audience and invited to the stage. There, Mr. Marron will hand each of them an MP3 player with a specialized track dictating a particular set of instructions. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/03/arts/03HYMAN/03HYMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/03/arts/03HYMAN/03HYMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 778}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/03/arts/03HYMAN/03HYMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 791, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/03/arts/03HYMAN/03HYMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1296, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/03/arts/03HYMAN/03HYMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/03/arts/03HYMAN/03HYMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/03/arts/03HYMAN/03HYMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tales of Dating, Whose Audience Is Also the Cast', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tales of Dating, Whose Audience Is Also the Cast', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marron, Dylan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Neo-Futurists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Human Symphony (Play)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-02T21:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/960fca0d-654a-5e85-a992-3cd58c8a1f94', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/960fca0d-654a-5e85-a992-3cd58c8a1f94'}, {'abstract': 'Last week’s winter storm in the Northeast did only minimal damage to ticket sales for Broadway shows.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/02/how-broadway-weathered-last-weeks-storm/', 'snippet': 'Last week’s winter storm in the Northeast did only minimal damage to ticket sales for Broadway shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week’s winter storm in the Northeast did only minimal damage to ticket sales for Broadway shows, according to box-office data released on Monday. Broadway musicals and plays grossed $17.5 million for a total of 25 productions, compared to $16.7 million for 26 productions for the same week in January 2014. Attendance was 177,158 last week, compared to 183,092 a year ago. Shows benefited from the snow coming during a traditionally slow-selling week and on a night – Monday – when few performances are held anyway; and snowfall was below projections in New York City, leading most shows to resume performances on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Broadway Weathered Last Week’s Storm', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-02T21:46:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2880f0c-ce70-5255-a5da-bca2017be81d', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2880f0c-ce70-5255-a5da-bca2017be81d'}, {'abstract': 'Some states let doctors give antibiotics to a patient and to a nonpatient sexual partner. But not all doctors are comfortable treating someone they haven’t seen.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/02/s-t-d-care-for-two/', 'snippet': 'Some states let doctors give antibiotics to a patient and to a nonpatient sexual partner. But not all doctors are comfortable treating someone they haven’t seen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recently, while William, 21, was manning the chicken-wing fryer at a fast-fo