ed Monday by the police near the European Parliament, prompting the temporary evacuation of several buildings. The man, a Slovakian citizen born in 1982 and identified only as W.R., was dressed in camouflage and sought a meeting with the Parliament’s president, according to a statement by the Brussels prosecutor’s office. He was arrested, accused of threatening an attack and possessing unauthorized weapons. Belgium has been on alert since recent raids in which the authorities killed two men suspected of planning a terrorist attack. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Belgium: Man Accused of Threat Close to Parliament', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Belgium: Man Accused of Threat Close to Parliament', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Parliament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kanter, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Belgium', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evacuations and Evacuees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-03T01:04:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Kanter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kanter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3381661-5e0c-557b-8ed8-d3a87b62aae1', 'word_count': 96, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3381661-5e0c-557b-8ed8-d3a87b62aae1'}, {'abstract': 'The special United Nations investigator of human rights abuses in North Korea suggested that the ruling Kim family must be replaced before the country’s extensive network of prison camps can be broken up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/03/world/asia/north-korea-investigator-rules-out-reforms-by-kim.html', 'snippet': 'The special United Nations investigator of human rights abuses in North Korea suggested that the ruling Kim family must be replaced before the country’s extensive network of prison camps can be broken up.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The special United Nations investigator of human rights abuses in North Korea suggested in an interview published Monday that the ruling Kim family must be replaced before the country’s extensive network of prison camps can be broken up. The investigator, Marzuki Darusman, emphasized in the interview with The Associated Press that he was not advocating force to oust North Korea’s government. But in discussing how to end abuses, most notably in the North’s penal system, he was quoted as saying: “That can only happen if this cult leadership system is completely dismantled. And the only way to do that is if the Kim family is effectively displaced, is effectively removed from the scene, and a new leadership comes into place.” North Korea’s rights abuses were documented in a United Nations report published last year, which the government of Kim Jong-un has denounced as propaganda orchestrated by the United States.  ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'North Korea: Investigator Rules Out Reforms by Kim', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Korea: Investigator Rules Out Reforms by Kim', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong-un', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Darusman, Marzuki', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-03T01:06:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3de3e63a-ff63-5880-97ce-bd9ee4ba4096', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3de3e63a-ff63-5880-97ce-bd9ee4ba4096'}, {'abstract': 'The new contract, which will run through November 2018, provides for a new base minimum salary in the first year of $2,892 a week — the third highest in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/02/san-francisco-symphony-ratifies-musicians-contract/', 'snippet': 'The new contract, which will run through November 2018, provides for a new base minimum salary in the first year of $2,892 a week — the third highest in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a reminder that labor negotiations in the world of classical music are not always marked by disharmony, the San Francisco Symphony announced Monday that it had ratified a new contract that would keep its players among the best-paid in the world.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'San Francisco Symphony Ratifies Musicians’ Contract', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Francisco Symphony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-03T01:12:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastna