 'Margot', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SANGER-KATZ', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/c883d679-14c6-534f-8fd9-3975259331a4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/c883d679-14c6-534f-8fd9-3975259331a4'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/03/arts/television/whats-on-tv-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '2:30 P.M. (Sundance) THE KING’S SPEECH (2010) Colin Firth, in the role that won him an Oscar for best actor, portrays Albert Frederick Arthur George, or Bertie, as his family called him, the shy second son of George V of Britain (Michael Gambon) in this film from Tom Hooper. Geoffrey Rush is Lionel Logue, the speech therapist who helps Bertie conquer a debilitating stutter. And Helena Bonham Carter is Elizabeth, Albert’s wife and staunch support system, who can’t quite stomach Wallis Simpson (Eve Best), the American divorcée who leads Bertie’s older brother, David (Guy Pearce), to abdicate as Edward VIII. “It isn’t exactly ‘Pygmalion,’ not least because Mr. Hooper has no intention of satirizing the caste system that is one of this movie’s biggest draws,” Manohla Dargis, writing in The New York Times, said of the film, which received 12 Oscar nominations and also won for best director, best picture and original screenplay. “Like many entertainments of this pop-historical type,” she added, “\xa0‘The King’s Speech’ wears history lightly no matter how heavy the crown.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Tuesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Tuesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-03T06:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'sect