', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN ISRAEL PUBLIC AFFAIRS COMMITTEE', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-04T08:27:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas L. Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ee80c78-1a13-5040-b96b-71f679572982', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ee80c78-1a13-5040-b96b-71f679572982'}, {'abstract': 'Extending the full state minimum wage to tipped workers in New York would help combat poverty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/04/opinion/it-is-time-to-end-subminimum-wages.html', 'snippet': 'Extending the full state minimum wage to tipped workers in New York would help combat poverty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In New York, restaurants are allowed to pay servers a minimum wage of $5 an hour, as long as that hourly wage plus tips equals at least $8.75, the state minimum for nontipped workers (rising to $9 by 2016). That subminimum “tipped” wage is a vestige of the early days of modern labor law, when minimum-wage mandates were riddled with industry-specific exemptions, long since ended. It survives for tipped workers because of the strength of the restaurant lobby. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/04/opinion/04wed3webSUB/04wed3webSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/04/opinion/04wed3webSUB/04wed3webSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 39