glocations', 'value': 'Taipei (Taiwan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Taiwan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-04T14:52:56+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'Reuters', 'person': [], 'organization': 'Reuters'}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/6495bbef-9997-5a6a-831d-66d75bafc130', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/6495bbef-9997-5a6a-831d-66d75bafc130'}, {'abstract': 'Two new books about the aftermath of the assassination of the 16th president.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/mourning-lincoln-and-lincolns-body.html', 'snippet': 'Two new books about the aftermath of the assassination of the 16th president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Abraham Lincoln died on April 15, 1865, of a gunshot wound to the head. He was 56 years old and 6 feet 4 inches tall: a giant, felled. He was the 16th president of the United States and the first one killed in office. (A madman once tried to shoot Andrew Jackson, but both of his pistols misfired.) An assassination is treason by way of murder. Lincoln’s assassination was more — an act of war. Lee surrendered to Grant on April 9. Lincoln was the last of the Union dead. His death had another meaning, too. It founded a civic religion, dedicated to the memory of Lincoln’s sacrifice and to the proposition that emancipation is redemption.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08LEPORE-FAUST/08LEPORE-FAUST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08LEPORE-FAUST/08LEPORE-FAUST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 349}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08LEPORE-FAUST/08LEPORE-FAUST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 595, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08LEPORE-FAUST/08LEPORE-FAUST-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1190, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08LEPORE-FAUST/08LEPORE-FAUST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08LEPORE-FAUST/08LEPORE-FAUST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08LEPORE-FAUST/08LEPORE-FAUST-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Mourning Lincoln’ and ‘Lincoln’s Body’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'O Captain!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hodes, Martha', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fox, Richard Wightman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-04T14:57:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jill Lepore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lepore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e33dc04-2c3d-557d-9645-f8066806a08c', 'word_count': 1388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e33dc04-2c3d-557d-9645-f8066806a08c'}, {'abstract': 'In the four mischievous stories in “A Wonderful Year,” Nick Bruel adds madcap humor to a trip through the calendar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/04/books/review/nick-bruels-a-wonderful-year.html', 'snippet': 'In the four mischievous stories in “A Wonderful Year,” Nick Bruel adds madcap humor to a trip through the calendar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a very particular allure to a picture book that teeters on the verge of becoming a chapter book — that divides into three or more micro-stories featuring the same characters, with a title page for each vignette. James Marshall’s classic “George and Martha” books from the 1970s, with their hilariously bickering pair of hippos, are probably the gold standard, but there are many good examples from recent years, like Laura Vaccaro Seeger’s “Dog and Bear” books and Grace Lin’s “Ling and Ting” series.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/04/books/review/04childrens/04childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/04/books/review/04childrens/04childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/04/books/review/04childrens/04childrens-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/04/books/review/04childrens/04childrens-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1399, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/04/books/review/04childrens/04childrens-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/04/books/review/04childrens/04childrens-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/04/books/review/04childrens/04childrens-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Laugh for Every Season', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-04T15:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed93bf84-7a16-5e27-9edb-045ca17af08d', 'word_count': 586, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed93bf84-7a16-5e27-9edb-045ca17af08d'}, {'abstract': '“The Salt of the Earth,” about the photographer Sebastião Salgado, was made by Wim Wenders and Mr. Salgado’s son. But it was almost as if they were making two separate films.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/04/a-documentary-collaboration-that-didnt-start-out-promisingly/', 'snippet': '“The Salt of the Earth,” about the photographer Sebastião Salgado, was made by Wim Wenders and Mr. Salgado’s son. But it was almost as if they were making two separate films.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The German film director Wim Wenders is also a photographer with numerous exhibitions and books to his credit, and has collected the work of the Brazilian photographer Sebastião Salgado since the 1980s. But the two men met for the first time only about six years ago, and initially there was no talk of the collaboration that led to the making of “The Salt of the Earth,” one of five nominees for the Oscar for best feature documentary.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Documentary Collaboration That Didn’t Start Out Promisingly', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'u2018The Salt of the Earthu2019', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salgado, Juliano Ribeiro', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salgado, Sebastiao', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wenders, Wim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-04T15:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9876f26a-f1ce-561f-8ada-42d63c9265cf', 'word_count': 1263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9876f26a-f1ce-561f-8ada-42d63c9265cf'}, {'abstract': 'The society will present six complete string quartet cycles next season — including Beethoven’s 16 quartets, along with cycles by Bartok, Zemlinsky, Carl Nielsen, Alberto Ginastera and Leon Kirchner.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/04/chamber-music-society-of-lincoln-center-to-spotlight-string-quartet-works/', 'snippet': 'The society will present six complete string quartet cycles next season — including Beethoven’s 16 quartets, along with cycles by Bartok, Zemlinsky, Carl Nielsen, Alberto Ginastera and Leon Kirchner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center announced Wednesday that it would present six complete string quartet cycles next season — including Beethoven’s 16 quartets, along with cycles of quartets by Bartok, Zemlinsky, Carl Nielsen, Alberto Ginastera and Leon Kirchner.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center to Spotlight String Quartet Works', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-04T15:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63e7c761-4afe-5a89-8f6d-4f5e44006a59', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63e7c761-4afe-5a89-8f6d-4f5e44006a59'}, {'abstract': 'An education journalist makes the case against high-stakes standardized testing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/the-test-by-anya-kamenetz.html', 'snippet': 'An education journalist makes the case against high-stakes standardized testing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Teachers and their unions have resisted standardized testing since the early 20th century. Their critics have claimed they do so because they fear being held accountable for how much children learn. But over the past few years, on both the right and the left, in scattered suburbs and in urban neighborhoods, a small yet growing group of parents have begun to join teachers in denouncing standardized testing. They complain about the narrowing of the school curriculum to the questions asked on high-stakes exams, the many hours and days — sometimes up to a quarter of the school year — spent testing and prepping for tests, and the stress that testing imposes on children. Some parents even have their children opt out of standardized testing altogether.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-Goldstein/0208-bks-Goldstein-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 369, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-Goldstein/0208-bks-Goldstein-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 369}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-Goldstein/0208-bks-Goldstein-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 630, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-Goldstein/0208-bks-Goldstein-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1261, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-Goldstein/0208-bks-Goldstein-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75,