ighly desirable piece of Manhattan real estate is once again on the market. Its location — to use the name of the Stephen Adly Guirgis play opening at Second Stage on Feb. 11 — is “Between Riverside and Crazy,” and it is a place where distinctions of mental health and morality blur vividly. First seen in an Atlantic Theater Company production last summer, this constantly surprising work from Mr. Guirgis (author of the unprintably titled “The ______ With the Hat”) tells the story of a retired police officer (the wonderful Stephen McKinley Henderson) doing his best to hold onto to his dignity, his family and, most crucially, a rent-controlled apartment on the Upper West Side. Mr. Henderson’s character is called Pops, but this angry old man is far less cozy and more complicated than his folksy nickname suggests. The same is true of this play, directed by Austin Pendleton with a sharp ear for everyday urban ambivalence. (Through March 22, 305 West 43rd Street, Clinton, 2st.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE1/08GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE1/08GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE1/08GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE1/08GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image