r', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-04T23:02:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By First Draft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'First', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ec376b6-a95a-5d4a-8f63-c73140341b40', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ec376b6-a95a-5d4a-8f63-c73140341b40'}, {'abstract': 'Neither digital-world natives nor victims of the Great Recession, the children of the early ’80s deserve a different label.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/05/opinion/wait-what-im-a-millennial.html', 'snippet': 'Neither digital-world natives nor victims of the Great Recession, the children of the early ’80s deserve a different label.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The news that millennials will soon overtake baby boomers as the United States’ largest living generation disturbed me. Not the prediction itself, but the realization that, according to the standard definition, I am a millennial. Anyone born between 1981 and 1997 has been thrust into that category, and I was born in 1983. The label doesn’t feel right. I don’t identify with the kids that Time magazine described as technology-addled narcissists, the Justin Bieber fans who “boomerang” back home instead of growing up.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/05/opinion/05thu3web/05thu3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/05/opinion/05thu3web/05thu3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth':