nk': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a18d01d6-d98f-57c1-b6d1-55fdc5a46024', 'word_count': 651, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a18d01d6-d98f-57c1-b6d1-55fdc5a46024'}, {'abstract': 'The hidden beauty of an effective under-eye concealer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/05/fashion/a-sweet-blur-hides-all.html', 'snippet': 'The hidden beauty of an effective under-eye concealer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEE SHAPIRO — Somewhere amid attending law school, pulling corporate lawyer hours, switching careers and having a baby, I gave up on under-eye concealer. I had all the typical complaints — puffiness, dark circles and even the beginnings of something crepe-y — but concealer didn’t do much more than my foundation did. Besides, many under-eye concealers rely on radiance (a.k.a., shimmery stuff) to distract from imperfections. Instead I would pat-pat some liquid or powder base below my bloodshot peepers and call it a day. So you could say I was skeptical about the Giorgio Armani Maestro Eraser Dark Circle Concealer ($42).', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/05/fashion/05CONCEALER/05CONCEALER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1103, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/05/fashion/05CONCEALER/05CONCEALER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1103}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/05/fashion/05CONCEALER/05CONCEALER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 557, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/05/fashion/05CONCEALER/05CONCEALER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1114, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop