e': 'organizations', 'value': "Young Men's Christian Assn (YMCA)", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-05T12:30:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Cacciola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cacciola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fdaf7fc-7890-5b86-bfec-3cd8a49a06a9', 'word_count': 1236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fdaf7fc-7890-5b86-bfec-3cd8a49a06a9'}, {'abstract': 'In Anne Tyler’s new novel, “A Spool of Blue Thread,” a middle-class Baltimore family is shaken up by the unexpected return of its problem child.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/06/books/anne-tylers-20th-novel-a-spool-of-blue-thread.html', 'snippet': 'In Anne Tyler’s new novel, “A Spool of Blue Thread,” a middle-class Baltimore family is shaken up by the unexpected return of its problem child.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anne Tyler’s novels tend to walk a narrow line between the moving and the sentimental, the sympathetic and the maudlin, the evocative and the banal. Her best work — like “The Accidental Tourist,” “Breathing Lessons” and the ambitious “Digging to America” — gives us intimate portraits of middle-class life, tracing the centrifugal and centripetal forces of family that can propel individuals outward into the world at large or hold them close at home. Her lesser books, like “A Patchwork Planet” and “Noah’s Compass,” stumble into predictability and cliché. Ms. Tyler’s latest novel, “A Spool of Blue Thread,” unfortunately falls into that category. It recycles virtually every theme and major plot point she has used in the past and does so in the most perfunctory manner imaginable. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'm