ncesca Woodman, Ana Mendieta, Adrian Piper, Hannah Wilke, Urs Lüthi or Cindy Sherman, Ms. Guy turned the camera on herself. And like many of these artists, she photographed herself nude, assuming the position of both author and subject of the work.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 925, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 925}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 665, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1760, 'width': 1142, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4/06JANICEGUYGALLERY4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Janice Guy: ‘Janice With Camera’', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Janice Guy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guy, Janice (1953- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-05T21:02:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Schwendener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwendener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1234c345-95ab-5e0c-9170-800041181b8d', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1234c345-95ab-5e0c-9170-800041181b8d'}, {'abstract': 'Film shoots involving actors in police or military uniforms barred by Paris authorities after terror attacks.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/05/paris-calls-a-halt-to-outdoor-filming-of-some-action-scenes/', 'snippet': 'Film shoots involving actors in police or military uniforms barred by Paris authorities after terror attacks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS – Authorities have imposed new restrictions on filming movies or television shows in the city of Paris nearly a month after shootings that killed 17 people in and around the French capital. Outdoor scenes of actors in police or military uniforms and the use of fake weapons or explosives have been banned, underlining the heightened level of security after the attacks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Calls a Halt to Outdoor Filming of Some Action Scenes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johansson, Scarlett', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Charlie Hebdo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-05T21:07:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aurelien Breeden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aurelien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Breeden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9759f4f-64a6-5071-ae92-b2ae2739915b', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9759f4f-64a6-5071-ae92-b2ae2739915b'}, {'abstract': 'The director of the documentary “Ballet 422,” about the choreographer Justin Peck, discusses his work.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/05/jody-lee-lipes-narrates-a-scene-from-ballet-422/', 'snippet': 'The director of the documentary “Ballet 422,” about the choreographer Justin Peck, discusses his work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the documentary “Ballet 422,” the director Jody Lee Lipes chronicles the staging of a new dance by the choreographer Justin Peck, of New York City Ballet. This scene looks at a moment in the rehearsal process. Mr. Lipes discusses how the sequence was shot and what intrigues him about capturing the process of art creation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jody Lee Lipes Narrates a Scene From ‘Ballet 422’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lipes, Jody Lee', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peck, Justin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-05T21:11:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93e765d5-a4ce-5a8d-a331-a0032b1fbb17', 'word_count': 56, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93e765d5-a4ce-5a8d-a331-a0032b1fbb17'}, {'abstract': 'Two introductory exhibitions suggest that the transfer of the Corcoran Gallery’s artworks gives the National Gallery of Art not only new holdings but also new vigor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/06/arts/design/a-partial-look-at-the-corcoran-collection.html', 'snippet': 'Two introductory exhibitions suggest that the transfer of the Corcoran Gallery’s artworks gives the National Gallery of Art not only new holdings but also new vigor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — With the closing of the Corcoran Gallery of Art here, we’ve lost a venerable museum but gained access to an eclectic, in odd corners brilliant, time capsule of a collection. Long housed in a block-filling chunk of Beaux-Arts granite that also held an art school, the museum had limited exhibition space and could show only a fraction of its holdings of 17,000 works. Now nearly half of them are being absorbed into the National Gallery of Art. And maybe just as important, the remainder will be parceled out to other Washington-area museums, raising the chances that, with a little schlepping, we’ll eventually be able to see more of the Corcoran than we could when it was intact.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06NOTEBOOK/06NOTEBOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 911, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06NOTEBOOK/06NOTEBOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 911}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06NOTEBOOK/06NOTEBOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 675, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06NOTEBOOK/06NOTEBOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1349, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06NOTEBOOK/06NOTEBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06NOTEBOOK/06NOTEBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06NOTEBOOK/06NOTEBOOK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dusting Off Gems From the Attic', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dusting Off Gems From the Attic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Corcoran Gallery of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Gallery of Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-05T21:12:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname