0:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Mahler, Ravi Somaiya and Emily Steel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a268cd94-8e92-5c37-af97-35a03ed359d3', 'word_count': 1459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a268cd94-8e92-5c37-af97-35a03ed359d3'}, {'abstract': 'Future Brown, a bicoastal R&B group, has also collaborated with Pérez Art Museum Miami.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/05/future-brown-album-vernaculo/', 'snippet': 'Future Brown, a bicoastal R&B group, has also collaborated with Pérez Art Museum Miami.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everything about the R&B group Future Brown’s self-titled debut album, out Feb. 24 on Warp Records, comes across as perfectly calculated. The group strikes a balance between male and female vocals, between futuristic, icy beats and incisive lyrics and even between coasts: It consists of the Los Angeles producers Asma Maroof and Daniel Pineda (who also perform together as Nguzunguzu) and their New York counterparts, Fatima Al Qadiri and J-Cush. But in fact, according to Al Qadiri, the group’s modus operandi is less deliberate than it seems. “It’s all on accident,” as Al Qadiri says, backstage at a show at Los Angeles’ Catch One disco. “The thing that unites Future Brown is that there’s very little agenda.” The four D.J.s, who sit at the intersection of music, art and fashion, are linked through their ties to DIS Magazine, a tongue-in-cheek lifestyle webzine that Al Qadiri describes as an incubator for artists working in different mediums. “We’re indebted to curators that want to bring fresh air into the art world,” Al Qadiri says.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/05/t-magazine/05symonds-futureb/05symonds-futureb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/05/t-magazine/05symonds-futureb/05symonds-futureb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/05/t-magazine/05symonds-futureb/05symonds-futureb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/05/t-magazine/05symonds-futureb/05symonds-futureb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/05/t-magazine/05symonds-futureb/05symonds-futureb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/05/t-magazine/05symonds-futureb/05symonds-futureb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/05/t-magazine/05symonds-futureb/05symonds-futureb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Internet-Age Music Collective Beloved by Telfar and Hood by Air', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Al Qadiri, Fatima', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DIS Magazine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-05T22:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marissa G. Muller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marissa', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Muller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29fcb8aa-eff0-5254-a7a9-ee673a0dc2f6', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29fcb8aa-eff0-5254-a7a9-ee673a0dc2f6'}, {'abstract': 'The documentary “Call for Help” trails five people, all in their 20s, who went to Haiti after the 2010 earthquake as volunteers for a group calling itself Global DIRT.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/06/movies/call-for-help-documents-young-people-on-a-mission-in-haiti.html', 'snippet': 'The documentary “Call for Help” trails five people, all in their 20s, who went to Haiti after the 2010 earthquake as volunteers for a group calling itself Global DIRT.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the point of “Call for Help” is to glorify a handful of off-the-grid heroes, it fails. If the point is to follow some young people who took their aimless wanderlust to a trouble spot and perhaps created more problems than they solved, it succeeds.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06CALLFOR/06CALLFOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name'