2015/02/06/opinion/the-spreading-rage-at-isis.html', 'snippet': 'If regional fury translates into a broader fight against jihadism, the barbaric death of Moaz al-Kasasbeh may prove not to have been in vain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Terrorism has long had a gruesome role in conflicts, often among revolutionary groups so fanatically certain of their ends that they readily justify the most barbaric of means. But it can also turn with a vengeance against those who inflict it, as the Islamic State is learning with its most recently publicized atrocity — murdering a Jordanian air force pilot by burning him alive. The video of the killing was meant to dissuade Arabs from participating in the Western coalition against the group; instead, it has succeeded in fostering rage and revulsion against the jihadists throughout the Arab world. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/opinion/06fri2WEB/06fri2WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/06/opinion/06fri2WEB/06fri2WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 448}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/opinion/06fri2WEB/06fri2WEB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 765, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/opinion/06fri2WEB/06fri2WEB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1278, 'width': 1711, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/opinion/06fri2WEB/06fri2WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/06/opinion/06fri2WEB/06fri2WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/opinion/06fri2WEB/06fri2WEB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Spreading Rage at ISIS', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Spreading Rage at ISIS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kasasbeh, Moaz al-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jordan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-05T22:54:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/db6516f2-16d6-55d2-873c-a73bc9c842df', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db6516f2-16d6-55d2-873c-a73bc9c842df'}, {'abstract': 'In the art exhibition “Araya Rasdjarmrearnsook,” at SculptureCenter, videos featuring dogs and human corpses are used in discomfiting ways.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/06/arts/design/araya-rasdjarmrearnsook-a-sculpturecenter-exhibition.html', 'snippet': 'In the art exhibition “Araya Rasdjarmrearnsook,” at SculptureCenter, videos featuring dogs and human corpses are used in discomfiting ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a leave of absence in 2003, the Thai artist Araya Rasdjarmrearnsook returned to Chiang Mai University, where she teaches, apparently pregnant. Her friends and co-workers reacted joyfully to this surprising turn of events. Then, nine days later, she announced that she wasn’t with child at all, and that her fake pregnancy was a work of performance art called “The Nine-Day Pregnancy of a Single Middle-Aged Associate Professor.” She documented it in a fragmentary five-minute video, in which viewers hear her thoughts on pregnancy and about her colleagues’ disappointed and angry reactions to her deception. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06SCULPTURE1/SCULPTURE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06SCULPTURE1/SCULPTURE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06SCULPTURE1/SCULPTURE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06SCULPTURE1/SCULPTURE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1399, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06SCULPTURE1/SCULPTURE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06SCULPTURE1/SCULPTURE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201