t_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Samantha Stark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samantha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/4f760308-9031-5da0-aaa2-efd9837c4401', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/4f760308-9031-5da0-aaa2-efd9837c4401'}, {'abstract': 'Performing in recital at Alice Tully Hall, the Met tenor Matthew Polenzani sang works by Beethoven, Ravel, Satie and others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/06/arts/music/matthew-polenzani-of-the-met-opera-in-a-solo-recital-at-alice-tully.html', 'snippet': 'Performing in recital at Alice Tully Hall, the Met tenor Matthew Polenzani sang works by Beethoven, Ravel, Satie and others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The bright-toned, openhearted tenor Matthew Polenzani, who returned to his native New York in recital at Alice Tully Hall on Wednesday, rose through the ranks at the Metropolitan Opera the old-fashioned way. He started in the late 1990s in truly tiny roles — remind me, who’s Khrushchov in Mussorgsky’s “Boris Godunov”? — then gradually moved into minor but noticeable parts, the likes of Arturo in Donizetti’s “Lucia di Lammermoor” and Jaquino in Beethoven’s “Fidelio.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/05POLENZANI/POLENZANI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/05POLENZANI/POLENZANI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/05POLENZANI/POLENZANI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/05POLENZANI/POLENZANI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/05POLENZANI/POLENZANI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/05POLENZANI/POLENZANI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/05POLENZANI/POLENZANI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Setting Well Suited to Humility', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Setting Well Suited to Humility', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Polenzani, Matthew', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T00:43:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/86227080-33fb-5ace-9c2a-9e880b3003d3', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86227080-33fb-5ace-9c2a-9e880b3003d3'}, {'abstract': 'Being clever isn’t enough.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/05/a-difficult-technical-subject-personal-meta/', 'snippet': 'Being clever isn’t enough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his incredible essay “The Great Slump of 1930” – an essay that reads remarkably well to this day, as an analysis of our crisis as well as his – Keynes briefly vents a bit of frustration. “For — though no one will believe it—economics is a technical and difficult subject.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Difficult, Technical Subject (Personal Meta)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T00:45:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75394a4a-d4c3-5b1e-a2ce-86cfa18cca83', 'word_count': 558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75394a4a-d4c3-5b1e-a2ce-86cfa18cca83'}, {'abstract': 'In the aftermath of the Charlie Hebdo killings, there are new questions about the concept of state secularism and whether it is being fairly applied in a France where an estimated five million Muslims live and worship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/06/world/old-tradition-of-secularism-clashes-with-frances-new-reality.html', 'snippet': 'In the aftermath of the Charlie Hebdo killings, there are new questions about the concept of state secularism and whether it is being fairly applied in a France where an estimated five million Muslims live and worship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROSNY-SOUS-BOIS, France — Laïcité, the concept of state secularism, is a defining principle of the French republic, right up there with the national motto of liberty, equality and fraternity. Developed in the French Revolution, which targeted the Roman Catholic Church as much as the monarchy, laïcité governs the public life of a nation that sharply delineates the realms of Caesar and God.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/world/SECULAR/SECULAR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/06/world/SECULAR/SECULAR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/world/SECULAR/SECULAR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/world/SECULAR/SECULAR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1357, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/world/SECULAR/SECULAR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/06/world/SECULAR/SECULAR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/world/SECULAR/SECULAR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Old Tradition of Secularism Clashes With France’s New Reality ', 'kicker': 'Memo From Rosny-Sous-Bois', 'content_