ness with the publication of her second novel, “Go Set a Watchman,” amid growing speculation that she may have been pressured into signing over publication rights for the book.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/06/books/harper-lee-says-shes-excited-for-new-novel.html', 'snippet': 'The author of “To Kill a Mockingbird” has issued a statement expressing her happiness with the publication of her second novel, “Go Set a Watchman,” amid growing speculation that she may have been pressured into signing over publication rights for...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harper Lee has issued a new statement expressing her happiness with the publication of her second novel, “Go Set a Watchman,” amid growing speculation that the fiercely private novelist may have been pressured into signing over publication rights for the book.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Statement, Harper Lee Backs New Novel ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Statement, Harper Lee Backs New Novel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Harper', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Go Set a Watchman (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T01:02:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Alter and Serge F. Kovaleski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Serge', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Kovaleski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9892ed47-686e-58a4-885d-5f31429ef900', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9892ed47-686e-58a4-885d-5f31429ef900'}, {'abstract': 'There are strategies for limiting potential damage, although all involve greater vigilance and at least some degree of inconvenience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/06/business/protecting-yourself-from-the-consequences-of-anthems-data-breach.html', 'snippet': 'There are strategies for limiting potential damage, although all involve greater vigilance and at least some degree of inconvenience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Given the steady beat of security breaches, consumers may assume that a cybercriminal already has at least some of their personal information. But in the latest intrusion, at the health insurer Anthem, hackers got their hands on an especially valuable collection of sensitive information on millions of people.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Protecting Yourself From the Consequences of Anthem’s Data Breach ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Protecting Yourself From the Consequences of Anthem’s Data Breach', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernard, Tara Siegel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ANTHEM INC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Identity Theft', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T01:09:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1196bdfa-b26e-56a9-a472-3a68cbfbe49e', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1196bdfa-b26e-56a9-a472-3a68cbfbe49e'}, {'abstract': 'In “Seventh Son,” Jeff Bridges roars, Julianne Moore fumes, and Kit Harington leaves early. There are also dragons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/06/movies/seventh-son-stars-jeff-bridges-julianne-moore-and-kit-harington.html', 'snippet': 'In “Seventh Son,” Jeff Bridges roars, Julianne Moore fumes, and Kit Harington leaves early. There are also dragons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every year, in between the big heave known as the fall awards season and that folly we call the Academy Awards, the major movie companies turn into a cinematic slag heap of the bad, the risible and the inexplicable. Even on paper, the latest February throwaway, “Seventh Son,” sounds like a bad idea, even if it does feature the wasted talent of the veteran production designer Dante Ferretti and was directed by Sergei Bodrov, who has a few fine credits to his name. As it turns out, there are those, including this reviewer, who can be seduced into seeing just about anything if it comes with name actors like Jeff Bridges and Julianne Moore, a herd of digital dragons and the cute guy who plays that incurably know-nothing Jon Snow in the HBO show “Game of Thrones.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06SEVENTH1/SEVENTH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 251, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/06/arts/06SEVENTH1/SEVENTH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 251}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag