: 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/15218913-dc9e-5e9e-8e6a-b105a0b75378', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15218913-dc9e-5e9e-8e6a-b105a0b75378'}, {'abstract': 'Clancy Martin fuses personal memoir and philosophy in this study of deceit, especially in romantic relationships.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/love-and-lies-by-clancy-martin.html', 'snippet': 'Clancy Martin fuses personal memoir and philosophy in this study of deceit, especially in romantic relationships.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it comes to personal life, even the smartest people, those most capable of irony, humor and subtlety, may well show a weakness for the kind of thinking that precludes all three. This is one explanation for “Love and Lies: An Essay on Truthfulness, Deceit, and the Growth and Care of Erotic Love,” a new and remarkably insight-free book of self-reflection by the novelist and philosophy professor Clancy Martin.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08WALDMAN/0208-bks-WALDMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 547, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08WALDMAN/0208-bks-WALDMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 547}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08WALDMAN/0208-bks-WALDMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 934, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08WALDMAN/0208-bks-WALDMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1867, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08WALDMAN/0208-bks-WALDMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08WALDMAN/0208-bks-WALDMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08WALDMAN/0208-bks-WALDMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Love and Lies,’ by Clancy Martin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Confessions of a Two-Timer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Clancy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adelle Waldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d95e4ed-7fd7-5525-9269-322d6f7d7a73', 'word_count': 1339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d95e4ed-7fd7-5525-9269-322d6f7d7a73'}, {'abstract': 'For two young girls — in the Jim Crow South and on an island in Lake Erie — the world changes suddenly and irrevocably.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/stella-by-starlight-and-moonpenny-island.html', 'snippet': 'For two young girls — in the Jim Crow South and on an island in Lake Erie — the world changes suddenly and irrevocably.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Childhood may be the easiest time to go to bed as one person and wake up feeling like someone else. In two new middle-\xadgrade novels, young heroines discover that adults are not always truthful, and that the world is more dangerous than anyone has been letting on. They’re life-\xadchanging realizations, for sure, and in both books they present an opportunity to find a moral compass.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Stella by Starlight’ and ‘Moonpenny Island’', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Coming Into Their Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Draper, Sharon M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Springstubb, Tricia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ford, Gilbert (1977- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Goldberg Sloan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Goldberg', 'lastname': 'Sloan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/442b34f7-1c7f-53de-808d-e2a7e0202801', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/442b34f7-1c7f-53de-808d-e2a7e0202801'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KINDER THAN SOLITUDE,  by Yiyun Li. (Random House, $16.) Li’s intricate mystery spans the two decades after the Tiananmen crackdown of 1989. Shaoai, a brash student protester in Beijing, is poisoned, and three members of her circle are implicated: Ruyu, her orphaned teenage cousin, and their friends Moran and Boyang. As Shaoai lingers for years, brain-damaged and uncommunicative, the three become separated by distance and estrangement, haunted by what happened in their youth.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ce148fa-ecf3-5f1e-b935-5be47509e331', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ce148fa-ecf3-5f1e-b935-5be47509e331'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GUANTÁNAMO DIARY,  by Mohamedou Ould Slahi. Edited by Larry Siems. (Little, Brown, $29.) A longtime captive has written the most profound and disturbing account yet of what it’s like to be collateral damage in the war against terror.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d18077e-6fda-5b25-b093-4127de2e0860', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d18077e-6fda-5b25-b093-4127de2e0860'}, {'abstract': 'First published in 1987, Magda Szabo’s novel follows the relationship between two very different women in Communist Hungary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/the-door-by-magda-szabo.html', 'snippet': 'First published in 1987, Magda Szabo’s novel follows the relationship between two very different women in Communist Hungary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Magda Szabo, who died in 2007, was one of Hungary’s most important 20th-\xadcentury writers. Not that most of us Anglophones would know it, as very little of her work has been translated into English. “The Door,” her best-known novel, which appeared in Hungary in 1987, was initially translated by Stefan Draughon and brought out here by an academic publisher in 1995. Subsequently translated into French, the book won the Prix Femina Étranger in 2003 and was beautifully retranslated by Len Rix for British publication in 2005. A decade later, New York Review Books Classics — acting, yet again, in its capacity as the Savior of Lost Greats — has now delivered this version to an American audience.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08MESSUD/08messud-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 862, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08MESSUD/08messud-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 862}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08MESSUD/08messud-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 713, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08MESSUD/08messud-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1177, 'width': 819, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08MESSUD/08MESSUD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08MESSUD/08MESSUD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08MESSUD/08MESSUD-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Door,’ by Magda Szabo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fierce Devotions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Szabo, Magda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rix, Len (1942- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Messud', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Messud', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f43a703-2e07-5119-876f-63d07ac7000d', 'word_count': 1249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f43a703-2e07-5119-876f-63d07ac7000d'}, {'abstract': 'The novelist and Nobel laureate Mo Yan depicts the horrors of China’s one-child policy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/mo-yans-frog.html', 'snippet': 'The novelist and Nobel laureate Mo Yan depicts the horrors of China’s one-child policy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In October 2012, Mo Yan became the first citizen of mainland China to be awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature. Since then, he has been attacked both inside and outside China for his collaboration with the Communist literary establishment: for his vice chairmanship of the state writers’ association, for remarking that censorship falls in the same category as airport security. In the summer of 2012, he controversially hand-copied, for a special commemorative edition, part of the 1942 “Talks at the Yan’an Forum on Literature and Art,” Mao Zedong’s statement of orthodoxy on the arts that became the theoretical ur-text of Chinese socialist realism and literary censorship. Yet readers will find little in “Frog,” Howard Gold\xadblatt’s fluent translation of Mo Yan’s 2009 novel about his country’s one-child policy, that validates the society created by the Chinese Communist Party. It is an anarchic, brutal book about the inhumanity of servants of the Communist state, the inadequacy of Chinese men and the moral vacuum at the heart of post-Mao China.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 140, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 140}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 239, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 478, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub/0208-bks-LOVELL-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mo Yan’s ‘Frog’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Missing Children', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mo Yan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldblatt, Howard (1939- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Lovell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d46bdd2-a28f-5365-a000-773877f4838c', 'word_count': 1220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d46bdd2-a28f-5365-a000-773877f4838c'}, {'abstract': 'These works of biography and historical fiction immerse young readers in the 19th- and 20th-century struggles for equal rights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/my-name-is-truth-chasing-freedom-and-the-case-for-loving.html', 'snippet': 'These works of biography and historical fiction immerse young readers in the 19th- and 20th-century struggles for equal rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At home in the world. The first time I heard this expression, I reveled in the very idea of it. It felt like the perfect life goal for all humankind: a feeling of complete belongingness and freedom, unconstrained by society’s perceptions of your capabilities, wherever you happen to be — at home or in public. The phrase resonates in three new picture books that highlight issues of race, gender and justice. These stories — about Sojourner Truth, Harriet Tubman and Susan B. Anthony, and the Loving v. Virginia case that struck down laws prohibiting interracial marriage — all have main characters with a steadfast determination to change their lives. They are willing to fight their known hell for an unknown, possibly harsher fate. In each book, the law looms as a character, too, a force that can save lives or kill spirits. And all three books are, in different ways, interracial tales, reminding us that diverse coalitions have always worked together to achieve racial progress.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08RUSSELLBROWN1/08RUSSELLBROWN1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 934, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08RUSSELLBROWN1/08RUSSELLBROWN1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 934}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08RUSSELLBROWN1/08RUSSELLBROWN1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 658, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08RUSSELLBROWN1/08RUSSELLBROWN1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1981, 'width': 1272, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08RUSSELLBROWN1/08RUSSELLBROWN1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08RUSSELLBROWN1/08RUSSELLBROWN1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08RUSSELLBROWN1/08RUSSELLBROWN1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘My Name Is Truth,’ ‘Chasing Freedom’ and ‘The Case for Loving’', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Liberty and Justice for All', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turner, Ann', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grimes, Nikki', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wood, Michele', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Alko, Selina', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tubman, Harriet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTHONY, SUSAN B', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qualls, Sean', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ransome, James E', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katheryn Russell-Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katheryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russell-Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/45825a2d-2890-58fc-8636-0671894a980e', 'word_count': 1143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45825a2d-2890-58fc-8636-0671894a980e'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to recent reviews of “When Books Went to War,” “The Forgotten Depression” and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/letters-books-at-war.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to recent reviews of “When Books Went to War,” “The Forgotten Depression” and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Books at War', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: Books at War', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e87ef03-71bc-5d97-bf3d-f0990fb50485', 'word_count': 948, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e87ef03-71bc-5d97-bf3d-f0990fb50485'}, {'abstract': 'Jill Leovy, whose “Ghettoside” is No. 8 on the hardcover nonfiction list, once kept a blog tracking every murder in Los Angeles County.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/15/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Jill Leovy, whose “Ghettoside” is No. 8 on the hardcover nonfiction list, once kept a blog tracking every murder in Los Angeles County.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Equal Weight: Jill Leovy’s “Ghettoside,” a true-crime narrative about a murder case in South Los Angeles that opens into a larger exploration of black-on-black violence, enters the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 8. Leovy is a longtime reporter for The Los Angeles Times, where for years she maintained the Homicide Report blog to track every murder in the county, in an effort to “communicate the horror of it,” as she said on NPR’s “Fresh Air” recently. “I don’t like blogs very much,” she told the host Dave Davies. “You can scan a newspaper front page and get an idea of what you need to be thinking about very quickly. A blog is just a stack, an undifferentiated stack of news. You can’t tell the trivial from the weighty. And so it doesn’t work for a lot of things — but it occurred to me that it would work for this, that the form would suit what I was trying to say exactly, which is to give everything equal weight. . . . This is not a new story about the sensational case, about the case getting attention. This is just all of them stacked up in a row so that the reader can peruse them and get an idea of who’s dying.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08leovy/08leovy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08leovy/08leovy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08leovy/08leovy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08leovy/08leovy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08leovy/08leovy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42299a56-c833-580e-bc5d-01f931556d2f', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42299a56-c833-580e-bc5d-01f931556d2f'}, {'abstract': 'Two books for young adults explore the guilt, anger and overwhelming sadness that follow a teenager’s death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/the-last-time-we-say-goodbye-and-i-was-here.html', 'snippet': 'Two books for young adults explore the guilt, anger and overwhelming sadness that follow a teenager’s death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This review comes with a disclaimer: The subject is radioactively sad. There are no silver linings or surprise resurrections in either of these devastating novels in which teenagers take their own lives. Even if you think you have a high tolerance for gut-wrenching fiction, the tragedy may knock you flat.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Last Time We Say Goodbye’ and ‘I Was Here’', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Among the Survivors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forman, Gayle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hand, Cynthia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fbb6857-6157-59df-84d3-affca2fa89a2', 'word_count': 719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fbb6857-6157-59df-84d3-affca2fa89a2'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Brookhiser casts Lincoln as what might today be called an originalist, a leader who acted in accordance with the founders’ intentions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/founders-son-a-life-of-abraham-lincoln-by-richard-brookhiser.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Brookhiser casts Lincoln as what might today be called an originalist, a leader who acted in accordance with the founders’ intentions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2012, Ford’s Theater, the site of Lincoln’s assassination, erected an unusual tribute to the 16th president: a 34-foot tower, eight feet around, created from some 7,000 of the more than 15,000 books about Lincoln that have been published since his death. Richard Brookhiser, the award-winning author of biographies of the founding fathers, now adds to that list, venturing past the Revolution into a new era to explore the life of this most written-about American. In many ways, however, this is less a book in which Brookhiser moves forward in time than one that endeavors to pull Lincoln back into the 18th century, claiming him, in the title’s words, as fundamentally the “founders’ son.” Brookhiser casts Lincoln as what might today be called an originalist, a leader who applied the doctrines of both the Declaration of Independence and the Constitution in accordance with the founders’ intentions in order to chart his way through a new national crisis.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08FAUST/08faust-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1003, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08FAUST/08faust-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1003}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08FAUST/08faust-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 613, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08FAUST/08faust-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1225, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08FAUST/08FAUST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08FAUST/08FAUST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08FAUST/08FAUST-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Founders’ Son: A Life of Abraham Lincoln,’ by Richard Brookhiser', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Founders’ Son’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brookhiser, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Drew Gilpin Faust', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Drew', 'middlename': 'Gilpin', 'lastname': 'Faust', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbd5b531-dc59-5356-9576-ac4530c1626d', 'word_count': 1145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbd5b531-dc59-5356-9576-ac4530c1626d'}, {'abstract': 'A critic rereads “Poets in Their Youth,” Eileen Simpson’s memoir about John Berryman and his circle of friends, and sees Simpson as a heroic figure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/rereading-eileen-simpsons-poets-in-their-youth.html', 'snippet': 'A critic rereads “Poets in Their Youth,” Eileen Simpson’s memoir about John Berryman and his circle of friends, and sees Simpson as a heroic figure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'W. H. Auden said that a great book reads you. Eileen Simpson’s beautiful, recently reissued memoir of her doomed marriage to the poet John Berryman, “Poets in Their Youth” (1982), read me twice, just a few weeks ago and about 30 or so years before that, when I was in my early 20s. I might well have been two different people.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08SIEGEL/08siegel-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08SIEGEL/08siegel-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 853}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08SIEGEL/08siegel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 720, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08SIEGEL/08siegel-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 1406, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08SIEGEL/08siegel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08SIEGEL/08siegel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/books/review/08SIEGEL/08siegel-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Poet’s Keeper', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Take', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Poet’s Keeper', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Siegel, Lee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simpson, Eileen (1918-2002)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berryman, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lee Siegel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Siegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c091f13-5d8c-527b-a2d1-e74f0324e72e', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c091f13-5d8c-527b-a2d1-e74f0324e72e'}, {'abstract': 'A young-adult novel based on the black leader’s early years, co‑written by one of his daughters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/books/review/x-a-novel-about-malcolm-x.html', 'snippet': 'A young-adult novel based on the black leader’s early years, co‑written by one of his daughters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It would be fascinating to get Malcolm X’s take on the current surge of racial tension in America. At first glance, we seem to have made great strides toward equality since the civil rights movement. We elected Barack Obama, a black man, president. Twice. We almost universally condemn outright racism. But is it possible that racism (most notably against African-\xadAmericans) hasn’t receded so much as it has evolved and become more refined? “The elegant racist,” Ta-Nehisi Coates has written, “knows how to injure nonwhite people while never summoning the specter of white guilt.” Housing segregation, for instance, is both devastating and hard to detect. And according to the Sentencing Project, a group that advocates for prison reform, racial minorities are more likely than whites to be arrested, to be convicted and to face stiff sentences. With recent racially charged events like the deaths of Michael Brown in Ferguson, Mo., and Eric Garner in New York City — as well as the rising plea for more representation of minorities in children’s literature from the We Need Diverse Books movement — I can’t think of a more appropriate time for a book about the early years of Malcolm X.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘X,’ a Novel About Malcolm X', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Becoming Malcolm X', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shabazz, Ilyasah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Magoon, Kekla', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malcolm X', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-06T16:12:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt de la Peña', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de la Peña', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca3a278f-7c38-5ee4-bcce-8ec14aa46e15', 'word_count': 749, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca3a278f-7c38-5ee4-bcce-8ec14aa46e15'}, {'abstract': 'New books include “Love Always Everywhere” and “There’s This Thing.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2015/02/06/books/review/08love-bookshelf.html', 'snippet': 'New books include “Love Always Everywhere” and “There’s This Thing.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'New books include “Love Always Everywhere” and “There’s This Thing.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/06/books/review/08love-bookshelf-slide-SX8Z/08love-bookshelf-slide-SX8Z-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'art