00', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/340ff56b-f2c9-54f2-9624-07847842a2ef', 'word_count': 2159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/340ff56b-f2c9-54f2-9624-07847842a2ef'}, {'abstract': 'When the Department of Education audits universities, reports of sex crimes rise dramatically. More transparency would be useful.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/opinion/sunday/confusion-about-college-sexual-assault.html', 'snippet': 'When the Department of Education audits universities, reports of sex crimes rise dramatically. More transparency would be useful.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The exact size of the campus sexual assault problem remains unclear. The commonly cited statistic that one in five women who attend college is assaulted before she graduates — repeated by the White House — comes from a flawed 2007 study based on undergraduates at just two unnamed public universities. That figure often shocks, yet there is no reliable alternative estimate. Under the federal Clery Act universities are required to publish data on campus crime, but activists have long suspected that administrators underreport sex crimes. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/opinion/08sun3WEB/08sun3WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/opinion/08sun3WEB/08sun3WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/opinion/08sun3WEB/08sun3WEB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'super