tly what we’re thinking. We have to disguise our feelings, to feint, to smile sometimes when we want to shout. In short, we have to lie. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08VALENTINE/08VALENTINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 635, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08VALENTINE/08VALENTINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 635}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08VALENTINE/08VALENTINE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 967, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08VALENTINE/08VALENTINE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08VALENTINE/08VALENTINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08VALENTINE/08VALENTINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08VALENTINE/08VALENTINE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Good Lovers Lie ', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Good Lovers Lie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lying', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Relationships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Love (Emotion)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Valentine's Day", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-07T19:30:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clancy Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2f1aff6-536f-5f7d-9fa9-8d77ec5359d3', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2f1aff6-536f-5f7d-9fa9-8d77ec5359d3'}, {'abstract': 'To avoid “he-said, she-said” vaccine coverage, it’s important to clearly state the settled science.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/opinion/sunday/another-outbreak-of-false-balance.html', 'snippet': 'To avoid “he-said, she-said” vaccine coverage, it’s important to clearly state the settled science.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON the list of journalistic subjects that New York Times readers care about most, false balance is surely in the top 10. It may even be in the top three, right after fairness and accuracy.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Outbreak of ‘False Balance’?', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Another Outbreak of ‘False Balance’?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Editor (Times Column)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Margaret M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Measles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-07T19:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Public Editor', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/07edd1c9-1f6e-599d-b455-eb46027568a9', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07edd1c9-1f6e-599d-b455-eb46027568a9'}, {'abstract': 'Lawmakers have been putting their political interests ahead of good stewardship of public money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/opinion/sunday/the-pentagons-excess-space.html', 'snippet': 'Lawmakers have been putting their political interests ahead of good stewardship of public money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the past three years, officials at the Pentagon have asked Congress for permission to take stock of how many of the military’s vast network of installations across the country have become obsolete and ought to be shrunk or shuttered. The Defense Department, by far the nation’s largest and costliest bureaucracy, estimates that it could operate far more efficiently and save billions of dollars each year by shedding at least 20 percent of its real estate. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/07/opinion/08sun1WEB/08sun1WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/07/opinion/08sun1WEB/08sun1WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargehe