udiciary', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-07T19:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Shulevitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shulevitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c421c920-5ec4-5d0d-b7a0-471b015be1df', 'word_count': 1064, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c421c920-5ec4-5d0d-b7a0-471b015be1df'}, {'abstract': 'The president’s focus on past sins has diminishing returns in the present.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/opinion/sunday/ross-douthat-obama-the-theologian.html', 'snippet': 'The president’s focus on past sins has diminishing returns in the present.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PRESIDENT OBAMA, like many well-read inhabitants of public life, is a professed admirer of Reinhold Niebuhr, the famous mid-20th-century Protestant theologian. And more than most presidents, he has tried to incorporate one of Niebuhr’s insights into his public rhetoric: the idea that no society is innocent, and that Americans in particular need to put aside illusions about our own alleged perfection. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/douthat-circular/douthat-circular-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/douthat-circular/douthat-circular-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/douthat-circular/douthat-circular-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height':