type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Lichtblau', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lichtblau', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3ad07ad-d5b4-5dbf-8612-21d0048119e4', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3ad07ad-d5b4-5dbf-8612-21d0048119e4'}, {'abstract': 'Stop demanding attention. Be like the arctic fox.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/opinion/sunday/how-to-be-invisible.html', 'snippet': 'Stop demanding attention. Be like the arctic fox.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN one of the most popular ads shown during the Super Bowl last weekend, the Indian-American actress Mindy Kaling thinks she’s become invisible. Cabs pass her by, and men barely look up on the sidewalk. And yet she embraces her invisibility as a kind of liberation. More of us should do the same. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08INVISIBLEsub/08INVISIBLEsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 488, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08INVISIBLEsub/08INVISIBLEsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 488}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08INVISIBLEsub/08INVISIBLEsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 833, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08INVISIBLEsub/08INVISIBLEsub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1667, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08INVISIBLEsub/08INVISIBLEsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08INVISIBLEsub/08INVISIBLEsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/sunday-review/08INVISIBLEsub/08INVISIBLEsub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Be Invisible ', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Be Invisible', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Busch, Akiko', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foxes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camouflage', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-07T19:41:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Akiko Busch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Akiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Busch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f048224d-b64f-5816-b3d0-9c3b1d55162f', 'word_count': 700, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f048224d-b64f-5816-b3d0-9c3b1d55162f'}, {'abstract': 'As blood sports go, the Super Bowl has nothing on the Colosseum and its events.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/arts/television/ancient-savagery-and-how-it-worked.html', 'snippet': 'As blood sports go, the Super Bowl has nothing on the Colosseum and its events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As blood sports go, the Super Bowl has nothing on the Colosseum and its events. Built near Nero’s onetime pleasure palace by the emperor Vespasian as a symbol of Roman military might, the amphitheater (capacity: 50,000 or so) boasted gladiators, wild beasts, crucifixions and elaborately staged Greek myths that often didn’t end well for the performers, as when criminals were catapulted above the arena to recreate Icarus’s flight toward the sun. The pièce de résistance might be a mock sea battle, the better for mass drownings, in which the amphitheater would be flooded in the morning and drained in time for the afternoon mayhem. But how, exactly, was all this achieved? With a labyrinthine system of underground pulleys, lifts and trap doors to rival the most elaborate Broadway show, says Heinz-Jürgen Beste of the German Archaeological Institute in Rome. In “Colosseum: Roman Death Trap,” the first installment of “Building Wonders” on “Nova,” Wednesdays on PBS, Mr. Beste proves his point, recreating an ancient lifting machine to release a wolf into the arena. (Check local listings.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ancient Savagery, and How It Worked', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Television: Ancient Savagery, and How It Worked', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-07T20:01:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aeb1c916-0942-5d76-aa9e-9cdaf0f2fdd8', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aeb1c916-0942-5d76-aa9e-9cdaf0f2fdd8'}, {'abstract': 'Gluck’s operas don’t get enough hearings, but the Juilliard School has done well by him in its collaborations with the Metropolitan Opera.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/arts/music/from-iphigenie-to-modern-morsels.html', 'snippet': 'Gluck’s operas don’t get enough hearings, but the Juilliard School has done well by him in its collaborations with the Metropolitan Opera.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gluck’s operas don’t get enough hearings, but the Juilliard School has done well by him in its collaborations with the Metropolitan Opera. The early-music specialist Jane Glover conducted a Met-Juilliard “Armide” in 2012, and she returns with semi-staged performances of “Iphigénie en Aulide” on Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday. The cast includes Yunpeng Wang, Liv Redpath, Ying Fang, Andrew Stenson, Takaoki Onishi and Brandon Cedel. (8 p.m., Tuesday and Thursday, and 2 p.m. Saturday, Peter Jay Sharp Theater, Lincoln Center, 212-769-7406, juilliard.edu.) On Monday, the brilliant keyboard performer Richard Egarr plays Bach and Handel at Carnegie Hall. (7:30 p.m., Weill Recital Hall, carnegiehall.org.) The same night, the New York Philharmonic’s Contact! series offers new music from Israel jointly presented by the 92nd Street Y, including chamber pieces by Josef Bardanashvili, Avner Dorman, Yotam Haber and Shulamit Ran. (7:30 p.m., SubCulture, 45 Bleecker Street, near Lafayette Street, East Village; 212-875-5656, nyphil.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE5/08GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE5/08GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE5/08GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE5/08GUIDE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1369, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE5/08GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE5/08GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE5/08GUIDE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From ‘Iphigénie’ To Modern Morsels', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classical: From ‘Iphigénie’ to Modern Morsels', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-07T20:01:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/190676fa-1a01-5071-bb44-0fea151d63e4', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/190676fa-1a01-5071-bb44-0fea151d63e4'}, {'abstract': 'John Boorman, now in his 80s, may not be the most prolific director in movie history, but he has certainly shown versatility.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/movies/weeklong-tribute-to-a-genre-juggler.html', 'snippet': 'John Boorman, now in his 80s, may not be the most prolific director in movie history, but he has certainly shown versatility.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Boorman, now in his 80s, may not be the most prolific director in movie history, but he has certainly shown versatility, with a résumé that includes the crime drama “Point Blank,” the fantasy “Excalibur” and the lighthearted “Hope and Glory.” Film Forum begins a weeklong tribute to him on Wednesday that includes screenings of those three films and several others, including the movie that provided perhaps the biggest jolt of all, “Deliverance,” the 1972 survival adventure based on James Dickey’s novel.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE8/08GUIDE8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE8/08GUIDE8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE8/08GUIDE8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE8/08GUIDE8-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1840, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE8/08GUIDE8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE8/08GUIDE8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE8/08GUIDE8-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Weeklong Tribute to a Genre Juggler', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Film: Weeklong Tribute to a Genre Juggler', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-07T20:01:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f81c3181-459d-5074-b4dc-e97e3e9042f7', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f81c3181-459d-5074-b4dc-e97e3e9042f7'}, {'abstract': 'Masculinity and tradition can seem like stable formulas in modern country, but there will always be those who complicate the math.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/arts/music/outliers-defying-easy-definition.html', 'snippet': 'Masculinity and tradition can seem like stable formulas in modern country, but there will always be those who complicate the math.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Masculinity and tradition can seem like stable formulas in modern country, but there will always be those who complicate the math. Sam Hunt is a former football star whose debut album, “Montevallo” (MCA Nashville), is chock-full of aw-shucks entreaties — including some, like “Take Your Time,” that sound about as indebted to R&B as to any country norm. On Wednesday, he performs at Irving Plaza. (7 p.m., irvingplaza.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE2/08GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE2/08GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE2/08GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE2/08GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE2/08GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE2/08GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE2/08GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Outliers Defying Easy Definition', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop: Outliers Defying Easy Definition', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-07T20:01:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54e2b795-9399-5ef9-9099-64224900806f', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54e2b795-9399-5ef9-9099-64224900806f'}, {'abstract': 'In 1881, a year after opening its building on Fifth Avenue at 82nd Street, the Metropolitan Museum of Art received its first official bequest of Japanese art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/08/arts/design/glories-of-japan-aglow-at-the-met.html', 'snippet': 'In 1881, a year after opening its building on Fifth Avenue at 82nd Street, the Metropolitan Museum of Art received its first official bequest of Japanese art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1881, a year after opening its building on Fifth Avenue at 82nd Street, the Metropolitan Museum of Art received its first official bequest of Japanese art: a collection formed by Stephen Whitney Phoenix, scion of a New York merchant and political family. The Met has since amassed a world-class Japanese collection. That story will be told in “Discovering Japanese Art: American Collectors and the Met,” opening at the museum on Saturday. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE4/08GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1284, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/08/arts/08GUIDE4/08GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1284}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/0