ion': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c00f151-4400-5bcd-97ac-99cb8ead298d', 'word_count': 814, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c00f151-4400-5bcd-97ac-99cb8ead298d'}, {'abstract': 'In Monroeville, the town square has murals depicting scenes from “To Kill a Mockingbird,” and for several weeks each year, residents perform a play based on the book, acting out some scenes in a former courthouse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2015/02/09/books/20150208HARPER.html', 'snippet': 'In Monroeville, the town square has murals depicting scenes from “To Kill a Mockingbird,” and for several weeks each year, residents perform a play based on the book, acting out some scenes in a former courthouse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Monroeville, the town square has murals depicting scenes from “To Kill a Mockingbird,” and for several weeks each year, residents perform a play based on the book, acting out some scenes in a former courthouse.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/08HARPER-slide-HTL7/08HARPER-slide-HTL7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/08HARPER-slide-HTL7/08HARPER-slide-HTL7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/08HARPER-slide-HTL7/08HARPER-slide-HTL7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/08HARPER-slide-HTL7/08HARPER-slide-HTL7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/08HARPER-slide-HTL7/08HARPER-slide-HTL7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/08HARPER-slide-HTL7/08HARPER-slide-HTL7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/08HARPER-slide-HTL7/08HARPER-slide-HTL7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Monroeville, Ala., Home of Harper Lee', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Harper', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Go Set a Watchman (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'To Kill a Mockingbird (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-08T17:09:29+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/61db51c8-1802-5fbf-bbde-1a1986fcfa6b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/61db51c8-1802-5fbf-bbde-1a1986fcfa6b'}, {'abstract': 'The plan to publish “Go Set a Watchman” has ignited fierce debate, but Ms. Lee’s lawyer, Tonja B. Carter, and other friends say the author is capable of making up her own mind.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/09/books/harper-lee-lawyer-offers-more-details-on-discovery-of-go-set-a-watchman.html', 'snippet': 'The plan to publish “Go Set a Watchman” has ignited fierce debate, but Ms. Lee’s lawyer, Tonja B. Carter, and other friends say the author is capable of making up her own mind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONROEVILLE, Ala. — One morning late last summer, Tonja B. Carter was doing some legal work for her prized client, Harper Lee, when she found herself thumbing through an old manuscript of what she assumed was “To Kill a Mockingbird.” The characters were familiar, as they would be to millions of readers — the crusading lawyer, Atticus Finch, and his feisty daughter, Scout. But the passages were different. Atticus was much older. Scout was grown up. The story unfolded in Alabama during the racial turmoil of the 1950s, not the Depression of the 1930s.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Harper Lee Novel Surfaces, Plots Arise', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Harper Lee Novel Surfaces, Plots Arise', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Harper', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Go Set a Watchman (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'To Kill a Mockingbird (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HarperCollins Publishers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Tonja B', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-08T17:14:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Alter and Serge F. Kovaleski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Serge', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Kovaleski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bcccd91-33db-5fa8-8653-1794d4575ad6', 'word_count': 1772, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bcccd91-33db-5fa8-8653-1794d4575ad6'}, {'abstract': 'The singer D’Angelo, back in full swing after a long hiatus, brought his version of soul to the Apollo Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/09/arts/music/dangelos-apollo-theater-show-featured-signature-songs-and-style.html', 'snippet': 'The singer D’Angelo, back in full swing after a long hiatus, brought his version of soul to the Apollo Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the end of the night on Saturday, one by one, D’Angelo’s band members took leave of the Apollo Theater stage. They’d been playing “Untitled (How Does It Feel),” the solemn declaration of lust that had, thanks to its erotic video 15 years ago, transformed D’Angelo from soul music savior to something far trickier, and sweatier. It has become the song that, for many, defines this singer. And it has at times been the song that D’Angelo runs away from.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09DANGELOWEB1/09DANGELOWEB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09DANGELOWEB1/09DANGELOWEB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09DANGELOWEB1/09DANGELOWEB1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09DANGELOWEB1/09DANGELOWEB1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumb