'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mahler, Donald', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ogura, Rie', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-08T22:50:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/112e5722-ec73-5e6b-9699-1488f1cac95d', 'word_count': 334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/112e5722-ec73-5e6b-9699-1488f1cac95d'}, {'abstract': '“Cambuyón,” a percussive music-and-dance show at the New Victory Theater, incorporates clogging, tap, hip-hop and salsa in one fast-paced hour.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/09/arts/dance/cambuyon-a-percussive-show-at-new-victory-theater.html', 'snippet': '“Cambuyón,” a percussive music-and-dance show at the New Victory Theater, incorporates clogging, tap, hip-hop and salsa in one fast-paced hour.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Cambuyón,” a percussive dance and music show that opened at the New Victory Theater on Friday, rhythm comes from many sources: wooden crates, metal buckets, matchsticks, glass bottles, kitchen utensils, feet slapping the floor and hands slapping the body. The concept bears more than a little resemblance to “Stomp,” but in this production by Enlace Servicios Culturales, a company based in the Canary Islands and in Barcelona, all of the tapping and clanging happens aboard a ship whose location in time and space remains open to interpretation. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09CAMBUYON/09CAMBUYON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09CAMBUYON/09CAMBUYON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09CAMBUYON/09CAMBUYON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09CAMBUYON/09CAMBUYON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1403, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09CAMBUYON/09CAMBUYON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09CAMBUYON/09CAMBUYON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09CAMBUYON/09CAMBUYON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Play Space for Rhythm Set on the High Seas', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Play Space for Rhythm Set on the High Seas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Victory Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Enlace Servicios Culturales', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-08T22:53:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siobhan Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siobhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/83cd512d-cab6-50ab-9766-205355ff90fa', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83cd512d-cab6-50ab-9766-205355ff90fa'}, {'abstract': 'The choreographer, performer and musician Steven Reker of Brooklyn is the winner of the American Dance Institute’s second annual Solange MacArthur Award for New Choreography.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/09/arts/dance/brooklynite-wins-choreography-award.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographer, performer and musician Steven Reker of Brooklyn is the winner of the American Dance Institute’s second annual Solange MacArthur Award for New Choreography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The choreographer, performer and musician Steven Reker of Brooklyn is the winner of the American Dance Institute’s second annual Solange MacArthur Award for New Choreography, organizers of that Maryland-based nonprofit announced Sunday. The award recognizes a rising American choreographer with financial and administrative support for presenting a commissioned work. Mr. Reker said in a statement that the award, which includes a $10,000 commission, would allow him to create “a piece I have wanted to make for years but could only imagine making since my resources are somewhat limited in NYC.” Mr. Reker was selected by the institute’s artistic advisory board — which includes the choreographers Brian Brooks, David Dorfman and Doug Elkins — after a closed nomination process. The award is named for Solange MacArthur, a former member of American Ballet Theater and supporter of the institute, who died in 2012. The first recipient was Chris Schlichting of Minnesota.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brooklynite Wins Choreography Award', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brooklynite Wins Choreography Award', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Dance Institute (ADI)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reker, Steven (1982- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-08T22:56:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/aab75710-07f2-5491-a4f9-9705778a33b0', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aab75710-07f2-5491-a4f9-9705778a33b0'}, {'abstract': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum surpassed its goal of raising 2.5 million pounds ($3.8 million) to purchase the four statues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/09/arts/design/wolsey-angels-to-go-to-the-va-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum surpassed its goal of raising 2.5 million pounds ($3.8 million) to purchase the four statues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum surpassed its goal on Sunday of raising 2.5 million pounds ($3.8 million) to purchase four statues created by the Italian sculptor Benedetto da Rovezzano for the tomb of Cardinal Thomas Wolsey, an adviser to Henry VIII and once one of England’s most powerful men. The statues, known as the Wolsey Angels, were commissioned in 1524, just before the cardinal lost his power after failing to convince the pope to annul the king’s marriage. The cardinal appeared in Hilary Mantel’s novel “Wolf Hall.” Ms. Mantel said in a statement that the recovery of the angels “is one of those miracles that historians pray for; something that seems irrevocably lost has been there all the time.” After Wolsey’s death in 1530, Henry VIII acquired the statues for his own tomb, which was never completed. They were eventually sold during the 1640s to raise money during England’s Civil War and were unheard of again until two surfaced at an auction in 1994. The other pair was discovered in 2008 at a country house in Northamptonshire in central England. The majority of the funds for the purchase came from a two-million pound grant from the National Heritage Memorial Fund, while another 500,000 pounds came from the Art Fund, a charity that helps British museums purchase art. Additional funds came from donations and museum supporters — including 33,000 pounds from on-site donations and one-pound “Save the Wolsey Angels” badges. The statutes are on display at the museum but will be temporarily removed to undergo conservation treatment.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09ARTS4/09ARTS4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 981, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09ARTS4/09ARTS4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 981}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09ARTS4/09ARTS4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 627, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/09ARTS4/09ARTS4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1253, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/09/arts/