sibility of a depolarizing pope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Soon after Jorge Bergoglio became Pope Francis, I wrote several\xa0pieces\xa0exploring the idea that the new pope\xa0might be striving to effectively transcend the liberal/conservative civil war that’s dominated Catholic life in the West since the Second Vatican Council, and find a new synthesis or center for the church somewhere beyond that post-1960s conflict.\xa0I have not written as much on that theme in the last year, mostly because of what’s been happening with the debate around divorce and communion: There the pope’s choices have at least temporarily added fuel to Catholicism’s internal conflict, rather than cooling it, in ways that threaten to overshadow other elements of his agenda, other possibilities for his time as Peter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pope Francis As Unifier', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beatifications and Canonizations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Priests', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benedict XVI', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'John Paul II', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shining Path', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Latin America', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-09T18:36:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 