'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef27c751-e294-534d-9e94-4245056ef22f', 'word_count': 364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef27c751-e294-534d-9e94-4245056ef22f'}, {'abstract': 'Writing as Harry Brandt, Richard Price follows characters, many of them New York detectives, haunted by, and hunting for, unpunished criminals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/10/books/review-in-the-whites-richard-price-tries-on-a-pseudonym-in-a-world-of-brooding-cops.html', 'snippet': 'Writing as Harry Brandt, Richard Price follows characters, many of them New York detectives, haunted by, and hunting for, unpunished criminals.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Whites,” the title of Richard Price’s riveting new novel, is a reference to the great white whale in “Moby-Dick” — a book that seems to be a kind of touchstone for the author (writing here under the pen name Harry Brandt), having popped up, at least glancingly, in such earlier novels as “Clockers” and “Freedomland.” This time, the parallels to Melville’s classic are beyond obvious: All of the story’s main characters are haunted by something terrible that happened in the past, and all are obsessed with hunting down their nemeses.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/10/arts/10BOOK/10BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 913, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/10/arts/10BOOK/10BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 913}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/10/arts/10BOOK/10BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 674, 's