article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ernesto Londo&#241;o', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ernesto', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Londo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34258448-d812-58c2-a804-b614f2329179', 'word_count': 477, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34258448-d812-58c2-a804-b614f2329179'}, {'abstract': 'Teams are trying to extract as much money as possible from fans for whom cost is no barrier, even as they improve the game experience for fans in the least expensive seats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/10/business/arenas-hit-status-into-the-cheap-seats.html', 'snippet': 'Teams are trying to extract as much money as possible from fans for whom cost is no barrier, even as they improve the game experience for fans in the least expensive seats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It wasn’t long ago that fans at sporting events had only a handful of ticket options: Box seats, reserved seats and general admission seats, with perhaps a premium for the most desirable locations, like behind home plate or on the 50-yard line. Clubs and suites were often tucked into the ends of stadiums and the upper decks.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/10/business/10ARENAS_combo/10ARENAS_combo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 484, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/10/business/10ARENAS_combo/10ARENAS_combo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 484}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/10/business/10ARENAS_combo/10ARENAS_combo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 826, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'le