graph': 'This week, the trial of the man accused of killing Chris Kyle opens in a Texas courtroom, even as the Clint Eastwood film based on Mr. Kyle’s life, “American Sniper,” is playing in a theater three miles away. Much as Mr. Kyle’s death shocked the nation, the film has generated fierce debate nationally over the meaning of his life and his death, and the Iraq war itself. To some, Mr. Kyle represents all that was right with the American-led invasion, to others, all that was wrong. Yet to many veterans, his story offers a chance to discuss and debate a remarkable array of complex and personal questions: the mix of motivations that lead people to sign up in the military, the riot of emotions troops feel when they kill or witness death, the struggle to reengage with civilian society upon coming home. A number of people have sent At War essays about how they viewed the film, including the piece below by a former Marine. What do you think? Send us your thoughts: atwar@nytimes.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/10/us/atwar-sniper/atwar-sniper-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 851, 'width': 525, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/10/us/atwar-sniper/atwar-sniper-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 851, 'width': 525, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/10/us/atwar-sniper/atwar-sniper-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/10/us/atwar-sniper/atwar-sniper-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/10/us/atwar-sniper/atwar-sniper-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Many Veterans, ‘American Sniper,’ Right or Wrong, Starts an Important Conversation', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kyle, Chris (1974-2013)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Navy Seals', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marine Corps', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-10T18:37:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas W. Jackson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Jackson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd2fd6b5-c249-5a60-9bb1-ea8dbc3e43bc', 'word_count': 1280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd2fd6b5-c249-5a60-9bb1-ea8dbc3e43bc'}, {'abstract': 'It’s back, with a new campaign from Senator Rand Paul (whose father led the earlier version) to push for a bill to do just that in a now-Republican-led Congress.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2015/02/10/from-the-upshot-the-fed-a-beacon-of-openness/', 'snippet': 'It’s back, with a new campaign from Senator Rand Paul (whose father led the earlier version) to push for a bill to do just that in a now-Republican-led Congress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What People Mean When They Say ‘Audit the Fed’', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From The Upshot: The Fed, a Beacon of Openness', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-10T19:20:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By First Draft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'First', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bedbc568-01a3-5bd1-a023-cfe3db65a779', 'word_count': 9, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bedbc568-01a3-5bd1-a023-cfe3db65a779'}, {'abstract': 'It’s time for a joint solution between Greece, which has more debt than it can ever repay, and its creditors.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/10/will-greece-ever-get-out-of-debtors-prison/', 'snippet': 'It’s time for a joint solution between Greece, which has more debt than it can ever repay, and its creditors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The efforts of the new Greek government to write off a portion of its debt are being cast by powerful European policy makers as a bid for “debt forgiveness.” But the whole concept of “forgiveness” implies that there is a magnanimous creditor and a profligate borrower, and that’s just not true. Greece now has more debt than it can ever repay, and lenders share some of the blame for this. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Greece Ever Get Out of Debtor’s Prison?', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, '