': 'organizations', 'value': 'Waterfront NY Realty Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'High Line (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chelsea (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meyer, Danny', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-10T20:01:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'subsection_name': 'Commercial Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. J. Hughes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Hughes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3145aea5-1661-57af-812a-2ce96f2a50cc', 'word_count': 1087, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3145aea5-1661-57af-812a-2ce96f2a50cc'}, {'abstract': 'On the problem with comprehensive moral judgments of complicated historical conflicts.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/10/the-case-against-the-case-against-the-crusades/', 'snippet': 'On the problem with comprehensive moral judgments of complicated historical conflicts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In\xa0my Sunday column on the controversy surrounding President Obama’s National Prayer Breakfast speech, I tried to explain what I saw as the problems with the president’s “high horse” references to Western Christian sins without letting the column get\xa0sucked down too deep into the quicksand of historical-moral controversy.\xa0But now (because what else are blogs for?) let’s step a little deeper into that quicksand, and talk a little bit about the Crusades, the most contested of the various examples — the others were Jim Crow, slavery, and the Inquisition\xa0— that the president cited as Islamic State-like case studies in which Christianity was\xa0used as a license for cruelty and persecution. The references to slavery and segregation inspired\xa0debates about how deeply religion was implicated in those institutions\xa0(the short answer: a lot more deeply than many Christian conservatives care\xa0to admit today, though not in exactly the same way that religion is implicated in the crimes of ISIS; the South was many things, including a home to terrorism, but it was not a utopian theocracy); the reference to the Inquisition inspired some discussion of\xa0just how bad the institution really was (the answer: not as bad as Black Legend-style\xa0history would have it, but at its worst quite, quite bad enough). But only the Crusades inspired a debate about whether they deserve to be condemned\xa0at all.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case Against the Case Against the Crusades', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crusades (Christianity)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pilgrimages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Segregation and Desegregation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Henry V, King of England', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Central Europe', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jerusalem (Israel)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhineland (Germany)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rouen (France)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sicily (Italy)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-10T20:02:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e30c6a8-2ce4-5998-87ab-1a9c3d44cfc6', 'word_count': 1566, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e30c6a8-2ce4-5998-87ab-1a9c3d44cfc6'}, {'abstract': 'The lineup includes the horror documentary “The Nightmare” and “He Never Died,” featuring Henry Rollins as an immortal cannibal.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/10/sxsw-film-festival-announces-midnight-slate/', 'snippet': 'The lineup includes the horror documentary “The Nightmare” and “He Never Died,” featuring Henry Rollins as an immortal cannibal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The midnight slate at this year’s South by Southwest film festival will include haunted houses, unpleasant dinner parties, not-so-sweet dreams and a little bit of cannibalism. Festival organizers announced today the horror and genre-focused collection of movies that will round out their feature lineup: 11 films that range from the weird to the terrifying.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/10/movies/sxsw-hangman/sxsw-hangman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'a