_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Space & Cosmos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenneth Chang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenneth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00a327a1-3d88-539b-afd7-41f4159a91d9', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00a327a1-3d88-539b-afd7-41f4159a91d9'}, {'abstract': 'The Manhattan District Attorney announced the indictments of 50 people involved in the building business in New York, and the lineup is stunning.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/10/new-yorks-building-horror-stories/', 'snippet': 'The Manhattan District Attorney announced the indictments of 50 people involved in the building business in New York, and the lineup is stunning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Almost anyone trying to develop a building or repair a brownstone or hold onto a budget-rate apartment in New York City has a horror story to tell.\xa0 \xa0For the builders, it’s often about strange, excruciating delays and rules that seem straight out of the Charles Dickens Circumlocution Office. For the tenants, it’s the way landlords always seem to have a way with the authorities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York’s Building Horror Stories', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vance, Cyrus R Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Buildings Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Investigation (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-11T00:37:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eleanor Randolph', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eleanor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Randolph', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e99e59b1-370c-5810-9e64-b58e121118fb', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e99e59b1-370c-5810-9e64-b58e121118fb'}, {'abstract': 'The move is a departure for Yelp, whose main business has been selling advertising bought by local businesses with those reviews.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/11/business/yelp-pays-134-million-for-food-ordering-service.html', 'snippet': 'The move is a departure for Yelp, whose main business has been selling advertising bought by local businesses with those reviews.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The online review service Yelp said on Tuesday that it had acquired Eat24, an online food-ordering start-up, for $134 million in cash and stock. The move is a departure for Yelp, whose main business has been selling advertising bought by local businesses with those reviews. But Yelp’s growth is starting to slow, and it reported a decrease in regular visitors in its most recent quarter. In a statement, Jeremy Stoppelman, Yelp’s chief executive, said the purchase would help Yelp customers more easily do business with companies online. Yelp will have plenty of competitors. GrubHub, an online food ordering company and a longtime competitor of Eat24, recently acquired DiningIn and Restaurants on the Run, two delivery services that take food directly to customers who order it through the company. Yelp also faces start-ups like Postmates, which enables customers to order food from almost any restaurant for delivery, and Square, which recently bought the food delivery start-up Caviar. Bigger competitors may be coming, too. Amazon and Google have experimented with on-demand delivery services, and Uber, the ride-hailing start-up, has signaled its own ambitions of becoming a way to deliver anything to anyone. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yelp Pays $134 Million for Food-Ordering Service', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yelp Pays $134 Million for Food-Ordering Service', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yelp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eat24.com LLC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Delivery Services', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-11T00:47:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Isaac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isaac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cd2f840-9f89-5be4-bd53-fbfb04e2a412', 'word_count': 190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cd2f840-9f89-5be4-bd53-fbfb04e2a412'}, {'abstract': '“There are plenty of collectors who can take a painting that big,” a New York dealer said. “There are private museums opening up all over the world.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/10/richter-leads-sothebys-london-contemporary-art-sales/', 'snippet': '“There are plenty of collectors who can take a painting that big,” a New York dealer said. “There are private museums opening up all over the world.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Gerhard Richter abstract painting sold Tuesday night at Sotheby’s contemporary art auction in London f