d, we’re getting in line.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COKER, Ala. — MONDAY morning began like any other morning on our little farm in Alabama, where we have lived together for more than three decades. We already knew that Roy S. Moore, the chief justice of the State Supreme Court, had commanded the state’s probate judges to refuse to grant marriage licenses to same-sex couples. But turning on the radio, we learned that the judge in our county, Tuscaloosa, home to the state’s flagship university, had decided to go along with him. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11eichelbergermillsweb/11eichelbergermillsweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11eichelbergermillsweb/11eichelbergermillsweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11eichelbergermillsweb/11eichelbergermillsweb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11eichelbergermillsweb/11eichelbergermillsweb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11eichelbergermillsweb/11eichelbergermillsweb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11eichelbergermillsweb/11eichelbergermillsweb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11eichelbergermillsweb/11eichelbergermillsweb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Quiet Fight to Marry', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Alabama, a Quiet Fight to Marry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mills, Jean', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eichelberger, Carol', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Roy S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tuscaloosa (Ala)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-11T08:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jean Mills and Carol Eichelberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mills', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eichelberger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/93f43736-dbc3-5687-9132-bc11087fb7c4', 'word_count': 886, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93f43736-dbc3-5687-9132-bc11087fb7c4'}, {'abstract': 'A new report aims to force Southern towns and the nation to confront an era of racial terror directly and recognize its ramifications today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/11/opinion/lynching-as-racial-terrorism.html', 'snippet': 'A new report aims to force Southern towns and the nation to confront an era of racial terror directly and recognize its ramifications today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is important to remember that the hangings, burnings and dismemberments of black American men, women and children that were relatively common in this country between the Civil War and World War II were often public events. They were sometimes advertised in newspapers and drew hundreds and even thousands of white spectators, including elected officials and leading citizens who were so swept up in the carnivals of death that they posed with their children for keepsake photographs within arm’s length of mutilated black corpses.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11wed1WEB/11wed1WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 509, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11wed1WEB/11wed1WEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 509}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11wed1WEB/11wed1WEB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 868, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11wed1WEB/11wed1WEB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1737, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11wed1WEB/11wed1WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/11/opinion/11wed1WEB/11wed1WEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'c