'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/12/fashion/fashions-racial-divide.html', 'snippet': 'Fewer than 4 percent of the 260 fashion designers showing at New York Fashion Week are African-American.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Michelle Obama stepped onto the stage at the Democratic National Convention in 2012 to talk about her husband and the coming election, the Internet went into an effective group swoon. Words like “spotlight stealing” and “dazzling” were used with abandon. Not about the first lady’s speech, but about her dress: a shimmering pink and silver sleeveless number by the designer Tracy Reese.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/fashion/12BLACK/12BLACK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/12/fashion/12BLACK/12BLACK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/fashion/12BLACK/12BLACK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/fashion/12BLACK/12BLACK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/fashion/12BLACK/12BLACK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/12/fashion/12BLACK/12BLACK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/fashion/12BLACK/12BLACK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fashion’s Racial Divide', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fashion’s Racial Divide', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Fashion Week', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oliver, Shayne', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reese, Tracy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cushnie, Carly', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-11T23:52:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vanessa Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vanessa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cab418f-4bf1-5065-a118-85d0bb078ff7', 'word_count': 3169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cab418f-4bf1-5065-a118-85d0bb078ff7'}, {'abstract': 'The Alabama judge isn’t George Wallace.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/11/magazine/in-sort-of-defense-of-roy-moore.html', 'snippet': 'The Alabama judge isn’t George Wallace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chief Justice Roy Moore of the Alabama Supreme Court is trying to block same-sex marriage in his state, despite a federal court order allowing it. Moore told “Good Morning America” that he is confident he will “absolutely not” end up on the wrong side of history. The fact that 36 states (apart from Alabama) now allow same-sex marriage, with rising support from the public, suggests he’s probably wrong about that. His central legal claim — that same-sex marriage is a states’ rights issue, rather than one that implicates the guarantee of equality in the Constitution — is also a loser. Moore argued that “redefinition of the word marriage is not found within the powers designated to the federal government,” but Congress makes all kinds of rules involving marriage (and pensions and taxes) and the Supreme Court has previously ruled that marriage is a fundamental right.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/magazine/11alabama1/11alabama1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 374, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/11/magazine/11alabama1/11alabama1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 374}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/magazine/11alabama1/11alabama1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 639, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/magazine/11alabama1/11alabama1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1278, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/magazine/11alabama1/11alabama1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/11/magazine/11alabama1/11alabama1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/11/magazine/11alabama1/11alabama1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Sort-of-Defense of Roy Moore', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Roy S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-11T23:52:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Bazelon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bazelon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae10afd9-7c08-5aba-9c2a-bb0ab75378ce', 'word_count': 1485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae10afd9-7c08-5aba-9c2a-bb0ab75378ce'}, {'abstract': 'Jeffrey Herr, curator of Hollyhock House, on the restoration and reopening of Frank Lloyd Wright’s first Los Angeles house.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/12/garden/the-hollyhock-house-comes-into-its-own.html', 'snippet': 'Jeffrey Herr, curator of Hollyhock House, on the restoration and reopening of Frank Lloyd Wright’s first Los Angeles house.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Finally, the planets seem to have aligned for Frank Lloyd Wright’s Hollyhock House. On Jan. 30, the United States Department of the Interior announced its decision, seven years in the making, to nominate the 1921 Los Angeles home, along with nine other works by Wright, to the Unesco World Heritage List, which includes icons like the Taj Mahal and Statue of Liberty. And tomorrow, the building, now a house museum, will reopen after a four-year $4.3 million restoration.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/garden/12QNA1-copy/12QNA1-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/12/garden/12QNA1-copy/12QNA1-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 421}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/garden/12QNA1-copy/12QNA1-copy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/garden/12QNA1-copy/12QNA1-copy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1439, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/garden/12QNA1-copy/12QNA1-copy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/12/garden/12QNA1-copy/12QNA1-copy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/garden/12QNA1-copy/12QNA1-copy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hollyhock House Comes Into Its Own', 'kicker': 'Currents: Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A House Comes Into Its Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 1, 'major': '