adline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-12T15:53:11+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/af033761-5d87-5419-bf70-5008790330a5', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/af033761-5d87-5419-bf70-5008790330a5'}, {'abstract': 'The Nashville-based chanteuse’s forthcoming album “Dark Place” ends with a dreamy, sultry version of Bette Midler’s “Do You Want to Dance,” which premieres here.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/12/jessie-baylin-do-you-want-to-dance-bette-midler-cover/', 'snippet': 'The Nashville-based chanteuse’s forthcoming album “Dark Place” ends with a dreamy, sultry version of Bette Midler’s “Do You Want to Dance,” which premieres here.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lot has changed for Jessie Baylin since the release of her 2012 album, “Little Spark.” For starters, the New Jersey-born singer-songwriter has put down some serious roots in Tennessee with her husband, the Kings of Leon drummer Nathan Followill. She also became a mother to their now-2-year-old daughter Violet, and she strongly considered putting her music career to bed for good. “I wasn’t sure if I wanted to make another album,” she recalls from her Nashville home. “I felt something was happening and I had some ideas, but I wasn’t writing as much and my focus was elsewhere.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/12/t-magazine/12baylin-comingore/12baylin-comingore-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/12/t-magazine/12baylin-comingore/12baylin-comingore-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 