ajor': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mohamed, Baher', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-12T21:40:14+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'Reuters', 'person': [], 'organization': 'Reuters'}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f78ab5ec-9643-51a2-9e13-6263e9a738c0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f78ab5ec-9643-51a2-9e13-6263e9a738c0'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Schoonhoven belonged to the Zero Group of European avant-gardists, but lacked their anarchic DNA.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/13/arts/design/jan-schoonhoven-at-david-zwirner.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Schoonhoven belonged to the Zero Group of European avant-gardists, but lacked their anarchic DNA.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the largest overview yet mounted in New York of the tender, Minimally inclined art of Jan Schoonhoven (1914-1994), a Dutch artist who emerged in the postwar period and by the early 1960s was making white-on-white wall reliefs of shallow grids. Built from paper, cardboard and wood painted white, these grids dominate the show, their units square or rectangular, their perpendicular divisions thick or thin. Invariably enriched by the play of light — especially when their interior planes are slanted — they achieve both a quietly stunning variety and a sense of timelessness through utmost simplicity.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13SCHOONHOVENGALLERY2/SCHOONHOVEN-GALLERY2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 493, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13SCHOONHOVENGALLERY2/SCHOONHOVEN-GALLERY2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 493, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13SCHOONHOVENGALLERY2/SCHOONHOVEN-GALLERY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13SCHOONHOVENGALLERY2/SCHOONHOVEN-GALLERY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13SCHOONHOVENGALLERY2/SCHOONHOVEN-GALLERY2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jan Schoonhoven at David Zwirner', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jan Schoonhoven', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schoonhoven, Jan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-12T21:40:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e756105-8d9f-5bb1-98d1-8faffb89bdf0', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e756105-8d9f-5bb1-98d1-8faffb89bdf0'}, {'abstract': 'Resurfaced photographs by Susan Bee show how her interests shifted increasingly toward painting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/13/arts/design/susan-bee-photograms-and-altered-photos-from-the-1970s.html', 'snippet': 'Resurfaced photographs by Susan Bee show how her interests shifted increasingly toward painting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the 1970s, the artist Susan Bee was writing a master’s thesis on the photograms of Moholy-Nagy and Man Ray at Hunter College; helping to edit an experimental poetry magazine, L=A=N=G=U=A=G=E, with her husband, Charles Bernstein; and making her own photographic experiments in a cramped studio. As her personal interests shifted increasingly toward painting, she packed the photographs away. They recently resurfaced in storage and Southfirst has been smart enough to make a beautiful show of them.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13BEEGALLERY1/13BEE-GALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 650, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13BEEGALLERY1/13BEE-GALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 650}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13BEEGALLERY1/13BEE-GALLERY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 945, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13BEEGALLERY1/13BEE-GALLERY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1890, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13BEEGALLERY1/13BEE-GALLERY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13BEEGALLERY1/13BEE-GALLERY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13BE