 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: February 13, 2015', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T06:29:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/930d3421-5b29-5f26-96bc-dfcb2b229804', 'word_count': 389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/930d3421-5b29-5f26-96bc-dfcb2b229804'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Carr managed to see the complexities of digital-age journalism from every angle, and to write about them with unparalleled clarity and wit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/13/business/media/david-carr-a-journalist-at-the-center-of-the-sweet-spot.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Carr managed to see the complexities of digital-age journalism from every angle, and to write about them with unparalleled clarity and wit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a while, a few years ago, there was a weekly video on the New York Times website called The Sweet Spot, taped during off hours in the cafeteria on the 14th floor. The idea was that it would be an informal, incisive discussion of various developments in the world of culture and media. Sometimes it managed to live up to its name, and sometimes it didn’t, but for me the show — or webcast, or schmooze fest, or whatever it was — had a much simpler reason for being: It guaranteed that I would have a few hours a week in the company of David Carr. For anyone who cared about journalism, there was simply no better place to be.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/business/13appraisal1/13appraisal1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/13/business/13appraisal1/13appraisal1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/business/13appraisal1/13appraisal1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/business/13appraisal1/13appraisal1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1460, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/business/13appraisal1/13appraisal1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/13/business/13appraisal1/13appraisal1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/business/13appraisal1/13appraisal1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'David Carr, a Journalist at the Center of the Sweet Spot', 'kicker': 'An Appraisal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Journalist at the Center of the Sweet Spot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carr, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T06:36:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'An Appraisal', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a008e8f-c3b1-5953-b2b1-33378f361ded', 'word_count': 740, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a008e8f-c3b1-5953-b2b1-33378f361ded'}, {'abstract': 'Sometimes, David Carr was more quotable than the personalities he wrote about. Here’s a selection of his writing and public statements.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/13/business/media/david-carr-excerpts.html', 'snippet': 'Sometimes, David Carr was more quotable than the personalities he wrote about. Here’s a selection of his writing and public statements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Carr was often more quotable than the personalities he wrote about. His plainspoken style was sometimes blunt, and searingly honest about himself. The effect was both folksy and sophisticated, a voice from a shrewd and well-informed skeptic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Quotable David Carr', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carr, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T06:46:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f49e5dd-c63e-5786-8450-0dc14e521dd8', 'word_count': 844, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f49e5dd-c63e-5786-8450-0dc14e521dd8'}, {'abstract': 'The Coldplay singer will oversee the lineup for the anti-poverty group’s international music festival for the next 15 years.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/13/chris-martin-to-curate-global-citizen-festival-for-15-years/', 'snippet': 'The Coldplay singer will oversee the lineup for the anti-poverty group’s international music festival for the next 15 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chris Martin, the lead singer of Coldplay, has signed on as an ambassador for the anti-poverty group Global Citizen, and will serve as the curator of its international music festival for the next 15 years, said Hugh Evans, the chief executive of the Global Poverty Project, which oversees the campaign.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/12artsbeat-martin2/12artsbeat-martin2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/12artsbeat-martin2/12artsbeat-martin2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/12artsbeat-martin2/12artsbeat-martin2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/12artsbeat-martin2/12artsbeat-martin2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/12artsbeat-martin2/12artsbeat-martin2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/12artsbeat-martin2/12artsbeat-martin2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/12artsbeat-martin2/12artsbeat-martin2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chris Martin to Curate Global Citizen Festival for 15 Years', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A 15-Year Role for Chris Martin With Global Citizen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Millennium Development Goals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Chris (1977- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Global Poverty Project', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T07:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Coscarelli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Coscarelli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28ce1e2c-70b8-50e2-bf83-7faebfd9eac6', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28ce1e2c-70b8-50e2-bf83-7faebfd9eac6'}, {'abstract': 'As China’s anticorruption campaign enters its third year, the country is developing its first cash-detecting dog program for its borders, with the help of New Zealand.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/13/china-to-employ-customs-workers-with-a-nose-for-hard-cash/', 'snippet': 'As China’s anticorruption campaign enters its third year, the country is developing its first cash-detecting dog program for its borders, with the help of New Zealand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a country where cash is king but the 100 renminbi note is the highest denomination, it is not unheard of for individuals to make purchases with suitcases stuffed with pink bills emblazoned with Mao’s face. Some have also taken this habit abroad, ready to spend big on real estate or luxury goods, even though China limits the amount private citizens can transfer out of the country to $50,000 a year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China to Employ Customs Workers With a Nose for Hard Cash', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Zealand', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T07:19:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bree Feng', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bree', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feng', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/507094ba-b201-5b00-9fdd-954a54b08f22', 'word_count': 429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/507094ba-b201-5b00-9fdd-954a54b08f22'}, {'abstract': 'Welcome to Motherlode’s weekly open thread. Do you have thoughts about the news this week, and how it affects families? A question to ask? A rant to share? This is your place. Go.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/13/your-turn-a-weekly-thread-open-for-comments/', 'snippet': 'Welcome to Motherlode’s weekly open thread. Do you have thoughts about the news this week, and how it affects families? A question to ask? A rant to share? This is your place. Go.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Welcome to Motherlode’s weekly open thread. Do you have thoughts about the news this week, and how it affects families? A question to ask? A rant to share? This is your place. Go.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Your Turn: A Weekly Thread, Open for Comments', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T07:27:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/688fa8d8-b795-5084-a3e1-9df400fdc16c', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/688fa8d8-b795-5084-a3e1-9df400fdc16c'}, {'abstract': 'Jason Lam stepped out from his lion dance costume to document a tradition that serves as a hinge between his Chinese ancestry and his American upbringing.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/13/celebrating-the-chinese-lion-dance/', 'snippet': 'Jason Lam stepped out from his lion dance costume to document a tradition that serves as a hinge between his Chinese ancestry and his American upbringing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Southern Chinese lion dancing comes from an ancient tale: Terrorized by a mythical monster, a horde of villagers descended from the mountains and huddled under an enormous monster of their own in an attempt to repel the beast. Year after year, to the accompaniment of firecracker bursts and drums, the ritual is repeated as protection against evil spirits. At least that’s one version.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Celebrating the Chinese Lion Dance', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Enter the Lion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Center for Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major':