DAYFILE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 491, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13FRIDAYFILE/13FRIDAYFILE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13FRIDAYFILE/13FRIDAYFILE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/arts/13FRIDAYFILE/13FRIDAYFILE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Entertainments from the Archives of The New York Times', 'kicker': 'Friday File', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murphy, Mary Jo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Westminster Kennel Club Dog Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T14:58:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Jo Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Jo', 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3f8c978-7aa8-5150-9905-eac9a87f9b5e', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3f8c978-7aa8-5150-9905-eac9a87f9b5e'}, {'abstract': 'Georgie Newbery made a name for herself in the fashion world before moving to the country to start Common Farm Flowers.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/13/georgie-newbery-flower-farmers-year/', 'snippet': 'Georgie Newbery made a name for herself in the fashion world before moving to the country to start Common Farm Flowers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Georgie Newbery welcomes guests in the fields of her small farm on a rainy winter day in Somerset, England — her Wellingtons muddied and water dripping off the rim of her soggy hat\xa0—\xa0it’s hard to imagine that she was once a regular guest at Paris fashion shows. During her glamorous previous life in the 1990s, Newbery was the assistant to Susan Train, the legendary Paris bureau chief of American Vogue, and later served as office manager for John Galliano, back when he was being courted by LVMH to take over Givenchy. Next, she moved to London where she penned three rollicking romans à clef about the fashion industry, before leaving that world for good. Today, with her press-shy English husband, Newbery runs Common Farm Flowers, a floral company that delivers cheerful bouquets and centerpieces by mail throughout Britain. They grow most of the flowers organically themselves at Common Farm, a seven-acre spread with a ramshackle house they are restoring. Newbery has also returned to her original love of writing and combined it with her third career as a florist, producing “The Flower Farmer’s Year: How to Grow Cut Flowers for Pleasure and\xa0Profit” ($30, Green Books), a richly illustrated how-to guide in which Newbery shares all that she has learned in her 10\xa0years in the fields of southern England.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/13/t-magazine/13georgie-thomas-1/13georgie-thomas-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/13/t-magazine/13georgie-thomas-1/13georgie-thomas-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo'