et': 'Far from home, an American father and son contend with the incomprehensible death of a loved one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine being in a foreign airport with your father, and your sister is somewhere in the same airport in a coffin waiting for you to fly her home, but the airport is fogbound and heaving with unhappy souls and you are given hours and then days to contemplate her death and your life. This is the plot, such as it is, of Greg Baxter’s second novel. The airport in question is Munich, and the fog is a confusion that seeps into the whole novel — Baxter is the fog machine and we the readers are also fogbound.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15LEDGARD/0215-bks-LedgaRD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 196, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15LEDGARD/0215-bks-LedgaRD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 196}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15LEDGARD/0215-bks-LedgaRD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 334, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15LEDGARD/0215-bks-LedgaRD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15LEDGARD/0215-bks-LedgaRD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15LEDGARD/0215-bks-LedgaRD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth'