op computer. I moved a document to the OneDrive, which I did not mean to do, so how do I delete it?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deleting Files From Microsoft’s OneDrive', 'kicker': 'Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desktop Computers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Windows (Operating System)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Storage', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T17:43:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. D. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f9273ac-6824-5bf2-9578-642100a4adca', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f9273ac-6824-5bf2-9578-642100a4adca'}, {'abstract': 'New York Fashion Week is sharing the city with All-Star weekend and the N.B.A., whose most stylish players aspire to be icons of an industry other than basketball.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/14/sports/basketball/the-biggest-thing-in-fashion-an-nba-player-of-course.html', 'snippet': 'New York Fashion Week is sharing the city with All-Star weekend and the N.B.A., whose most stylish players aspire to be icons of an industry other than basketball.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nick Young was slouched in a booth inside Santina, a restaurant under the High Line park, where the Italian fashion house Ermenegildo Zegna had organized a private dinner Thursday to fete the Knicks’ Carmelo Anthony, a featured face in one of its coming campaigns.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Posing for Fashion Houses, N.B.A. Stars Feel at Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Posing for Fashion Houses, N.B.A. Stars Feel at Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Fashion Week', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Westbrook, Russell', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harden, James', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young, Nick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T17:48:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab66ca6d-62e9-59af-adbe-2f0263b117c1', 'word_count': 1087, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab66ca6d-62e9-59af-adbe-2f0263b117c1'}, {'abstract': 'The characters in Anne Tyler’s novel, four generations of them, are drawn to a commodious house in suburban Baltimore.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/15/books/review/a-spool-of-blue-thread-by-anne-tyler.html', 'snippet': 'The characters in Anne Tyler’s novel, four generations of them, are drawn to a commodious house in suburban Baltimore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Picture a middle-class American family, four generations, living in the suburbs. The “founder” is an upstart with a murky past, a hard-working construction guy in a bleak marriage. Next in line are his son and the son’s wife — he’s a chip off the old patriarch, inherits the construction business, soldiers on. She’s the maddening, meddling family fixer-upper, determined that her four children will, against all odds, tolerate if not love one another. And then there are the children, at midlife and with children of their own: a couple of daughters who annoy each other, an “acquired” son (the product of a “crazy arrangement”) and of course the obligatory black sheep, Denny, the prodigal son who surfaces intermittently to provide the drama, which, after all, is his job — in the family as in the plot of Anne Tyler’s graceful and capacious new novel, “A Spool of Blue Thread.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/25SINKLER/25sinkler-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/25SINKLER/25sinkler-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 674}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/25SINKLER/25sinkler-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 912, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/25SINKLER/25sinkler-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1824, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/25SINKLER/25SINKLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/25SINKLER/25SINKLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/25SINKLER/25SINKLER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Spool of Blue Thread,’ by Anne Tyler', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'You Can Go Home Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tyler, Anne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Spool of Blue Thread (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T17:49:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Pepper Sinkler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'Pepper', 'lastname': 'Sinkler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/81007182-2ef6-5e1d-af2a-b34849665604', 'word_count': 1214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81007182-2ef6-5e1d-af2a-b34849665604'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist investigates the Tahrir Square revolution and the reasons for its disappointing aftermath.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/15/books/review/once-upon-a-revolution-by-thanassis-cambanis.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist investigates the Tahrir Square revolution and the reasons for its disappointing aftermath.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Western coverage of the Arab Spring was often simple-minded, as journalists focused on the dramatic confrontations between the police and protesters in Tahrir Square in Cairo or on Libyan militiamen fighting Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s soldiers on the road south of Benghazi. Uprisings were reported like scenes out of “Les Misérables,” with an oppressed people battling a brutal tyranny everywhere from Tunis to Bahrain.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15cockburn/15cockburn-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 582, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15cockburn/15cockburn-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 582}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15cockburn/15cockburn-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 993, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15cockburn/15cockburn-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1666, 'width': 1718, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15cockburn/15cockburn-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15cockburn/15cockburn-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/books/review/15cockburn/15cockburn-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Once Upon a Revolution,’ by Thanassis Cambanis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tomorrow Never Came', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cambanis, Thanassis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-13T17:50:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Cockburn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cockburn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/be1a7a8c-e684-5092-8f8c-f516edc58aa8', 'word_count': 1188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be1a7a8c-e684-5092-8f8c-f516edc58aa8'}, {'abstract': 'Welcome to your new life as a middle-aged single dude. Here’s what it will be like.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/15/opinion/sunday/to-a-friend-on-his-divorce.html', 'snippet': 'Welcome to your new life as a middle-aged single dude. Here’s what it will be like.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YOU were 7 and I was 6 when we met near the jungle gym. After becoming bandmates in high school and enjoying a brief twinkle of local stardom, we became men and did man things, one of which was each finding someone we loved enough to marry. I have proof that both of us were happy then. The photos from our weddings show us laughing, smiling, greeting friends and relatives, eating cake, smoking cigars and dancing.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/sunday-review/15DIVORCE/15DIVORCE-articleLarge-v2.gif', 'height': 556, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/15/sunday-