Tribune archives: Largest British air raid to date is incredibly successful in 1915.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/13/1915-britain-hails-air-raids-success/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Largest British air raid to date is incredibly successful in 1915.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — All England is rejoicing to-day [Feb. 13] at the superb flight of her great air fleet, thirty-four strong, across the Channel, with not a serious injury to a pilot, and accidents to only two of the machines. It seems almost incredible that it was only five years ago that Blériot made the pioneer aeroplane trip across the Channel, so vast and rapid has been the progress since. The raid not only represents the largest fleet of air craft that has ever operated together, but it is a fitting reply to the German bombardment of British towns, and a warning to the Kaiser’s forces in Belgium of further damage. One fact well demonstrated by the raid is that in case of such an attack, land guns are unreliable defences, and it is necessary to meet aeroplanes with aeroplanes. The attack probably puts an end to Germany’s reputed intention to use Zeebrugge and other places on the Belgian coast as bases for submarines. The Admiralty states that no submarines were seen. This indicates that German preparations are too incomplete for them to trust these craft in Belgian harbors. — The New York Herald, European Edition, Feb. 14, 1915', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1915: Britain Hails Air Raid’s Success', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Aircraft', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'English Channel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'val