anization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b454f6c5-e1ab-5461-a44f-f2656ea0707d', 'word_count': 797, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b454f6c5-e1ab-5461-a44f-f2656ea0707d'}, {'abstract': 'The famous East Side Manhattan saloon P. J. Clarke’s now has a number of branches, and the one in Woodbury, on Long Island, opened in December.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/15/nyregion/a-suburban-bistro-born-of-an-east-side-legend-p-j-clarkes-in-woodbury.html', 'snippet': 'The famous East Side Manhattan saloon P. J. Clarke’s now has a number of branches, and the one in Woodbury, on Long Island, opened in December.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early on in James Salter’s recent novel “All That Is,” the hero, Philip Bowman, meets two women in an East Side Manhattan bar. “I was thinking of going over to Clarke’s,” he tells them. He is, of course, referring to P. J. Clarke’s, the 130-year-old saloon at Third Avenue and 55th Street. Bowman, recently back from the war in the Pacific and newly minted as an editor at a major publishing house, is meant to symbolize the romance and optimism of the “Greatest Generation.” That his preferred watering hole is P. J. Clarke’s is testimony to the author’s keen eye for detail. For a character like Bowman, there would be no other bar.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/nyregion/15DINELI2/15DINELI2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/15/nyregion/15DINELI2/15DINELI2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/nyregion/15DINELI2/15DINELI2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1023, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0