 'headline': {'main': 'From Diabelli, a Variety of Variations', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classical: Variety in Variations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-14T21:57:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f60a0810-5d63-5e6d-9ca4-c0ebf67df064', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f60a0810-5d63-5e6d-9ca4-c0ebf67df064'}, {'abstract': 'The group will celebrate the release of its first record, “From the Vine,” at Rockwood Music Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/15/arts/music/fresh-cut-orchestra-a-jazz-band-thats-hard-to-define.html', 'snippet': 'The group will celebrate the release of its first record, “From the Vine,” at Rockwood Music Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Fresh Cut Orchestra is a new young 10-piece Philadelphia-jazz municipality, a little hard to define and very much worth looking into. It’s a well-drilled band with three different leaders: the trumpeter Josh Lawrence, the bassist Jason Fraticelli and the drummer Anwar Marshall. (You might know them from records or bands led by Orrin Evans, Taylor McFerrin, Erykah Badu and Dave Douglas). ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fresh Cut Orchestra, a Jazz Band That’s Hard to Define', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop: Hard to Define, but Worth a Look', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-14T21:57:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbf75303-3c8b-5ce7-9924-bcd89f5a70e8', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbf75303-3c8b-5ce7-9924-bcd89f5a70e8'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Maple’s two features are part of the center’s series “Tell It Like It Is: Black Independents in New York, 1968-1986.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/15/movies/films-by-jessie-maple-in-lincoln-center-series.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Maple’s two features are part of the center’s series “Tell It Like It Is: Black Independents in New York, 1968-1986.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The work of Jessie Maple, a filmmaker and the first African-American woman to join New York’s camera operators union in 1975, will be celebrated on Monday with the program “An Evening With Jessie Maple” at the Elinor Bunin Munroe Film Center (144 West 65th Street). Ms. Maple, who wrote a book, “How to Become a Union Camerawoman,” about her life and hard-fought battle to join the union, directed two narrative features. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/arts/15GUIDE4/15GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 475, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/15/arts/15GUIDE4/15GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 475}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/arts/15GUIDE4/15GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 810, 'width':