ion_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbd8c21d-0e3b-553f-8780-6b37c707d657', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbd8c21d-0e3b-553f-8780-6b37c707d657'}, {'abstract': 'Does the urban climb from developing to developed economies have to be smoggy? So far, yes.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/15/london-l-a-beijing-delhi-nairobi-is-smog-an-inevitable-urban-growing-pain/', 'snippet': 'Does the urban climb from developing to developed economies have to be smoggy? So far, yes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gardiner Harris has a detailed story in The Times on what could be seen as New Delhi’s race to outdo Beijing in the deadly flows of air pollution from its traffic, industries, garbage dumps and kitchens. The soupy air is, on closer look, deadlier than that in China’s cities, he reports:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/world/JP-INDIAAIR/JP-INDIAAIR-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/15/world/JP-INDIAAIR/JP-INDIAAIR-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 415}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/world/JP-INDIAAIR/JP-INDIAAIR-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/15/world/JP-INDIAAIR/JP-INDIAAIR-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1417, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {