a.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Fashion Week: DKNY Fall/Winter 2015', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Karan, Donna, International', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-15T22:45:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alfredo Piola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alfredo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94f494c4-9a8a-5a7a-a840-e2085ac1a909', 'word_count': 41, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94f494c4-9a8a-5a7a-a840-e2085ac1a909'}, {'abstract': 'In his debut novel, Elliot Ackerman, a veteran of five tours of duty in Afghanistan and Iraq, conveys the toll of war on ordinary Afghans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/16/books/review-in-green-on-blue-an-afghan-boy-narrates-story-of-war.html', 'snippet': 'In his debut novel, Elliot Ackerman, a veteran of five tours of duty in Afghanistan and Iraq, conveys the toll of war on ordinary Afghans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This haunting debut novel by Elliot Ackerman — a decorated veteran who served five tours of duty in Afghanistan and Iraq — is told not from the point of view of an American soldier, but in the voice of an Afghan orphan named Aziz who is forced to join a United States-funded Afghan militia called the Special Lashkar after his beloved older brother Ali is gravely wounded. “Green on Blue” is a novel that conveys, with harrowing power, the fallout that decades of war (going back through the Soviet occupation of the 1980s) has had on that country’s people, and at the same time, it’s a kind of Greek tragedy about the cycles of revenge and violence that can consume families and tribes, generation after generation. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16BOOKJP/16BOOKJP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16BOOKJP/16BOOKJP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16BOOKJP/16BOOKJP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16BOOKJP/16BOOKJP-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1599, 'width': 1199, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16BOOKJP/16BOOKJP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16BOOKJP/16BOOKJP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16BOOKJP/16BOOKJP-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Green on Blue,’ an Afghan Boy Narrates Story of War', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Child Dragooned by Revenge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ackerman, Elliot', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001-14)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Green on Blue (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-15T22:50:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/91730cbf-bc3e-53e2-b627-2fbd38f6c308', 'word_count': 989, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91730cbf-bc3e-53e2-b627-2fbd38f6c308'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Fairchild and Sterling Hyltin danced the leads as their company’s production began a two-month run at the David H. Koch Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/16/arts/dance/review-original-leads-return-in-city-ballets-romeo-juliet.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Fairchild and Sterling Hyltin danced the leads as their company’s production began a two-month run at the David H. Koch Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was 2007 when Peter Martins unveiled his “Romeo + Juliet.” At the time, the New York City Ballet production — which began a run of nine performances on Friday at the David H. Koch Theater — had one thing going for it: Mr. Martins’s decision to cast his leads, particularly Juliet, young. (His Romeos required more experience and strength for partnering.) He worked with students at the City Ballet-affiliated School of American Ballet, including the now-esteemed soloist Russell Janzen, but eventually settled on two fresh company members for the premiere: Robert Fairchild and Sterling Hyltin. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16ROMEOSUB/16ROMEOSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16ROMEOSUB/16ROMEOSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16ROMEOSUB/16ROMEOSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16ROMEOSUB/16ROMEOSUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1371, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16ROMEOSUB/16ROMEOSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16ROMEOSUB/16ROMEOSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/arts/16ROMEOSUB/16ROMEOSUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Original Leads Return in City Ballet’s ‘Romeo + Juliet’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Star-Crossed, but Less Dewy-Eyed Than of Yore', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martins, Peter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 