sword puzzle, which is what you would expect, especially on a Monday. But then you would remember that it’s Washington’s birthday, or Presidents’ Day, or whatever they’re calling it these days, and you would probably say, “Hey! Where my presidents at?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Imprint on a Dirt Road', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Presidents' Day", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-15T23:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7425517-71b2-5f3d-8235-651d180ada40', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7425517-71b2-5f3d-8235-651d180ada40'}, {'abstract': '“The Grand Budapest Hotel” and “The Imitation Game” were named best original and adapted screenplay, respectively, at the Writers Guild of America Awards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/16/movies/writers-guild-chooses-top-screenplays-of-2014.html', 'snippet': '“The Grand Budapest Hotel” and “The Imitation Game” were named best original and adapted screenplay, respectively, at the Writers Guild of America Awards.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Grand Budapest Hotel” and “The Imitation Game” were named best original and adapted screenplay, respectively, at the Writers Guild of America Awards, given out Saturday in Los Angeles and New York. The script for “The Grand Budapest Hotel,” a comedic caper about a charismatic concierge and a young bellboy at a popular 1930s European ski resort, was written by Wes Anderson. “The Imitation Game” was written by Graham Moore and based on Andrew Hodges’s book about the British code-breaker Alan Turing. Other winners included Brian Knappenberger’s “The Internet’s Own Boy: The Story of Aaron Swartz,” about a computer programming prodigy and information activist, for best documentary screenplay; Nic Pizzolatto’s “True Detective,” the HBO mini-series that revisits a 1995 murder case in rural Louisiana 17 years later, for best drama series and best new series; and “Louie,” a fictionalized series starring the comedian Louis CK, for best comedy series (written by Louis CK and Pamela Adlon) and best episodic comedy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Writers Guild Chooses Top Screenplays of 2014', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Writers Guild Chooses Top Screenplays of 2014', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Writers Guild of America', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANDERSON, WES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Graham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Imitation Game (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Grand Budapest Hotel (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-15T23:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'Awards Season', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/754679e4-c619-55c6-9be1-bd638d65109f', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/754679e4-c619-55c6-9be1-bd638d65109f'}, {'abstract': 'Decades of speculation about the meaning behind of one of the 20th century’s most renowned and enigmatic songs could finally end.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/16/arts/music/auction-may-reveal-secrets-of-american-pie.html', 'snippet': 'Decades of speculation about the meaning behind of one of the 20th century’s most renowned and enigmatic songs could finally end.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Decades of speculation about the meaning behind of one of the 20th century’s most renowned and enigmatic songs could finally end when the notes and manuscripts of Don McLean’s “American Pie” go up for sale this spring — that is, of course, if the highest bidder chooses to publicly share what insights they may hold. The auction house Christie’s announced last week that the original working manuscript and typed drafts to the song — 16 pages total — would be auctioned on April 7 in New York. Mr. McLean, 69, told Reuters he decided to sell the documents on a whim. Other than admitting that a line in the song, “the day the music died,” refers to the Feb. 3, 1959, deaths of Buddy Holly, Ritchie Valens and J. P. Richardson, known as the Big Bopper, Mr. McLean has been mostly vague about the meaning of other parts of the song. He told Reuters that the “writing and lyrics will divulge everything there is to divulge.” “American Pie” was released in 1971. The manuscript is estimated to fetch between $1 million and $1.5 million.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Auction May Reveal Secrets of ‘American Pie’', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Auction May Reveal Secrets of ‘American Pie’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Christie's", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McLean, Don', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-15T23:03:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae639163-d389-5250-834c-ed907df750f1', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae639163-d389-5250-834c-ed907df750f1'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from J. Crew, Rachel Comey, Creatures of the Wind and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/16/fashion/cool-girls-keep-it-comfortable.html', 'snippet': 'Highlights from J. Crew, Rachel Comey, Creatures of the Wind and more.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/fashion/16BROOKREVIEW2/16BROOKREVIEW2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/16/fashion/16BROOKREVIEW2/16BROOKREVIEW2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/fashion/16BROOKREVIEW2/16BROOKREVIEW2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/fashion/16BROOKREVIEW2/16BROOKREVIEW2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': '