k': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-16T05:06:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cff7be8b-939b-523a-b32a-80f927fcbc1f', 'word_count': 82, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cff7be8b-939b-523a-b32a-80f927fcbc1f'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/16/arts/whats-on-monday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10 P.M. (13) INDEPENDENT LENS Thomas Allen Harris’s documentary “Through a Lens Darkly: Black Photographers and the Emergence of a People” tells the story of the men and women who have captured African-American culture over the past 170 years, including Carrie Mae Weems, Lorna Simpson, Anthony Barboza, Lyle Ashton Harris, Hank Willis Thomas (“Crossroads,” above), Coco Fusco and Clarissa Sligh. A. O. Scott, writing in The New York Times, called the movie “a family memoir, a tribute to unsung artists and a lyrical, at times heartbroken, meditation on imagery and identity.” He added, “The film is always absorbing to watch, but only once it’s over do you begin to grasp the extent of its ambitions, and just how much it has done within a packed, compact hour and a half.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Monday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-16T05:28:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': 