Douglas Quenqua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quenqua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cc6c48e-6e81-5e30-8629-c947427d945e', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cc6c48e-6e81-5e30-8629-c947427d945e'}, {'abstract': 'In a rich and entertaining new book, a philosopher and a physicist describe how the new physics changed our world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/17/science/the-quantum-moment-recounts-the-end-of-determinism.html', 'snippet': 'In a rich and entertaining new book, a philosopher and a physicist describe how the new physics changed our world.', 'lead_paragraph': '“On or about September 1927,” wrote the philosopher Ray Monk, “the physical world changed.” Until then, according to Robert P. Crease and Alfred Scharff Goldhaber’s rich and entertaining new book, “The Quantum Moment,” we lived in a homogeneous, continuous, Newtonian world in which all objects moved seamlessly from the past to the future, governed by universal mathematical laws. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/17/science/17SCIBOOK/17SCIBOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/17/science/17SCIBOOK/17SCIBOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/17/science/17SCIBOOK/17SCIBOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/17/science/17SCIBOOK/17SCIBOOK-superJumbo.jpg',