le', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear and Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e12344e-d8c1-586e-a03f-d39098b3e682', 'word_count': 1169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e12344e-d8c1-586e-a03f-d39098b3e682'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/17/arts/whats-on-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (13, 49) THE ITALIAN AMERICANS More than four million Italians emigrated to the United States from 1880 to 1924, mostly coming from southern Italy and Sicily to escape poverty. This series, narrated by the actor Stanley Tucci and written by John Maggio, spans four generations of Italian-Americans, beginning with the large wave of immigration in the late 19th century and continuing to the present day. Along the way, the two-part series uses archival material and interviews with experts and prominent Italian-Americans (like Mario M. Cuomo, above; Tony Bennett; and Gay Talese, to name a few) to tell the stories of those who played important roles in shaping the relationships between Italians and Americans, and how immigrants, once considered outsiders, have risen over time to become leaders in business, politics and the arts. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-17T06:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By L