, 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f4d85d1-2d21-52fa-8997-6828a003ee0f', 'word_count': 1423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f4d85d1-2d21-52fa-8997-6828a003ee0f'}, {'abstract': 'A crop of fresh takes on the classic fisherman’s sweater appeals to a freezing fashion set during the New York shows.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/17/fisherman-sweaters-fall-2015-trend/', 'snippet': 'A crop of fresh takes on the classic fisherman’s sweater appeals to a freezing fashion set during the New York shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Never have the runways looked so tempting, as designers continue to introduce fur-hooded parkas, oversize shearling coats and rugged boots for fall — an appealing lot for the fashion set, which continues to trudge through snow and slush from one venue to the next. And a few fresh takes on the classic fisherman cable-knit sweaters are in the running for the coziest crop yet of winter-weather staples on fall 2015 runways. At Coach and Alexander Wang, the cream-colored tops — decked out with fringe and stud details, respectively — brightened up otherwise dark collections. And in Vera Wang’s gender-bending fashion show today, her oversize rendition of the traditional knit again lightened things up — especially paired with long shorts and high booties.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/17/t-magazine/17trend-fisher-gilchrist/17trend-fisher-gilchrist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/17/t-magazine/17trend-fi