o warn players about concussions, joined the agreement, according to a court filing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joe DeLamielluere, one the biggest critics of the settlement in a suit that accused the N.F.L. of failing to warn former players about the dangers of concussions, has reversed himself and joined the agreement, according to a court filing on Tuesday. DeLamielluere, a former offensive lineman, joined nine others who initially opted out but will now take part in the settlement, which provides up to $5 million for a limited number of severe neurological conditions. In all, 211 retired players, or about 1 percent of the total, opted out of the deal, which lawyers for the N.F.L. and the former players revised Friday to meet some concerns of Anita B. Brody, the federal judge in the case. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Critic of N.F.L. Settlement on Concussions Reverses Position', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Pro Football', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Critic Joins Settlement', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concussions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DeLamielluere, Joe (1951- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-18T05:09:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6c07a59-c6aa-54df-b5eb-acb8650a047a', 'word_count': 117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6c07a59-c6aa-54df-b5eb-acb8650a047a'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/18/arts/television/whats-on-tv-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (ABC Family) MATILDA (1996) For every child who ever thought the world was unfair, there’s hope that, like Matilda (Mara Wilson), he or she may one day discover a way to even the score. In this film based on Roald Dahl’s novel, Matilda is a clever heroine born to a loathsome set of parents — played here by Danny DeVito, who also directed the film, and Rhea Perlman — who is sent to a school overseen by a monstrous woman who torments her students. But things begin to change when Matilda realizes that she can use her telekinetic powers to defend herself and her fellow underdogs. Though the film may be too twisted for very young children, Janet Maslin wrote in The New York Times, the “sweet, self-possessed little heroine” is someone to admire: “Even when addressed by her father as ‘You lyin’ little earwig,’ Ms. Wilson’s poised Matilda never loses the quiet confidence that guarantees she will prevail.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-18T05:18:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/71414648-e4b9-5614-a266-220c3f91d388', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71414648-e4b9-5614-a266-220c3f91d388'}, {'abstract': 'The 139th annual Westminster Kenn