ich has remained largely unchanged for 30 years, is a unique destination in the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'London’s Walton Street, in the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, to the west of the city, has remained largely unchanged for the last 30 years. But for an increasingly international set of locals, that is its chief delight. Once home to the humorist P.G. Wodehouse, Walton Street still emanates an old-school English charm. Though flanked by Harrods and The Conran Shop, it’s an enclave of independent, if occasionally chichi, antiques and interiors shops, and art galleries and boutiques that has — so far — bucked the trend for high-end homogenization. There are, however, signs that change is afoot: Many of the streets’ stucco homes are straining under the scaffolds of hi-spec redevelopment, and rumor has it that the James Perse store may soon be joined by Charlotte Olympia and Christian Dior.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18walton-farrell-1/18walton-farrell-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18walton-farrell-1/18walton-farrell-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18walton-farrell-1/18walton-farrell-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18walton-farrell-1/18walton-farrell-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1229, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'im