e': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/19/arts/19BOOKJP2/19BOOKJP2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/19/arts/19BOOKJP2/19BOOKJP2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/19/arts/19BOOKJP2/19BOOKJP2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Schubert’s Winter Journey’ Allows Ian Bostridge to Obsess', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Walk Through Schubert, a Tenor as Your Guide', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bostridge, Ian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cooper, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schubert, Franz', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Schubert's Winter Journey: Anatomy of an Obsession (Book)", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-18T22:15:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e14b7ad-a1e9-57a5-b91b-09f5872132d1', 'word_count': 1104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e14b7ad-a1e9-57a5-b91b-09f5872132d1'}, {'abstract': 'The artist’s 18 “gazing globes,” with little landscapes inside, are one of two projects she is launching this week.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/18/madison-square-park-outdoor-art-globes-paula-hayes/', 'snippet': 'The artist’s 18 “gazing globes,” with little landscapes inside, are one of two projects she is launching this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paula Hayes can see into the future. “I’ve always been a bit witchy,” she squeals. It’s true: Hayes has been infusing her sculptures with a bit of history and magic since she began creating work back in the 1990s. But it wasn’t until her first show in 2004 with Salon 94, which represents her to this day, that it became clear the environmentally focused artist’s iconic medium, the round terrarium, would trickle down into critical mass. These days, retailers like Target unknowingly shill imitations of her work. Now that every twentysomething in Brooklyn (and their mothers elsewhere) own the little glass spheres that contain living plants, succulents and crystals, Hayes has moved on. “I’m contemplating in the realm of nature, but it has moved into sculptural form and illumination,” the 57-year-old says. On top of her terrariums, Hayes is internationally lauded as a landscape artist — even divining the backyards and terraces of the art world’s elite, including the gallerists Jeanne Rohatyn Greenberg (of Salon 94), David Zwirner and Marianne Boesky. For her next project, though, “I’m not using living plants,” Hayes says. “That’s a huge departure for my audience, the aspect of the botanical or horticultural, with more than 15 years in garden design,” Hayes continues. So what is she doing?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18hayes-baumgardner-2/18hayes-baumgardner-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18hayes-baumgardner-2/18hayes-baumgardner-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18hayes-baumgardner-2/18hayes-baumgardner-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18hayes-baumgardner-2/18hayes-baumgardner-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18hayes-baumgardner-2/18hayes-baumgardner-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18hayes-baumgardner-2/18hayes-baumgardner-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/18/t-magazine/18hayes-baumgardner-2/18hayes-baumgardner-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paula Hayes Reimagines a Victorian Totem in Madison Square Park', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boesky, Marianne', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dickinson, Emily', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feinstein, Rachel', 'rank': 6, 'major': '