'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Clean Air Task Force', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-18T23:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8835ee6e-1645-538d-b3e2-309c1e448d6f', 'word_count': 891, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8835ee6e-1645-538d-b3e2-309c1e448d6f'}, {'abstract': 'Why not a better hack?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/18/the-mystery-of-moore/', 'snippet': 'Why not a better hack?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jonathan Chait seems boggled at an op-ed from Stephen Moore, the chief economist at the Heritage Foundation, attacking Obamacare: Not one alleged fact cited in the piece is right. And we’re not talking about matters of interpretation. CBO has not changed its view that the ACA will reduce the deficit; health costs have not increased faster than before; premiums are not skyrocketing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Mystery of Moore', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-18T23:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4e2684e-2c75-5b4a-bcaf-606761456d31', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4e2684e-2c75-5b4a-bcaf-606761456d31'}, {'abstract': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center’s Film Comment Selects series features a tribute to Cannon Films.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/19/movies/film-society-of-lincoln-center-lauds-cannon-films.html', 'snippet': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center’s Film Comment Selects series features a tribute to Cannon Films.', 'lead_paragraph': '“My education was Bogart, Cagney and Cooper,” the director and producer Menahem Golan once said. Somewhere along the line, jiggling babes and giggling psychos, Sylvester Stallone and a voracious appetite for schlock became part of the mix, too. Mr. Golan is best known for the Cannon Group, the company he ran in the 1980s with his cousin Yoram Globus. The two men and the strange, wonderful and often terrible cinematic world they made are the subject of the buoyantly funny opening-night attraction of this year’s Film Comment Selects, the annual showcase that invades the Film Society of Lincoln Center on Friday, bringing with it edge and mess, a splatter of guts and some real glory. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/19/arts/19FILMCOMMENT1/19FILMCOMMENT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/19/arts/19FILMCOMMENT1/19FILMCOMMENT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/19/arts/19FILMCOMMENT1/19FILMCOMMENT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 624, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2