tract': 'Martin Lehmann knew the tale of Baby Rose, a child born with a heart defect, would not end well. But before it did, he was there to chronicle her life with a loving family.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/19/a-broken-heart-a-childs-life/', 'snippet': 'Martin Lehmann knew the tale of Baby Rose, a child born with a heart defect, would not end well. But before it did, he was there to chronicle her life with a loving family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Martin Lehmann says that between Afghanistan’s war and Thailand’s tsunami, he has photographed “so many dead people you wouldn’t believe it.” Yet nothing has affected him more than Rose, a little girl with “half a heart” who lived in Copenhagen, his hometown.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Broken Heart, a Child’s Life', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Childbirth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surgery and Surgeons', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Copenhagen (Denmark)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-19T08:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rena Silverman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silverman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0026a88-e5cf-5428-9fb6-cdad4da2a379', 'word_count': 918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0026a88-e5cf-5428-9fb6-cdad4da2a379'}, {'abstract': 'Will Shortz reports on the addition of new puzzles to The New York Times Magazine.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/19/good-puzzle-news-in-the-new-york-times-magazine/', 'snippet': 'Will Shortz reports on the addition of new puzzles to The New York Times Magazine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SPECIAL POST — Good news: Beginning today, the newly redesigned New York Times Magazine has an extra half-page of puzzles, making two pages altogether. The expanded puzzle section will contain three new mini-challenges of various sorts, all in the easy-to-easy/medium range, and all by well-known puzzlemakers. Everything will appear online, too (some of it in the form of PDFs), for Times crossword subscribers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Good Puzzle News in The New York Times Magazine', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-19T08:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Will Shortz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Will', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shortz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48ac9559-27d9-5a25-934b-f5d691534b3b', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48ac9559-27d9-5a25-934b-f5d691534b3b'}, {'abstract': 'Martha W. Lear, a reader, recalls a different experience for women in the chief medical examiner’s office, circa 1966.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/19/opinion/changes-at-the-morgue.html', 'snippet': 'Martha W. Lear, a reader, recalls a different experience for women in the chief medical examiner’s office, circa 1966.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Changes at the Morgue', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Changes at the Morgue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of the Chief Medical Examiner of the City of New York', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sampson, Barbara A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-19T08:27:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e1f1157-975d-5902-84ed-b3babb59f880', 'word_count': 104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e1f1157-975d-5902-84ed-b3babb59f880'}, {'abstract': 'We must demand more data, better teaching and fewer loan defaults.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/19/opinion/how-to-hold-colleges-accountable.html', 'snippet': 'We must demand more data, better teaching and fewer loan defaults.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — THE problems with college are well known. Too many students aren’t learning, don’t finish and fall deeply in debt. What will make college better? Federal officials, who are already alarmed by soaring tuitions, must tackle the mediocre education and outcomes at many schools with similar urgency. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Hold Colleges Accountable', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Hold Colleges Accountable', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Aid (Education)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'For-Profit Schools', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-19T08:27:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Cowan and Jim Kessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/261cc7dc-6d91-5948-81c7-41c24736ae60', 'word_count': 838, 'uri': 'nyt://article/261cc7dc-6d91-5948-81c7-41c24736ae60'}, {'abstract': 'New measures proposed by the Obama administration include many good ideas but do not do enough to protect the privacy of Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/19/opinion/regulating-the-drone-economy.html', 'snippet': 'New measures proposed by the Obama administration include many good ideas but do not do enough to protect the privacy of Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Interest in drones has been growing faster than government rules about how they can be used. That’s what makes the Obama administration’s  proposed rules for unmanned aircraft by businesses and federal agencies so important. The measures include many good ideas but do not do enough to protect the privacy of Americans.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Regulating the Drone Economy', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Regulating the Drone Economy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'sub