{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-19T08:27:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b8a9d9b-6fa8-5ec8-bd17-17de0c977346', 'word_count': 797, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b8a9d9b-6fa8-5ec8-bd17-17de0c977346'}, {'abstract': 'Her days as a tennis star were long gone.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/19/for-my-mother-one-last-grand-slam/', 'snippet': 'Her days as a tennis star were long gone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We were getting my mother ready for her big day. The indoor tennis facility in Maryland that she had helped build, and then managed for over 20 years, was being renamed in her honor by the Montgomery County Department of Parks. Hundreds of people were coming for the dedication ceremony. Billie Jean King would be there, so there was some cachet attached to the event. Billie Jean King was still somewhat famous. My mother, now 88, had also been famous, a long time ago. Still, this was better than never being famous at all. A dinner was planned at Bethesda Country Club. The day felt festive, like a wedding, people coming in from out of town, everyone getting prepared. I’d flown in from California the day before.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-privatelives/opinionator-pog-privatelives-articleLarge.png', 'height