n received of them. — The New York Herald, European Edition, Feb. 20, 1915', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1915: Neutral Ships Attacked', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-19T14:56:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c346d61c-19f7-5e68-96d6-ef061cd155e1', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c346d61c-19f7-5e68-96d6-ef061cd155e1'}, {'abstract': 'In “H Is for Hawk,” Helen Macdonald recounts her efforts to train a notoriously difficult bird of prey in the wake of her beloved father’s sudden death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/books/review/helen-macdonalds-h-is-for-hawk.html', 'snippet': 'In “H Is for Hawk,” Helen Macdonald recounts her efforts to train a notoriously difficult bird of prey in the wake of her beloved father’s sudden death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If birds are made of air, as the nature writer Sy Montgomery says, then writing a great bird book is a little like dusting for the fingerprints of a ghost. It calls for poetry and science, conjuring and evidence. In her breathtaking new book, “H Is for Hawk,” winner of the Samuel Johnson Prize and the Costa Book Award, Helen Macdonald renders an indelible impression of a raptor’s fierce essence — and her own — with words that mimic feathers, so impossibly pretty we don’t notice their astonishing engineering. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22CROKESUB/22CROKESUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22CROKESUB/22CROKESUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 704}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22CROKESUB/22CROKESUB-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 874, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22CROKESUB/22CROKESUB-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1520, 'width': 1296, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22CROKESUB/0222-bks-Croke-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22CROKESUB/0222-bks-Croke-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22CROKESUB/0222-bks-Croke-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Helen Macdonald’s ‘H Is for Hawk’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fly Away', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Macdonald, Helen (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hawks (Birds)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'H Is for Hawk (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-19T15:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vicki Constantine Croke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vicki', 'middlename': 'Constantine', 'lastname': 'Croke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2ffbb0a-ac17-5a4a-9021-6aa04b19f9dd', 'word_count': 1516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2ffbb0a-ac17-5a4a-9021-6aa04b19f9dd'}, {'abstract': 'Designers layered the prim and nostalgic top — infused with a little sass (and sexiness) — into their collections.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/19/new-york-fashion-week-fall-2015-victorian-lace-blouses/', 'snippet': 'Designers layered the prim and nostalgic top — infused with a little sass (and sexiness) — into their collections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'High-collared Victorian lace blouses rounded out many fall/winter 2015 collections over the past week, and were as diverse as the designers who sent them down the runway. Joseph Altuzarra went for a subtly sexy look, as he is wont to do, pairing one with his signature slit skirt (later, he also layered it under luxe fur coats and low V-neck dresses). At Oscar de la Renta, Peter Copping’s rendition felt prim and demure — despite being nearly transparent — thanks to the flouncy cream A-line skirt it accompanied. And at Zimmermann, under a ’70s-style jumpsuit, a more opaque version felt just as alluring as the others, but with a cooler, more bohemian vibe.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype'