{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yahoo! Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-19T20:43:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vindu Goel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vindu', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2569859-b67a-5478-9779-6489b6c3a4e7', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2569859-b67a-5478-9779-6489b6c3a4e7'}, {'abstract': 'Auction houses are now operating in “a massive global business focused on contemporary art,” Edward Dolman says, influenced by international collectors from all parts of the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/20/arts/design/chairman-of-phillips-sees-major-changes-in-auctions.html', 'snippet': 'Auction houses are now operating in “a massive global business focused on contemporary art,” Edward Dolman says, influenced by international collectors from all parts of the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was Phillips’s strongest contemporary sale in more than two years, with talked-about lots, including works by the Chinese artist Ai Weiwei and the Los Angeles-born Mark Bradford. On the heels of that success, in London last week, Phillips is preparing for its big spring auctions in New York. For May, Phillips says it already has a Cy Twombly lined up, “Untitled (Steps)” from 1960, estimated at $2 million to $3 million.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/20/arts/20INSIDEART/20INSIDEART-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/20/arts/20INSIDEART/20INSIDEART-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 4