e', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Graham Bowley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Graham', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bowley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad2a8c2d-2889-55f3-b9c4-550bfc4d5d39', 'word_count': 1021, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad2a8c2d-2889-55f3-b9c4-550bfc4d5d39'}, {'abstract': '“The Habsburgs: Rarely Seen Masterpieces from Europe’s Greatest Dynasty,” an exhibition now in Minnesota, features about 100 heirlooms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/arts/design/the-habsburgs-treasures-at-the-minneapolis-institute-of-arts.html', 'snippet': '“The Habsburgs: Rarely Seen Masterpieces from Europe’s Greatest Dynasty,” an exhibition now in Minnesota, features about 100 heirlooms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MINNEAPOLIS — Géza von Habsburg, an art historian in suburban New York, would have inherited part of an Austrian empire if only his ancestors had not made some terrible life choices. He did inherit the title of archduke and an interest in the history of luxury goods — the kind his family commissioned for centuries. Recently, he watched as about 100 of his family’s former heirlooms were installed at the Minneapolis Institute of Arts here for a new exhibition, “The Habsburgs: Rarely Seen Masterpieces from Europe’s Greatest Dynasty,” that runs through May. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/arts/22HAPBURG1/22HAPBURG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/22/arts/22HAPBURG1/22HAPBURG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None,