media', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': "Women's Runway", 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/57d52f14-34f1-5437-87b7-b09f4eae3cb0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/57d52f14-34f1-5437-87b7-b09f4eae3cb0'}, {'abstract': 'The report defines which problems need urgent attention, and what can be accomplished under the current administration.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/20/business/economy/obamas-economic-report-focuses-on-income-inequality.html', 'snippet': 'The report defines which problems need urgent attention, and what can be accomplished under the current administration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama on Thursday stepped up his efforts to define which economic problems need urgent attention — and what kind of attention would work — if not under his administration, then the next.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama’s Economic Report Focuses on Income Inequality', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Economic Report From Obama Focuses on Income Inequality', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harwood, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'White House Council of Economic Advisers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-19T21:27:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Harwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6701d5c4-7f51-56d9-ad7c-abe513595e51', 'word_count': 747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6701d5c4-7f51-56d9-ad7c-abe513595e51'}, {'abstract': '“A New Republic,” featuring 56 works by the award-winning artist, opens this week at the Brooklyn Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/19/kehinde-wiley-retrospective-brooklyn-museum/', 'snippet': '“A New Republic,” featuring 56 works by the award-winning artist, opens this week at the Brooklyn Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Barely two months in, it has already been a big year for the Brooklyn-based painter Kehinde Wiley. Last month, the artist received a 2014 U.S. State Department Medal of Arts; and this week, he prepares for his first museum retrospective, “A New Republic,” which will open at the Brooklyn Museum on Friday. Throughout his career, Wiley’s pieces have canonized the people they portray: Descendants from Africa, Haiti, Jamaica and elsewhere, depicted along with emblems of culture and identity. “My work, for a long time, has focused on the ideals that we celebrate in America and abroad,” he says. Many of his paintings outsource their influence from popular movements that predate the 19th century, like Flemish portraiture from the Northern Renaissance. But instead of using dark colors to create a sense of severity or authority, Wiley’s portraits explode with energy and color — particularly in their use of clothing, which occupies a central role in his work. “Fashion is fragile and fleeting,” he notes. “But it is also an indicator for the cultural and social appetites for a nation.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Sprawling Retrospective for Kehinde Wiley’s Heroic Portraits', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiley, Kehinde', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-19T21:30:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Johnny Magdaleno', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Johnny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Magdaleno', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24e4223f-c0ce-5da7-83cc-28e0392c6db9', 'word_count': 479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24e4223f-c0ce-5da7-83cc-28e0392c6db9'}, {'abstract': 'BottleRock Napa Valley, a music festival in May, will feature chefs and sommeliers in addition to bands like No Doubt and Imagine Dragons.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/19/at-napa-music-festival-chefs-are-in-the-lineup/', 'snippet': 'BottleRock Napa Valley, a music festival in May, will feature chefs and sommeliers in addition to bands like No Doubt and Imagine Dragons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chefs are often called the new rock stars, and now one California music festival plans to give some culinary headliners their own stage.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/19/travel/19dragons/19dragons-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/19/travel/19dragons/19dragons-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',