he quilts, made by generations of African-American women in a remote Alabama town, and Linda Day Clark’s related photographs have traveled to the Bronx.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many people are familiar with the Gee’s Bend quilts by now. Made by generations of African-American women who were the descendants of slaves, and later sharecroppers, the quilts developed on an isolated peninsula carved out by the Alabama River southwest of Selma. Over the last decade and a half, however, the art world has discovered Gee’s Bend, and the quilts have been exhibited in museums nationwide. A small show at the Lehman College Art Gallery of 14 quilts by seven artists, all made since 1990, and an accompanying exhibition of photographs, offers an update.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/20/arts/20GEESBEND/20GEESBEND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/20/arts/20GEESBEND/20GEESBEND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/20/arts/20GEESBEND/20GEESBEND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/20/arts/20GEESBEND/20GEESBEND-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/20/arts/20GEESBEND/20GEESBEND-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/20/arts/20GEESBEND/20GEESBEND-