f interior design.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York’s fall/winter 2015 Fashion Week went out on a high note, as per usual, with Marc Jacobs’s presentation at the Park Avenue Armory last night. As big a draw as the clothes (a darkly opulent ode to Diana Vreeland) and the people-watching (Nicki Minaj and Deborah Harry sat front-row) was the set: a Stefan Beckman-designed backdrop inspired by Jeremiah Goodman paintings of Vreeland’s famous red room, the latest in a series of immersive — and sometimes provocative — installations. Here, a look back at some of the brand’s other memorable set designs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art and Allure of Marc Jacobs’s Dramatic Stage Sets', 'kicker': 'By Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T05:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eviana Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eviana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c397ee7c-1432-5ac1-930b-dff51df71d66', 'word_count': 93, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c397ee7c-1432-5ac1-930b-dff51df71d66'}, {'abstract': 'This word has appeared in 824 New York Times articles in the past year.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/20/word-of-the-day-clout/', 'snippet': 'This word has appeared in 824 New York Times articles in the past year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'clout • \\ˈklau̇t\\ • noun and verb ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Word of the Day | clout', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T05:02:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/108a46ba-691e-56c2-b3d6-31cf3c583aff', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/108a46ba-691e-56c2-b3d6-31cf3c583aff'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/20/arts/television/whats-on-tv-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 A.M. (CUNY) A TIMESTALKS CONVERSATION: DAVID CARR INTERVIEWS LAURA POITRAS, GLENN GREENWALD AND EDWARD J. SNOWDEN On Feb. 12 Mr. Carr, who wrote about media for The New York Times, sat down at the New School with Ms. Poitras, the Oscar-nominated director of the documentary “Citizenfour,” about Mr. Snowden, the National Security Agency whistle-blower; Mr. Greenwald, the Pulitzer Prize-winning journalist who first wrote about Mr. Snowden’s revelations; and Mr. Snowden himself, who joined the discussion via a video link from Russia. Mr. Carr died later that night. (Repeats at 4 and 9 p.m.) In “Science Goes to the Movies,” a new series at 7:30 p.m., Faith Salie, an NPR host, and Heather Berlin, a neuroscientist, dissect the science portrayed in some current films, starting with hallucinations in “Birdman,” black holes and gravity in “Interstellar” and the work of the theoretical physicist Stephen Hawking in “The Theory of Everything.” Emily Rice, an astrophysicist with the Hayden Planetarium at the American Museum of Natural History, weighs in.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T06:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'se