paragraph': 'Good morning. Here is Daily Clip Report, a collection of articles about The Times and the media sent by email each morning to senior executives and newsroom editors. The email is produced by the Corporate Communications Department at The Times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Clip Report', 'kicker': 'Times Insider', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T13:11:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jordan Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jordan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04e06174-cbcd-5b8c-bc1c-b9be128734fb', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04e06174-cbcd-5b8c-bc1c-b9be128734fb'}, {'abstract': 'A veteran Afghanistan scholar and former American adviser shares his thoughts on China’s growing engagement with the troubled country.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/20/q-and-a-barnett-rubin-on-chinas-role-in-afghanistan/', 'snippet': 'A veteran Afghanistan scholar and former American adviser shares his thoughts on China’s growing engagement with the troubled country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent months, China has made pronouncements that reveal it is willing, perhaps even eager, to grow its engagement with troubled Afghanistan, whose remote Wakhan Corridor abuts China’s western border. On Feb. 12, while visiting Pakistan, Foreign Minister Wang Yi reiterated China’s desire to play a “constructive role” in a hypothetical peace process.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/18/world/18sino-rubin/18sino-rubin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 444, 'widt