ippet': 'A former journalist and adviser in Afghanistan says corruption is undermining American foreign policy and breeding insurgency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Across much of the world, populations suffer daily shakedowns by the police. At roadblocks, market stalls and entrances to government buildings, thugs in uniform gather “like spear fishermen hunting trout in a narrows,” as Sarah Chayes writes. But that isn’t the half of it. Globally, the three most important desiderata of our age — security, resilience and poverty reduction — are consistently being hollowed out by structural theft on a much larger scale, operating across corporations, governments, military establishments and civil services.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FODER/22FODER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FODER/22FODER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FODER/22FODER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 644, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FODER/22FODER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1288, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FODER/22FODER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FODER/22FODER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FODER/22FODER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Thieves of State,’ by Sarah Chayes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Everything Has Its Price', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chayes, Sarah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T17:41:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Giles Foden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Giles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Foden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc3036bf-f22e-59bb-b3c1-3a6fb8368ba7', 'word_count': 1254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc3036bf-f22e-59bb-b3c1-3a6fb8368ba7'}, {'abstract': 'Mathias Énard’s novel follows an anguished young Moroccan coming of age during the Arab uprisings of 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/books/review/street-of-thieves-by-mathias-enard.html', 'snippet': 'Mathias Énard’s novel follows an anguished young Moroccan coming of age during the Arab uprisings of 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mathias Énard is a French novelist who has gained recognition in Europe for the scale of his ambitions and the hurtling, anarchic energy of his prose. He has a large appetite for disaster, and his books are packed with wars, massacres, terrorism — with human depravity of every kind. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Street of Thieves,’ by Mathias Énard', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Days of Insurrection', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Worth, Robert F', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Enard, Mathias', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mandell, Charlotte', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T17:41:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert F. Worth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Worth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/abd896c8-f9b5-56a5-a212-020e31193156', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abd896c8-f9b5-56a5-a212-020e31193156'}, {'abstract': 'In Tom McCarthy’s novel, a ‘corporate anthropologist’ takes on the habits of homogenized global consumers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/books/review/tom-mccarthys-satin-island.html', 'snippet': 'In Tom McCarthy’s novel, a ‘corporate anthropologist’ takes on the habits of homogenized global consumers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After traveling widely throughout the world and studying its various peoples in all their kaleidoscopic glory, Claude Lévi-Strauss, the father of structural \xadanthropology, finally arrived at a dispiriting conclusion. “Mankind has opted for monoculture,” he observed ruefully in “Tristes Tropiques,” his classic 1955 \xadmemoir-cum-case study detailing his years spent among the indigenous tribes of Brazil. “It is in the process of creating a mass civilization, as beetroot is grown in the mass. Henceforth, man’s daily bill of fare will consist only of this one item.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22TURRENTINE/22TURRENTINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 587, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22TURRENTINE/22TURRENTINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 587}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22TURRENTINE/22TURRENTINE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1002, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22TURRENTINE/22TURRENTINE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2004, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22TURRENTINE/22TURRENTINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22TURRENTINE/22TURRENTINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22TURRENTINE/22TURRENTINE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tom McCarthy’s ‘Satin Island’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Culture-Jamming Provocateur', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Tom (1969- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Satin Island (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T17:41:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Turrentine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Turrentine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/10360b6d-01bb-5f59-94a5-55ed45e10d3d', 'word_count': 1250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10360b6d-01bb-5f59-94a5-55ed45e10d3d'}, {'abstract': 'In the stories in Jonathan Lethem’s third collection, the everyday conceals the absurd.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/books/review/jonathan-lethems-lucky-alan.html', 'snippet': 'In the stories in Jonathan Lethem’s third collection, the everyday conceals the absurd.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jonathan Lethem’s extraordinary career is a reminder of the not-so-distant past when working novelists published their new creations regularly and with a seemingly free-flowing hand. If one book wasn’t up to snuff, there would be another to redeem it a year or two later. It was all part of the ebb and flow of a lifetime of work. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22GREENBERG/22GREENBERG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 472, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22GREENBERG/22GREENBERG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 472}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22GREENBERG/22GREENBERG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 805, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22GREENBERG/22GREENBERG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1610, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22GREENBERG/22GREENBERG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22GREENBERG/22GREENBERG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22GREENBERG/22GREENBERG-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jonathan Lethem’s ‘Lucky Alan’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Under the Surface', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lethem, Jonathan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T17:43:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Greenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a116cad3-7e78-5d73-b53d-670900881a1f', 'word_count': 1211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a116cad3-7e78-5d73-b53d-670900881a1f'}, {'abstract': 'In Simenon’s non-Maigret “thrillers,” the central character is lured from the cocoon of himself into a wider world of sexual and philosophical peril.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/books/review/the-case-of-georges-simenon.html', 'snippet': 'In Simenon’s non-Maigret “thrillers,” the central character is lured from the cocoon of himself into a wider world of sexual and philosophical peril.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Georges Simenon (1903-89) had, at the very least, two identities. One was the Belgian altar boy who quickly made the transition from failed pastry chef to legendary cub reporter for the Gazette de Liège when he was only 16 and went on to write nearly 200 pulp novels between 1924 and 1931 (he referred to them as his “novels for secretaries”). Then, when he decided to write “good” books, he took the advice of his mentor, Colette — “Get rid of all the literature, and you’ve got it” — and spent the next five decades producing more than 200 serious novels, keeping meticulous accounts of his multitudinous foreign translations and film adaptations, and moving his family from the French countryside to Arizona, New England, Quebec and back to Europe again, even while finding time to enjoy three very French family meals each day, and to walk with his adored children in the local parks.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22BRADFIELD2/22BRADFIELD2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22BRADFIELD2/22BRADFIELD2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 638}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22BRADFIELD2/22BRADFIELD2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 963, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22BRADFIELD2/22BRADFIELD2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 1880, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22BRADFIELD2/22BRADFIELD2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22BRADFIELD2/22BRADFIELD2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22BRADFIELD2/22BRADFIELD2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case of Georges Simenon', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Case of Georges Simenon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simenon, Georges', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T17:43:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Bradfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f26bd266-06a8-5378-a68e-897a97fb52a5', 'word_count': 1438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f26bd266-06a8-5378-a68e-897a97fb52a5'}, {'abstract': 'For Gail Hareven’s heroine, an abusive relative’s return to Jerusalem stokes a desire for retribution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/books/review/lies-first-person-by-gail-hareven.html', 'snippet': 'For Gail Hareven’s heroine, an abusive relative’s return to Jerusalem stokes a desire for retribution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why are so many of our novels so good-mannered these days? Where is Ignatius J. Reilly’s cackle, the self-thwarting abdication of Graham Greene’s comedians, the fear and loathing of Dostoyevsky’s underground man? Where are the lunatics, madmen, misfits and misanthropes, the perfectly good people cracking up and squandering it all to nourish some sweet, unsubtle hatred? With so much enmity in our world, why is there so little in modern American fiction? Is there a liberal bias in books, too?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FISHMAN/22FISHMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 905, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FISHMAN/22FISHMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 905}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FISHMAN/22FISHMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FISHMAN/22FISHMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1358, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FISHMAN/22FISHMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FISHMAN/22FISHMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22FISHMAN/22FISHMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Lies, First Person,’ by Gail Hareven', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vengeance Is Hers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fishman, Boris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hareven, Gail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T17:43:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Boris Fishman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Boris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fishman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ce119d8-126f-5fce-b8e8-32342c7bea9e', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ce119d8-126f-5fce-b8e8-32342c7bea9e'}, {'abstract': 'A man practicing gun-slinging tricks killed a pregnant woman.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/20/wait-guns-do-kill-people/', 'snippet': 'A man practicing gun-slinging tricks killed a pregnant woman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last July, William DeHayes was practicing some innocent gun-slinging tricks in his kitchen in Brooksville, Florida, when he accidentally shot Katherine Hoover, the wife of his old friend, point blank in the temple as she sat eating at the kitchen table. Ms. Hoover was five months pregnant at the time, and doctors tried in vain to save both her and her fetus, a boy she had named Rehlin.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wait, Guns Do Kill People?', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Rifle Assn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T17:44:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Wegman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wegman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52ff578a-9224-5459-8ae5-ac118be775a8', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52ff578a-9224-5459-8ae5-ac118be775a8'}, {'abstract': 'A 10-room penthouse on the 14th and 15th floors of 1045 Fifth Avenue is poised to enter the market for the first time ever.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/realestate/fifth-avenue-penthouse-with-central-park-views.html', 'snippet': 'A 10-room penthouse on the 14th and 15th floors of 1045 Fifth Avenue is poised to enter the market for the first time ever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An anachronism in an Upper Fifth Avenue neighborhood dominated by the Metropolitan Museum of Art and limestone co-ops with prewar pedigrees, 1045 Fifth Avenue, the apartment building the developer Manny E. Duell considered his masterpiece, is a bronze glass column crowned by a duplex penthouse with two levels of panoramic 40-foot vistas across Central Park’s reservoir. From the start, the one-of-a-kind penthouse was reserved and customized for Mr. Duell, who died in 1977 just a decade after moving in, as a present to his wife, Irene, who lived and entertained there until her own death last summer at age 92.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Glass Amid the Limestone ', 'kicker': 'Exclusive', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Glass Amid the Limestone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Finn, Robin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fifth Avenue (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper East Side (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T17:44:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Finn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a19a3c59-d2cf-54ab-b1d3-75229b83deb7', 'word_count': 732, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a19a3c59-d2cf-54ab-b1d3-75229b83deb7'}, {'abstract': 'The longtime collaborators Tina Fey and Robert Carlock are returning to producing television (albeit the streaming variety) with a new series starring Ellie Kemper.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/arts/television/tina-feys-unbreakable-kimmy-schmidt-heads-to-netflix.html', 'snippet': 'The longtime collaborators Tina Fey and Robert Carlock are returning to producing television (albeit the streaming variety) with a new series starring Ellie Kemper.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If “30 Rock,” their screwball sitcom about television’s backstage antics, had lasted a bit longer, Tina Fey and Robert Carlock say, they might have pursued a plot about the fatuous executive Jack Donaghy and his further degradation of the traditional broadcast TV model. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'mult