'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Balthazar (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T18:04:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris and Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cfdd090-c817-5e8a-9094-e0acf3cd6821', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cfdd090-c817-5e8a-9094-e0acf3cd6821'}, {'abstract': 'The protagonist of David Duchovny’s debut novel is a talking cow who sets out for India.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'The protagonist of David Duchovny’s debut novel is a talking cow who sets out for India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Out There: From 1993 to 2002, David Duchovny starred in “The X-Files” as the persistent and slightly paranoid F.B.I. special agent Fox Mulder, investigator of and true believer in paranormal activity. Then, from 2007 to 2014, he took on the even scarier role of Hank Moody, the wild-boy writer in “Californication” — surely the most debauched novelist to appear on television since Angela Lansbury’s Jessica Fletcher in “Murder, She Wrote.” But Lansbury never produced a novel of her own. Duchovny has done just that, and the book — “Holy Cow,” his debut — enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 16. It’s not necessarily the novel you’d expect from the man who played Hank Moody. The protagonist, Elsie Bovary, is a talking cow who sets out for India after she learns that cattle there are viewed as more than hamburger meat; along the way she’s joined by a pig determined to reach Israel (where pork is off the menu) and a turkey bound for Turkey (“That was really just a play on words,” Duchovny told the trade magazine Kirkus Reviews). But beneath the goofy plot, which The Huffington Post described, not wholly favorably, as “a mash-up between ‘Animal Farm’ and ‘Chicken Run,’ ” Duchovny is thinking seriously about environmentalism and animal rights. “It’s an attitude of brotherhood,” he told Kirkus, “not only with other human beings, but with all the animals on the planet. I think it’s because of climate change that everyone is coming around to the idea that this is the finite planet and there’s a lot of unnecessary pain and overconsumption on it and there are things around us that have feelings that we don’t normally ascribe to having feelings. I don’t think rocks have feelings; I draw the line there.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22duchovny/22duchovny-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 268, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22duchovny/22duchovny-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 268, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22duchovny/22duchovny-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22duchovny/22duchovny-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/books/review/22duchovny/22duchovny-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-20T18:09:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb4ed337-8c95-5db2-acf8-0a4414b5a3d0', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb4ed337-8c95-5db2-acf8-0a4414b5a3d0'}, {'abstract': 'Nick Zito, a Hall of Fame trainer, will learn Saturday whether he has a player for the Kentucky Derby when Frammento runs in the Fountain of Youth Stakes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/21/sports/nick-zito-yearns-to-revive-stable-and-recapture-the-glory-of-the-big-races.html', 'snippet': 'Nick Zito, a Hall of Fame trainer, will learn Saturday whether he has a player for the Kentucky Derby when Frammento runs in the Fountain of Youth Stakes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOYNTON BEACH, Fla. — When Nick Zito ranked among the most prominent trainers the previous two decades, this time of year was special to him. It brought an unveiling of sorts as his newly turned 3-year-old colts justified his keen eye for talent by distinguishing themselves in prep races ahead of the Kentucky Derby.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/21/sports/21RACINGweb1/21RACI