w', 'section_name': 'NYT Now', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/cdafd916-dc3d-5508-a1d7-992c8af8e2db', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/cdafd916-dc3d-5508-a1d7-992c8af8e2db'}, {'abstract': 'This Italian composer explores the character of sounds, as opposed to pitch or volume.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/21/arts/music/review-works-by-stefano-gervasoni-at-miller-theater.html', 'snippet': 'This Italian composer explores the character of sounds, as opposed to pitch or volume.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the serialists to the spectralists, a fascination with timbre has long been a feature of contemporary composition. As the Italian composer Stefano Gervasoni, 52, explained during his worthwhile Composer Portrait at Miller Theater on Thursday, he started dealing explicitly with the character of sounds, as opposed to their pitch or volume, a decade ago.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/21/arts/21GERVASONI/21GERVASONI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 320, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/21/arts/21GERVASONI/21GERVASONI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 320}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/21/arts/21GERVASONI/21GERVASONI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 546, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/21/arts/21GERVASONI/21GERVASONI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1092, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'i