or': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-21T01:05:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Cunningham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cunningham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c90b138d-b393-5f6e-8593-d4cb58057b13', 'word_count': 37, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c90b138d-b393-5f6e-8593-d4cb58057b13'}, {'abstract': 'Officials cleared the way for an obstetrician-gynecologist to return to work after his license was suspended last year over accusations that he had sex with patients and drank on the job.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/21/us/new-mexico-board-votes-to-end-doctors-suspension.html', 'snippet': 'Officials cleared the way for an obstetrician-gynecologist to return to work after his license was suspended last year over accusations that he had sex with patients and drank on the job.', 'lead_paragraph': 'State medical officials have cleared the way for an obstetrician-gynecologist in Hobbs to return to practice after his medical license was suspended last year over accusations including having sex with patients and drinking on the job. The New Mexico Medical Board voted Thursday to lift the suspension of Christopher S. Driskill, the president-elect of the New Mexico Medical Society and one of the few practicing obstetrician-gynecologists around Hobbs, a city of about 40,000 on the eastern edge of the state. Dr. Driskill, who completed an evaluation at the Sexual Recovery Institute in Los Angeles as part of the settlement, can resume his practice after completing a course on professional and ethical behavior, board officials said. Upon his return, he is required to submit to monitoring, including random drug testing, therapy and having a chaperone with him when he meets patients. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', '