: 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mueth, Kate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Zima! (Play)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-21T05:47:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aileen Jacobson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aileen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c586b493-3a98-5409-b0a8-f7f31c50be89', 'word_count': 969, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c586b493-3a98-5409-b0a8-f7f31c50be89'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/21/arts/television/whats-on-tv-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (HBO) THE FAULT IN OUR STARS (2014) Two teenage “cancer kids” — Hazel Grace Lancaster (Shailene Woodley), who expects to die soon from the thyroid cancer that has plagued her since childhood, and Augustus Waters (Ansel Elgort), known as Gus, who lost part of his leg to cancer but has been healthy since — fall in love while trying to lead extraordinary lives in Josh Boone’s adaptation of the young-adult novel by John Green. Willem Dafoe plays Peter Van Houten, the misanthropic author of Hazel’s favorite cancer story, who lectures the couple on the difference between fiction and reality; Sam Trammell and Laura Dern are Hazel’s stricken parents; and Nat Wolff is Gus’s slightly goofy wingman. “Though it is a tragic love story, it is also a perfect and irresistible fantasy,” A. O. Scott wrote of the film in The New York Times. “Hazel and Gus possess an absolute moral authority, an ability to assert the truth of their experience that few can share and many might covet.” He added, “The loudest weeping you hear — including your own — may arise not from grief or admiration, but from envy.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-21T06:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/57720d4b-b16a-5692-af77-5607aaf5e7c2', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57720d4b-b16a-5692-af77-5607aaf5e7c2'}, {'abstract': "The exhibition “'Not Really’: Fictive Narratives in Contemporary Art” addresses the proliferation of contrivance and manipulation in 21st-century society.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/nyregion/at-the-college-of-new-rochelle-a-show-meant-to-provoke-double-takes.html', 'snippet': "The exhibition “'Not Really’: Fictive Narratives in Contemporary Art” addresses the proliferation of contrivance and manipulation in 21st-century society.", 'lead_paragraph': 'Entering the Castle Gallery at the College of New Rochelle, visitors encounter an arrangement of cherry blossoms in a glass vase. At least that’s what it appears to be. Closer observation reveals that the branch holding the flowers is actually a segment of plumbing pipe and that each delicate bud is a tiny pink toilet made of urethane foam. ', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/nyregion/22ARTSWE2/22ARTSWE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xl