: {'original': 'By Matt Richtel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richtel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e895fb7-c99c-5e8a-940c-e513b24774ed', 'word_count': 1277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e895fb7-c99c-5e8a-940c-e513b24774ed'}, {'abstract': 'Pitcher Nathan Eovaldi had a tough year last season with the Miami Marlins, but his velocity and fire portend a turnaround with the Yankees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/22/sports/baseball/eovaldis-untapped-potential-has-the-yankees-dreaming-of-big-things.html', 'snippet': 'Pitcher Nathan Eovaldi had a tough year last season with the Miami Marlins, but his velocity and fire portend a turnaround with the Yankees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAMPA, Fla. — When pitchers and catchers reported for spring training Friday, there were questions about the frayed right elbow of Masahiro Tanaka, the surgically repaired right knee of C. C. Sabathia and the expected return of Ivan Nova from elbow surgery by June. Then there is the frailty of Michael Pineda, who has been a revelation when he has not been on the disabled list, which is almost constantly.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/sports/DOG-22yankees/DOG-22yankees-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/22/sports/DOG-22yankees/DOG-22yankees-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/22/sports/DOG-22yankees/DOG-22yankees-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'r