 'nyt://article/1d6f8d7e-0063-533f-abbe-743cee1cbaf6', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d6f8d7e-0063-533f-abbe-743cee1cbaf6'}, {'abstract': 'One program, on History, is a scripted series based on an account of a federal informant, while the other, on WGN, is a reality show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/24/arts/television/gangland-undercover-and-outlaw-country-examine-biker-life.html', 'snippet': 'One program, on History, is a scripted series based on an account of a federal informant, while the other, on WGN, is a reality show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s an interesting week in the ever-evolving relationship between television and crime. The History channel, no doubt hoping to fill the “Sons of Anarchy” void, puts motorcycle gangs back on the air with “Gangland Undercover,” based on a federal informant’s real story. And WGN tries to push the police ride-along show to the next dubious level by adding a potential-lawbreaker ride-along.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/arts/24OUTLAW/24OUTLAW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/24/arts/24OUTLAW/24OUTLAW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/arts/24OUTLAW/24OUTLAW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/arts/24OUTLAW/24OUTLAW-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/arts/24OUTLAW/24OUTLAW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/24/arts/24OUTLAW/24OUTLAW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/arts/24OUTLAW/24OUTLAW-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Gangland Undercover’ and ‘Outlaw Country’ Examine Biker Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bikers and Their Dramatic Turf', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Motorcycles, Motor Bikes and Motorscooters', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'History Channel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Outlaw Country (TV Program)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Gangland Undercover (TV Program)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-23T22:28:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8919b5d6-5a99-5f59-9335-7699da579bc7', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8919b5d6-5a99-5f59-9335-7699da579bc7'}, {'abstract': 'When injected with human DNA, mouse embryos grew brains that were 12 percent larger than those of embryos injected with the same genes from chimpanzees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/24/science/mice-brain-may-give-insight-into-diseases-of-human-mind.html', 'snippet': 'When injected with human DNA, mouse embryos grew brains that were 12 percent larger than those of embryos injected with the same genes from chimpanzees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When injected with bits of human DNA, mouse embryos grew brains that were 12 percent larger than those of embryos injected with the same genes from chimpanzees. Scientists at Duke University, writing in the journal Current Biology, say the experiment demonstrates the role that a particular gene sequence, HARE5, plays in the development of the human brain, which is far heavier and more complex than the brains of our closest animal cousins. The research also may help reveal why humans develop conditions like Alzheimer’s and autism, but chimpanzees do not.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/science/24FLOATER/24FLOATER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 974, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/24/science/24FLOATER/24FLOATER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 974}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/science/24FLOATER/24FLOATER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 631, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/science/24FLOATER/24FLOATER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1944, 'width': 1197, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/science/24FLOATER/24FLOATER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/20