he second single from “Jackrabbit,” the sophomore album from the indie-rock band, which has roots in classical composition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/24/t-magazine/a-melancholy-and-danceable-new-song-from-brooklyns-san-fermin.html', 'snippet': '“Emily” is the second single from “Jackrabbit,” the sophomore album from the indie-rock band, which has roots in classical composition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brooklyn-based baroque-pop octet San Fermin doesn’t approach new songs the way most bands in its zip code do — by assembling in a rehearsal space and jamming until something worth recording emerges. Instead, its songwriter and composer Ellis Ludwig-Leone writes preliminary versions of all eight parts for each new song himself, and then delivers them to his bandmates in the basement below his apartment, where they gather to practice. “The worst thing that I ever do with the band is rehearse a new song with them,” he says with a laugh. “Suddenly it’s like, ‘Oh, my God, I hope this is good, because we’re going to have to play this hundreds of times,’ and it’s terrifying.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/23/t-magazine/23fermin-symonds/23fermin-symonds-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/23/t-magazine/23fermin-symonds/23fermin-symonds-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/23/t-magazine/23fermin-symonds/23fermin-symonds-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/23/t-magazine/23fermin-symonds/23fermin-symonds-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/23/t-magazine/23fermin-symonds/23fermin-symonds-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/23/t-magazine/23fermin-symonds/23fermin-symonds-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/23/t-magazine/23fermin-symonds/23fermin-symonds-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Melancholy — and Danceable — New Song from Brooklyn’s San Fermin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-24T05:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandria Symonds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Symonds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbd5f2f8-7975-5810-8ddb-3ae8ca798149', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbd5f2f8-7975-5810-8ddb-3ae8ca798149'}, {'abstract': 'The chef shares a dish from his Little Park restaurant, which will be served tonight at the magazine’s ad hoc dining club’s opening party at One World Trade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/24/t-magazine/winter-salad-recipe-andrew-carmellini-bon-appetit.html', 'snippet': 'The chef shares a dish from his Little Park restaurant, which will be served tonight at the magazine’s ad hoc dining club’s opening party at One World Trade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bon Appétit magazine’s BA Kitchen was already home to some of the most exclusive culinary events in the city, having opened its doors to celebrated chefs such as Carlo Mirarchi (Roberta’s), David Kinch (Manresa) and Inaki Aizpitarte (Le Chateaubriand). But now, with Condé Nast an official tenant of One World Trade Center, the ad hoc dining club will have a setting worthy of its food. “As good as a job as we did before, you can’t compare it to being on the 35th floor of the World Trade Center overlooking the city with a view of the Empire State,” says the Bon Appétit editor in chief Adam Rapoport, who co-hosts the new space’s opening party this evening. And he’s tapped the James Beard Award-winning chef Andrew Carmellini to christen the place. “Adam’s an old friend, and we’re in the neighborhood already,” explains Carmellini, whose fashionable restaurants (Little Park, the Dutch, Locanda Verde) are, like the new Condé Nast headquarters, a Lower Manhattan fixture. “We’re going to have a party.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24carmellini-reese-1/24carmellini-reese-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24carmellini-reese-1/24carmellini-reese-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24carmellini-reese-1/24carmellini-reese-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24carmellini-reese-1/24carmellini-reese-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'ran