rk bill, and Gov. Scott Walker has promised to sign it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Scott Walker won the nation’s attention four years ago by pushing to limit collective bargaining rights for most of Wisconsin’s public workers, but for months now, he has seemed to sidestep the possibility of any new moves against labor groups.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With Right-to-Work Bill, Scott Walker Risks a New Labor Fight', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collective Bargaining', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fitzgerald, Scott L', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walker, Scott K', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-24T13:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Davey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dff9cc24-316e-5521-9560-3f94b0ba5644', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dff9cc24-316e-5521-9560-3f94b0ba5644'}, {'abstract': 'We seem eager to sprinkle math-related terms as liberally as possible in our prose, but casual usage is more likely to suggest fuzziness or even laziness than rigor and precision.', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/24/words-we-love-too-much-14/', 'snippet': 'We seem eager to sprinkle math-related terms as liberally as possible in our prose, but casual usage is more likely to suggest fuzziness or even laziness than rigor and precision.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps hoping to disprove the cliché that journalists hate math, we seem eager to sprinkle a couple of math-related terms as liberally as possible in our prose. Unfortunately, this casual usage is more likely to suggest fuzziness or even laziness than rigor and precision.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Words We Love Too Much', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-24T13:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f14b6227-e267-5821-a0c6-55df629da50e', 'word_count': 1350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f14b6227-e267-5821-a0c6-55df629da50e'}, {'abstract': 'A video performance of “Giants in the Sky” from the Fiasco Theater’s production of “Into the Woods.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/24/in-performance-patrick-mulryan-of-into-the-woods/', 'snippet': 'A video performance of “Giants in the Sky” from the Fiasco Theater’s production of “Into the Woods.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s video features the actor Patrick Mulryan, with Matt Castle on piano, singing the number “Giants in the Sky” from the Fiasco Theater’s production of the Stephen Sondheim and James Lapine musical “Into the Woods.” The show, directed by Noah Brody and Ben Steinfeld, is at the Roundabout’s Laura Pels Theater through April 12.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: Patrick Mulryan of ‘Into the Woods’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mulryan, Patrick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sondheim, Stephen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiasco Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-24T13:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a68be53-a8a6-5fe0-b4a2-60ff7d8f530a', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a68be53-a8a6-5fe0-b4a2-60ff7d8f530a'}, {'abstract': 'How is this supposed to happen, exactly?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/24/phantom-phiscal-crises/', 'snippet': 'How is this supposed to happen, exactly?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Matthew Klein takes on a subject dear to my heart — the mysterious, persistent fear that Japan and other countries that borrow in their own currencies could suddenly face a Greek-style fiscal crisis if bond investors lose confidence, and that this is a reason to raise taxes or cut benefits even in a depressed economy. I devoted my Mundell-Fleming lecture at the IMF to this topic, where I had this to say:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Phantom Phiscal Crises', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-24T13:08:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {