ss', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Abrams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Abrams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ec53d78-dd43-57f2-8566-e3d1a17a3574', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ec53d78-dd43-57f2-8566-e3d1a17a3574'}, {'abstract': 'The restaurateur Brendan Sodikoff’s latest venture offers Texas barbecue, about as authentic as it can be 1,000 miles northeast of Austin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/travel/restaurant-report-green-street-smoked-meats-in-chicago.html', 'snippet': 'The restaurateur Brendan Sodikoff’s latest venture offers Texas barbecue, about as authentic as it can be 1,000 miles northeast of Austin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chicago does not want for meat in general, or the barbecued kind in particular. The city Carl Sandburg called the “hog butcher for the world" boils over with stellar kielbasas and, at least on the South Side, Delta-style vinegar-forward barbecue. There’s even a mash-up of traditional barbecue and Eastern European meats called boilbecue (not recommended by this meat-eater).', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01BITESSUB/01BITESSUB-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01BITESSUB/01BITESSUB-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01BITESSUB/01BITESSUB-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'capt