son-new-museum-triennial/', 'snippet': 'The Brooklyn-based artist Frank Benson’s piece in the show is a hyper-realistic, life-size 3D print of the downtown transgender figure Juliana Huxtable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brooklyn-based sculptor Frank Benson is one of the only hometown artists included in the New Museum’s thoroughly international 2015 Triennial exhibition. Another is the muse for his new piece, the emerging downtown legend Juliana Huxtable, who as an artist in her own right will also contribute a series of photographic self-portraits to the show. Benson’s piece, titled “Juliana” (2015), is a hyper-realistic, life-size 3D print of the transgender beauty, the third in a series of figurative sculptures whose previous subjects were a nude man and clothed woman. Draped horizontally on a plinth with fingers pinched in a Cleopatran gesture, Huxtable was originally meant to be immortalized in one of her signature looks before Benson, apprehensively, asked her to pose nude. “I was nervous of what she might think of that,” he says, “so I sent her this intense email full of historical references.” And though she is without clothing, her personal style is retained in the braids Benson painstakingly drew in a digital drafting program and the green makeup spray-painted onto the statue’s skin. The artwork stands as a token of how virtuosity and virtuality come together in the digital age. “I want the sculpture to exist as a completely finished entity inside the computer,” Benson says. “The 3D model is its ultimate version and the print is the real-world manifestation of it.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24symonds-sculpture1/24symonds-sculpture1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24symonds-sculpture1/24symonds-sculpture1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24symonds-sculpture1/24symonds-sculpture1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24symonds-sculpture1/24symonds-sculpture1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24symonds-sculpture1/24symonds-sculpture1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24symonds-sculpture1/24symonds-sculpture1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/24/t-magazine/24symonds-sculpture1/24symonds-sculpture1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At the 2015 New Museum Triennial, A High-Tech Take on Nude Sculpture', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Museum of Contemporary Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-24T19:30:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin McGarry', 'pe