 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-24T22:08:14+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/9b59b823-3af7-5cbe-bb6b-752b2f8989a2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/9b59b823-3af7-5cbe-bb6b-752b2f8989a2'}, {'abstract': '“Broken Story (wherein there is no ecstasy),” which had its premiere at the Guggenheim Museum, tweaks dance traditions of romantic fulfillment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/25/arts/dance/review-in-pam-tanowitzs-broken-story-wit-comes-full-circle.html', 'snippet': '“Broken Story (wherein there is no ecstasy),” which had its premiere at the Guggenheim Museum, tweaks dance traditions of romantic fulfillment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In several ways, Pam Tanowitz’s “Broken Story (wherein there is no ecstasy)” is accurately titled. The parenthetical phrase might mislead, though. The work, which had its premiere at the Guggenheim Museum on Sunday as part of the Works & Process series, does have droll fun tweaking dance traditions of romantic fulfillment. But Ms. Tanowitz and her collaborators maintain such a high level of imagination that what is fragmented by design can be experienced as a satisfying, organic whole. Ecstatic is a good word for how it made me feel. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/arts/25PROCESS/25PROCESS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/25/arts/25PROCESS/25PROCESS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/arts/25PROCESS/25PROCESS-jumbo.jpg', 'hei