ajor': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Churchill (Play)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-24T22:40:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Collins-Hughes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins-Hughes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c79d53d-108a-58a9-8ff7-0f9333de3645', 'word_count': 436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c79d53d-108a-58a9-8ff7-0f9333de3645'}, {'abstract': 'In abandoning a controversial exhibition, this institution in Mexico City may have hurt its credibility in the art world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/25/arts/design/museo-jumex-cancels-a-hermann-nitsch-show.html', 'snippet': 'In abandoning a controversial exhibition, this institution in Mexico City may have hurt its credibility in the art world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — Hermann Nitsch is hardly a stranger to controversy. This Austrian artist, whose notorious “actions” involve animal viscera and “crucified” participants bathed in blood, was arrested three times as a young man and expelled from Italy for disemboweling a sheep during a performance. But in all these years, Mr. Nitsch, 76, said in an interview, he has “never” had an institution cancel an exhibition of his work. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Museo Jumex Cancels a Hermann Nitsch Show', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Blood in the Street, Not on the Walls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burnett, Victoria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museo Jumex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico City (Mexico)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nitsch, Hermann', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-24T22:43:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victoria Burnett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victoria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burnett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fad48024-bfc4-508c-8a6b-06f346b6ff6c', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fad48024-bfc4-508c-8a6b-06f346b6ff6c'}, {'abstract': 'The Piano Studio of the Mason Gross School of the Arts at Rutgers, in a program called “The Unknown Diabelli Variations,” strayed far from the beaten path.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/25/arts/music/review-diabolical-diabelli-at-rutgers-with-new-variations.html', 'snippet': 'The Piano Studio of the Mason Gross School of the Arts at Rutgers, in a program called “The Unknown Diabelli Variations,” strayed far from the beaten path.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Piano Studio of the Mason Gross School of the Arts at Rutgers pulled off a neat trick last week. Its program at Weill Recital Hall, “The Unknown Diabelli Variations,” strayed far from the beaten path and, most notably, avoided Beethoven, the composer of the actual work called “Diabelli Variations.” Yet Beethoven’s ghost was a constant presence, looming over the proceedings with, undoubtedly, a wry smirk.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/02/arts/25DIABELLI/25DIABELLI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/02/arts/25DIABELLI/25DIABELLI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/02/arts/25DIABELLI/25DIABELLI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/02/arts/25DIABELLI/25DIABELLI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/02/arts/25DIABELLI/25DIABELLI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/02/arts/25DIABELLI/25DIABELLI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/02/arts/25DIABELLI/25DIABELLI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Diabolical Diabelli at Rutgers, With New Variations', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mason Gross School of the Arts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-24T22:52:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/62