com’s showrunner talks about that political cameo and whether or not Leslie made it to higher office.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite her negative-sounding surname, Leslie Knope (Amy Poehler) always stood out as one of TV’s most positive and upbeat characters, and this Pawnee, Ind., civil servant’s can-do spirit radiated throughout the sunny series finale of “Parks and Recreation.” Employing a flash-forward structure, the NBC sitcom’s send-off gave each of its characters a happy ending. For example, Leslie was elected governor of Indiana twice, with her beloved husband, Ben (Adam Scott), by her side. The show also implied that one of them ultimately reached even higher office.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/arts/television/schur2qanda/schur2qanda-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/25/arts/television/schur2qanda/schur2qanda-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/arts/television/schur2qanda/schur2qanda-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/arts/television/schur2qanda/schur2qanda-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/arts/television/schur2qanda/schur2qanda-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/25/arts/television/schur2qanda/schur2qanda-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/arts/television/schur2qanda/schur2qanda-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michael Schur on the ‘Parks and Recreation’ Series Finale', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Jill Tracy Jacobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Joseph R Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poehler, Amy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pratt, Chris', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schur, Michael', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Adam (1973- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-25T14:44:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Fretts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fretts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b824998e-142b-59af-82d8-f12aabab9816', 'word_count': 920, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b824998e-142b-59af-82d8-f12aabab9816'}, {'abstract': 'A picture book tells the story of the real bear who inspired A. A. Milne’s Winnie-the-Pooh.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/25/books/review/winnie-by-sally-m-walker.html', 'snippet': 'A picture book tells the story of the real bear who inspired A. A. Milne’s Winnie-the-Pooh.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the annals of human-animal comradeship, there is nothing quite like the tale of a brown bear cub named Winnipeg, Winnie for short, the inspiration behind A. A. Milne’s “Winnie-the-Pooh.” She was bought for $20 by a Canadian soldier at the outset of World War I, sailed with him to England, then ended up living out her years at the London Zoo. There, a little boy named Christopher Robin took a shine to her, renaming his own stuffed bear Winnie and prompting his father to write the classic children’s books. As a new picture book by Sally M. Walker plays it, this is a “gee-whiz” kind of story, with one improbable, satisfying moment after another and little to no conflict. Jonathan D. Voss’s sweetly realistic, nostalgic watercolor illustrations go down easy as well, casting a benign glow even over scenes of army life during wartime. The little brown bear is adorable and affectionate, winning the heart of every human she comes into contact with, and her owner, Harry Colebourn, is a paragon of kind, virtuous, never-say-never manhood. Though Winnie earned her place in history accidentally, Walker’s storybook narration makes her legendary status seem well deserved, even preordained.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/books/review/25childrens/25childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 376, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/25/books/review/25childrens/25childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 376}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/books/review/25childrens/25childrens-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 641, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/books/review/25childrens/25childrens-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1225, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/books/review/25childrens/25childrens-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/25/books/review/25childrens/25childrens-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/25/books/review/25childrens/25childrens-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bear Extraordinaire', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-25T15:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0fbad344-f6bb-5ec5-8f63-45f521c7ad62', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0fbad344-f6bb-5ec5-8f63-45f521c7ad62'}, {'abstract': 'The time for readers to help choose the next Big City Book Club selection has arrived.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/25/time-to-throw-the-book-at-us/', 'snippet': 'The time for readers to help choose the next Big City Book Club selection has arrived.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Comments on this post for the next Big City Book Club reading have been closed. The chosen book is “The Island at the Center of the World: The Epic Story of Dutch Manhattan and the Forgotten Colony That Shaped America” by Russell Shorto.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multim