/www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/books/review/welcome-to-braggsville-by-t-geronimo-johnson.html', 'snippet': 'College students head South to stage a misbegotten intervention in this darkly comic novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you imagine a satirical “The Indian Princess,” James Nelson Barker’s 1808 libretto about Pocahontas, or a macabre “E. T. the Extra-Terrestrial,” you might come close to T. Geronimo Johnson’s second novel, “Welcome to Braggsville.” This is a prototypical encounter narrative, rippling with irony, in which four Berkeley students visit Braggsville, a segregated Georgia town, population 712, smaller than their freshman class. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01BENJAMIN/01BENJAMIN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01BENJAMIN/01BENJAMIN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 737}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01BENJAMIN/01BENJAMIN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 835, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01BENJAMIN/01BENJAMIN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1844, 'width': 1502, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01BENJAMIN/01BENJAMIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01BENJAMIN/01BENJAMIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 7