iz Alderman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alderman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3436fe12-ee0b-54f8-97de-e5ac8ec0b856', 'word_count': 669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3436fe12-ee0b-54f8-97de-e5ac8ec0b856'}, {'abstract': 'Cush Jumbo brings her solo show “Josephine and I” to New York, playing the American performer Josephine Baker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/theater/josephine-baker-a-british-view.html', 'snippet': 'Cush Jumbo brings her solo show “Josephine and I” to New York, playing the American performer Josephine Baker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cush Jumbo is a name you don’t soon forget. Over the past couple of years, Ms. Jumbo, a British actress, has built a young career almost as unusual. In an acclaimed cross-gender revival of “Julius Caesar,” she played Mark Antony to very good reviews in London, then New York. This fall she starred on Broadway opposite Hugh Jackman in a modern play, “The River.” Now, to show some real range, she brings her solo show “Josephine and I” to New York, playing the American performer Josephine Baker, who became a star in Paris. The show, which begins previews on Feb. 27 and opens on March 10, juxtaposes Baker’s fabled career with a more personal story about struggling to get started as a performer. (Through April 5 at Joe’s Pub at the Public Theater, 425 Lafayette Street; 212-539-9778)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/arts/01GUIDE7/01GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/arts/01GUIDE7/01GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 656}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 