Spain, Ukraine, and Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photos from South Africa, Spain, Ukraine, and Afghanistan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Feb. 25, 2015 — Pictures of the Day', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-25T22:05:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2f5a529-7e68-5a4e-a93e-227b1f2d26b9', 'word_count': 13, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2f5a529-7e68-5a4e-a93e-227b1f2d26b9'}, {'abstract': 'The Nobel literary laureate’s latest novel focuses on the narrator’s aunt, an obstetrician who switches to an abortionist enforcing China’s one-child policy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/26/books/review-in-mo-yans-frog-a-chinese-abortionist-embodies-state-power.html', 'snippet': 'The Nobel literary laureate’s latest novel focuses on the narrator’s aunt, an obstetrician who switches to an abortionist enforcing China’s one-child policy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Chinese writer Mo Yan won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2012 and was warmly lauded by the Communist government, he became one of the most reviled winners in the history of that great honor. Among the more benign accusations lobbed at him was that he was undeserving. Herta Müller, a fellow literary laureate, called the choice “a catastrophe”;  Salman Rushdie called him “a patsy” for not supporting the jailed Chinese Nobel laureate and dissident Liu Xiaobo; and he was labeled an apologist for not denouncing the government. By the time he made his acceptance speech, Mr. Mo said he felt as if he were watching a play about someone else.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/arts/26BOOK/26BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 921, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/26/arts/26BOOK/26BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 921}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/arts/26BOOK/26BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 668, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/arts/26BOOK/26BOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1335, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/arts/26BOOK/26BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/26/arts/26BOOK/26BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/arts/26BOOK/26BOOK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In Mo Yan’s ‘Frog,’ a Chinese Abortionist Embodies State Power', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Abortionist Who Embodies State Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mo Yan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldblatt, Howard (1939- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Frog (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-25T22:09:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/309687ae-b6fc-599b-af68-9a7287886c9c', 'word_count': 984, 'uri': 'nyt://article/309687ae-b6fc-599b-af68-9a7287886c9c'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Szot used his powerful baritone in songs by Antônio Carlos Jobim and Irving Berlin during his tribute to Frank Sinatra.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/26/arts/music/review-paulo-szots-commanding-take-on-bossa-nova-at-54-below.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Szot used his powerful baritone in songs by Antônio Carlos Jobim and Irving Berlin during his tribute to Frank Sinatra.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A wrenching adjustment is required to accept performances of classic bossa nova songs sung in a voice more commanding than an intimate near whisper. Even Frank Sinatra, who recorded two albums of songs mostly composed by bossa nova’s founding father, Antônio Carlos Jobim, shed his swinging ring-a-ding-ding persona to adopt the murmur of an enraptured lover.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Paulo Szot’s Commanding Take on Bossa Nova at 54 Below', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bossy Bossa Nova', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '54 Below (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Szot, Paulo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-25T22:13:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/048d6c7f-5398-5f22-bd22-b7a4e7bccd62', 'word_count': 295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/048d6c7f-5398-5f22-bd22-b7a4e7bccd62'}, {'abstract': 'The top-ranked McIlroy, 25, is the new face of golf, and could remain so unless Tiger Woods regains his championship form when he returns to competition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/26/sports/golf/rory-mcilroy-says-he-can-handle-being-that-guy-in-golf-this-time.html', 'snippet': 'The top-ranked McIlroy, 25, is the new face of golf, and could remain so unless Tiger Woods regains his championship form when he returns to competition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PALM BEACH GARDENS, Fla. — On the 17th tee of his pro-am round at the Honda Classic on Wednesday, Rory McIlroy struck up a conversation with Mike Collis, an executive officer in the Coast Guard who served as his caddie for the hole. For about 15 minutes, they spoke animatedly about strength training, a shared passion.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/sports/26golf/26golf-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/26/sports/26golf/26golf-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 372}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None,