015-02-25T23:07:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim McKeough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKeough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91a8981f-0a90-5f7b-a7ee-4dc56caf01be', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91a8981f-0a90-5f7b-a7ee-4dc56caf01be'}, {'abstract': 'The annual Orchid Show at the New York Botanical Garden will display the flowers arranged in hanging baskets conceived as chandeliers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/26/garden/the-new-york-botanical-gardens-13th-annual-orchid-show.html', 'snippet': 'The annual Orchid Show at the New York Botanical Garden will display the flowers arranged in hanging baskets conceived as chandeliers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Starting Saturday, a profusion of frilly blooms will take the edge off winter in the New York Botanical Garden’s annual Orchid Show. Arranged in hanging baskets conceived as chandeliers, the blossoms will be “high and low and as far as the eye can see,” said Todd Forrest, the garden’s Arthur Ross vice president for horticulture and living collections. More than 30,000 orchid species grow in the wild, Mr. Forrest said, which makes them something like the beetles of plant life. Asked for another fun fact, he mentioned a variety that resembles female wasps and is pollinated with the assistance of male wasps buzzing from plant to plant in their futile attempts to mate.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/garden/26CURR4/26CURR4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/26/garden/26CURR4/26CURR4-ar