 Years later, he had our mother co-sign a business loan. When the business failed, my mother was sued for $3 million she didn’t have. After she died of cancer, my father fought the suit for many years. At 93, he settled for a $1.1 million loss. Unable to live his accustomed lifestyle, he approached my brother with the expectation that my brother would supplement his income. When my brother refused, my father sold the apartment and kept the $600,000. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01ethicists/01ethicists-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01ethicists/01ethicists-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01ethicists/01ethicists-jumbo-v3.png', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01ethicists/01ethicists-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 1410, 'width': 1424, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01ethicists/01ethicists-thumbStandard-v3.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01ethicists/01ethicists-thumbStandard-v3.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01ethicists/01ethicists-thumbLarge-v3.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can I Stay Out of an Estate Dispute?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicists', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can I Stay Out of an Estate Dispute?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wills and Estates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloom, Amy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yoshino, Kenji', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Rifle Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shafer, Jack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T09:58:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Bloom, Jack Shafer and Kenji Yoshino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bloom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shafer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Kenji', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yoshino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a3c99b0-89f2-5cf1-b7c9-184fc0a3a038', 'word_count': 1579, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a3c99b0-89f2-5cf1-b7c9-184fc0a3a038'}, {'abstract': 'In Louisiana, a wealthy white lawyer has spent 15 years turning the Whitney Plantation into a museum dedicated to telling the story of slavery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/magazine/building-the-first-slave-museum-in-america.html', 'snippet': 'In Louisiana, a wealthy white lawyer has spent 15 years turning the Whitney Plantation into a museum dedicated to telling the story of slavery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Louisiana’s River Road runs northwest from New Orleans to Baton Rouge, its two lanes snaking some 100 miles along the Mississippi and through a contradictory stretch of America. Flat and fertile, with oaks webbed in Spanish moss, the landscape stands in defiance of the numerous oil refineries and petrochemical plants that threaten its natural splendor. In the rust-scabbed towns of clapboard homes, you are reminded that Louisiana is the eighth-poorest state in the nation. Yet in the lush sugar plantations that crop up every couple of miles, you can glimpse the excess that defined the region before the Civil War. Some are still active, with expansive fields yielding 13 million tons of sugar cane a year. Others stand in states of elegant rot. But most conspicuous are those that have been restored for tourists, transporting them into a world of bygone Southern grandeur — one in which mint juleps, manicured gardens and hoop skirts are emphasized over the fact that such grandeur was made possible by the enslavement of black human beings.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1023, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2047, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS/01slave_ss-slide-7QVS-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Building the First Slavery Museum in America', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Peculiar Institution', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Amsden, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Whitney Plantation (Wallace, La)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Louisiana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cummings, John (1937- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery (Historical)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T09:58:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Amsden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amsden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15c9d89c-6621-559c-ab74-86b141410e81', 'word_count': 5270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15c9d89c-6621-559c-ab74-86b141410e81'}, {'abstract': 'How many bags of old stuff could you comfortably get rid of? Would ridding yourself of clutter make you feel happier or calmer? Is your clutter just tangible objects, or is there intangible clutter in your life as well?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/26/do-you-need-to-de-clutter-your-life/', 'snippet': 'How many bags of old stuff could you comfortably get rid of? Would ridding yourself of clutter make you feel happier or calmer? Is your clutter just tangible objects, or is there intangible clutter in your life as well?', 'lead_paragraph': 'How many bags of old stuff could you comfortably get rid of? Do you think ridding yourself of it would make you feel happier or calmer? Is your clutter just tangible objects, or is there intangible clutter in your life as well?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/opinion/16druckerman-LN/16druckerman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/16/opinion/16druckerman-LN/16druckerman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/opinion/16druckerman-LN/16druckerman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/16/opinion/16druckerm