4c8-a9b1-56d543a707bd', 'word_count': 1352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92b3d035-81ea-54c8-a9b1-56d543a707bd'}, {'abstract': 'A hit movie has a lot to say about franchises adapted from literary sources.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/movies/unexpected-lessons-from-fifty-shades-of-grey.html', 'snippet': 'A hit movie has a lot to say about franchises adapted from literary sources.', 'lead_paragraph': '“A man watches a movie, and the critic must acknowledge that he is that man.” So wrote Robert Warshow, almost exactly 60 years ago, setting down what would become an unofficial motto for the profession. The idea that movies and other forms of popular culture could be subjected to serious critical scrutiny was a new and controversial notion in American intellectual circles at the time, and Warshow wanted to make clear that any such criticism would have to take account of its origins in everyday experience. Wherever your thoughts and judgments might take you, you always start out as a consumer, a member of the audience, a fan.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/arts/01CROSSCUTSJP1/01CROSSCUTSJP1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/arts/01CROSSCUTSJP1/01CROSSCUTSJP1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/arts/01CROSSCUTSJP1/01CROSSCUTSJP1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/arts/01CROSSCUTSJP1/01CROSSCUTSJP1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'super