, 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcbe8e56-8549-5406-86b9-e81c8e483189', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcbe8e56-8549-5406-86b9-e81c8e483189'}, {'abstract': 'A few miles from the Adriatic Sea, the city has arcade-lined piazzas, wine bars brimming with locals and spectacular Byzantine mosaics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/travel/ravenna-italy-glittering-still.html', 'snippet': 'A few miles from the Adriatic Sea, the city has arcade-lined piazzas, wine bars brimming with locals and spectacular Byzantine mosaics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the heart of Ravenna, I saw some extraordinary fashion: men sporting leopard-spotted tights and velvet cloaks; women wearing dresses of shimmering silk, jewels gleaming at their ears and throats. But these stylish people were not strolling the Piazza del Popolo or sipping prosecco at a cafe; they were rendered in mosaic, created from tiny cubes of colored glass affixed over 1,500 years ago. Sparkling from the walls of eight early Christian monuments, the town’s Byzantine mosaics form the largest such collection in Western Europe.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01RAVENNA1/01RAVENNA1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01RAVENNA1/01RAVENNA1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01RAVENNA1/01RAVENNA1-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01RAVENNA1/01RAVENNA1-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01RAVENNA1/01RAVENNA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01RAVENNA1/01RAVENNA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01RAVENNA1/01RAVENNA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ravenna, Italy, Glittering Still     ', 'kicker': 'Overnighter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Glittering Still, With Stories to Tell', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mah, Ann', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Byzantine Civilization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ravenna (Italy)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T14:28:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Mah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/350404d7-beb0-53f0-9eb6-79558b5cb17a', 'word_count': 1001, 'uri': 'nyt://article/350404d7-beb0-53f0-9eb6-79558b5cb17a'}, {'abstract': 'Yes, Montpellier was the home of my husband’s family. But now I viewed it as a place of beauty and culture, not just an obligation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/travel/in-france-a-visit-with-the-in-laws-finally-becomes-a-vacation.html', 'snippet': 'Yes, Montpellier was the home of my husband’s family. But now I viewed it as a place of beauty and culture, not just an obligation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Montpellier, cypresses grow as high as the bell tower of Eglise Ste.-Thérèse. Whelks, caught that morning from the nearby Mediterranean Sea, are sold by the dozen with a side of aioli at the covered market Halles Castellane. The Musée Fabre, a national museum that houses works by the likes of Delacroix and Courbet in a grand 18th-century chateau, manages to feel inviting, not intimidating. Even its visitors, sipping their espresso at the cafe Insensé on the front lawn, look more like leisured houseguests than the sort of wearied tourists you see at the Louvre.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01MONTPELLIER4/01MONTPELLIER4-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01MONTPELLIER4/01MONTPELLIER4-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/travel/01MONTPELLIER4/01MONTPELLIER4-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 's