 educated, wanted to go to college, and a precious few universities were willing, very quietly, to help them pay for it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/nyregion/financialaidsub/financialaidsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/26/nyregion/financialaidsub/financialaidsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/nyregion/financialaidsub/financialaidsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/nyregion/financialaidsub/financialaidsub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/nyregion/financialaidsub/financialaidsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/26/nyregion/financialaidsub/financialaidsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/nyregion/financialaidsub/financialaidsub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Financial Aid for Undocumented Students Is Losing Its Stigma', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Financial Aid for Undocumented Students Is Losing Its Stigma', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Aid (Education)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T15:44:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60f89622-ab54-5ba1-be2c-0f349a423c3d', 'word_count': 1227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60f89622-ab54-5ba1-be2c-0f349a423c3d'}, {'abstract': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/26/classical-playlist-haydn-poul-ruders-vaughan-williams-and-more/', 'snippet': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'lead_paragraph': 'POUL RUDERS: ‘Nightshade Trilogy’Capricorn, Oliver Knussen, conductor; Odense Symphony Orchestra, Paul Mann and Scott Yoo, conductors(Bridge 9433; one CD)When the masterly Danish composer Poul Ruders wrote “Nightshade,” a work for 10 instruments, in 1986, he did not know it would be the first of three related scores written over 18 years, his “Nightshade Trilogy,” as he later called it. “The Second Nightshade,” a Symphonic Nocturne for chamber orchestra, came in 1991. “Final Nightshade,” an Adagio of the Night for full orchestra, ended the series in 2003. All three works explore extreme contrasts of high and low, loud and soft, fast and slow, and such, as the composer explains in liner notes. But nothing can explain the cosmic allure, audacious inventiveness and gripping strangeness of Mr. Ruders’s ingenious music. This recording album brings together three recordings of the three works made over 17 years, conducted respectively by Oliver Knussen (with the Capricorn ensemble), Paul Mann and Scott Yoo (with the Odense Symphony Orchestra). (Anthony Tommasini)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Classical Playlist: Haydn, Poul Ruders, Vaughan Williams and More', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T15:46:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f44551e-c2c1-5dc0-bbfe-a5b445251a3e', 'word_count': 622, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f44551e-c2c1-5dc0-bbfe-a5b445251a3e'}, {'abstract': 'The Conservative Political Action Conference has added question–and-answer periods to satisfy a desire for White House aspirants to move beyond the red meat of their stump speeches.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/27/us/politics/cpac-republicans.html', 'snippet': 'The Conservative Political Action Conference has added question–and-answer periods to satisfy a desire for White House aspirants to move beyond the red meat of their stump speeches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Prepared remarks are out. Answering tough questions is in. And there should be plenty of them — especially for a candidate named Bush.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/26/us/26ACTIVISTSweb/26ACTIVISTSweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/26/us/26ACTIVISTSweb/26ACTIVISTSweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargehei