: '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rappeport', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9df02e36-2fb9-58b0-8c26-71dd3c479a36', 'word_count': 935, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9df02e36-2fb9-58b0-8c26-71dd3c479a36'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: The disgraced mother of the traitor, Paul Ferdonnet, flees home in 1940.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/26/1940-aged-mother-of-traitor-flees/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: The disgraced mother of the traitor, Paul Ferdonnet, flees home in 1940.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NIORT (DEUX-SÈVRES) — The aged mother of Paul Ferdonnet, a broadcaster for the Germans, who is known as ‘‘The traitor of Stuttgart,’’ today [Feb. 26] sought shelter and seclusion in a convent on the outskirts of Niort. She was grief-stricken by the appearance on public edifices of official posters that declared Ferdonnet together with another French broadcaster named Obrecht traitors to the nation. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Feb. 27, 1940', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1940: Aged Mother of ‘Traitor’ Flees', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Stuttgart (Germany)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T15:52:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname