90ddee5'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Three castaways are rescued after four years on an uninhabited island by the American ship, Wanderer, in 1890.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/02/26/1890-modern-crusoes/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Three castaways are rescued after four years on an uninhabited island by the American ship, Wanderer, in 1890.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Despatches received here to- day [Feb. 26] from Vancouver Island state that three Englishmen landed there from the American ship Wanderer, which had rescued them from the uninhabited island of San Alesandro, where they had been for four years. They state that in October, 1885, they took passage on the Japanese vessel Matsuo Marie, at Hakodatt, for Anomari, and while crossing the Straits of Issugaru a gale was encountered, which blew them out of their course. For eighty days they were tossed about and finally the vessel was driven ashore on the island of San Alesandro. The three castaways lived chiefly on the fruit which grew on the island. They suffered considerable privations, and had all given way to despair when the Wanderer rescued them. — The New York Herald, European Edition, Feb. 27, 1890', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1890: Modern Crusoes', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rescues', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vancouver Island (British Columbia)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T16:49:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlen