utation as a “sex pest,” Jimmy Savile, the British television personality, had freewheeling access in a hospital where he sexually abused 60 people over more than two decades, according to a report released Thursday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Hospital Ignored Sexual Abuse Complaints Against Jimmy Savile, Inquiry Finds', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain: Report Details Abuser’s Actions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castle, Stephen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Savile, Jimmy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T18:35:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Castle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cad70357-423b-5318-a2ee-7149f4b1f3a0', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cad70357-423b-5318-a2ee-7149f4b1f3a0'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the second day of the CPAC conference in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2015/02/26/first-draft-focus-a-day-at-cpac/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the second day of the CPAC conference in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'First Draft Focus: A Day at CPAC', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T18:36:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By First Draft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'First', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d5220fb-48ff-583e-b049-598753435ab2', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d5220fb-48ff-583e-b049-598753435ab2'}, {'abstract': 'The film imagines Los Angeles as an inferno of narcissism, greed and sexual perversity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/27/movies/review-david-cronenberg-dissects-hollywood-in-maps-to-the-stars.html', 'snippet': 'The film imagines Los Angeles as an inferno of narcissism, greed and sexual perversity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the Oscars have left you with a residual hatred of Hollywood or a renewed appreciation of Julianne Moore, “Maps to the Stars” may be just what you need. Suavely directed by David Cronenberg from an elegantly waspish script by Bruce Wagner, it belongs to the venerable tradition of movieland self-loathing. The film, tipping its hat to Nathanael West’s “The Day of the Locust” and Mr. Wagner’s own novels, imagines Los Angeles as an inferno of narcissism, greed and sexual perversity. The radiant sunshine has a sinister glow, and the blossoms on the trees are surely poisonous. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27MAPJP1/27MAPJP1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27MAPJP1/27MAPJP1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27MAPJP1/27MAPJP1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27MAPJP1/27MAPJP1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1197, 'width': 1796, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27MAPJP1/27MAPJP1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27MAPJP1/27MAPJP1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27MAPJP1/27MAPJP1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: David Cronenberg Dissects Hollywood in ‘Maps to the Stars’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grand Delusions and Self-Loathing in La-La Land', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cronenberg, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Julianne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pattinson, Robert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Maps to the Stars (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T18:43:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/67494989-4ddf-5d44-a748-8e43d84a2007', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67494989-4ddf-5d44-a748-8e43d84a2007'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Beatty’s novel is told from the view of a young isolated black man born on outskirts of Los Angeles who lands before the Supreme Court in a race trial appeal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/27/books/review-the-sellout-paul-beattys-biting-satire-on-black-american-culture.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Beatty’s novel is told from the view of a young isolated black man born on outskirts of Los Angeles who lands before the Supreme Court in a race trial appeal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Beatty is the author of four novels and two books of poetry, all of them worthwhile. But the book of his that I return to most is one he edited. It’s called “Hokum: An Anthology of African-American Humor” (2006).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27BOOK/27BOOK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 878, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27BOOK/27BOOK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 878}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27BOOK/27BOOK-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 701, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27BOOK/27BOOK-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1723, 'width': 1178, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27BOOK/27BOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width'