'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Eli', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yokley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ec42fba-2de5-5bcc-a1d5-eb8d7606e960', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ec42fba-2de5-5bcc-a1d5-eb8d7606e960'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition of early and late works casts Louise Nevelson more as a restless searcher than a high priestess of wood reliefs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/27/arts/design/louise-nevelson-collage-and-assemblage.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition of early and late works casts Louise Nevelson more as a restless searcher than a high priestess of wood reliefs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The sculptor Louise Nevelson took up a fair amount of space in New York in the 1960s and early ’70s. She was one of the most prominent artists of her generation, known for her imperious personality and a penchant for false eyelashes, heavy jewelry and chinchilla capes. More important were her forward-looking wood reliefs, painted entirely black or white, which linked the found-object aesthetic of assemblage to the clean lines and orderly sequences of Minimalism (and also presaged the all-black assemblage sculptures of Rashid Johnson). But by the time she died at age 88 in 1988, the art world had moved on.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27NEVELSONGALLERY1/27NEVELSONGALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27NEVELSONGALLERY1/27NEVELSONGALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', '