alue': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-26T23:01:42+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By SHREEYA SINHA', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shreeya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SINHA', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/e22f82e3-9b64-5dd1-b978-9a0d804bae4d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/e22f82e3-9b64-5dd1-b978-9a0d804bae4d'}, {'abstract': 'The Gagosian Gallery has mounted not one but two museum-quality exhibitions in its space on West 21st Street in Chelsea and at its flagship gallery on the Upper East Side.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/27/arts/design/review-the-artists-studio-from-refuge-to-gallery-in-shows-at-the-gagosian.html', 'snippet': 'The Gagosian Gallery has mounted not one but two museum-quality exhibitions in its space on West 21st Street in Chelsea and at its flagship gallery on the Upper East Side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In these days of post-studio, post-Internet artistry, of nomadic careers and collaborative cohorts, the artist’s studio can sometimes seem like a thing of the past, a relic from that bygone era before the supposed death of the author and the age of mechanical reproduction. But even if young artists work increasingly out of backpacks, on laptops or in tandem, alone time in a fairly private work space remains an essential condition for creativity.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27SUBGAGOSIAN1/27SUBGAGOSIAN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 718, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27SUBGAGOSIAN1/