Bank', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corkery, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CIT Group Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of the Comptroller of the Currency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T00:58:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Corkery', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Corkery', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51d465ae-46da-5653-812a-16cfe71b7229', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51d465ae-46da-5653-812a-16cfe71b7229'}, {'abstract': 'Robin Campillo’s film stars Olivier Rabourdin as a French businessman and Kirill Emelyanov as the young man who turns an assignation into a home invasion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/27/movies/review-in-eastern-boys-a-train-station-pickup-gone-wrong.html', 'snippet': 'Robin Campillo’s film stars Olivier Rabourdin as a French businessman and Kirill Emelyanov as the young man who turns an assignation into a home invasion.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Eastern Boys,” the most compelling film to be shown at last year’s Rendez-Vous With French Cinema series at Lincoln Center and just now getting an American release, is impossible to shoehorn into a generic box. This story of a lonely middle-aged businessman whose involvement with a scrawny rent boy he meets at the Gare du Nord in Paris explores interlocking themes of sexuality, immigration and power dynamics with a cleareyed sensitivity and refuses to demonize even its shadiest characters.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27EASTER/27EASTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 339, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27EASTER/27EASTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 339}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27EASTER/27EASTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 578, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27EASTER/27EASTER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1155, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27EASTER/27EASTER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27EASTER/27EASTER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/arts/27EASTER/27EASTER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Eastern Boys,’ a Train-Station Pickup Gone Wrong', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lust and the Social Pecking Order', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campillo, Robin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Eastern Boys (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T00:59:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/554bb256-04c4-5e1b-a85c-a287423feebd', 'word_count': 719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/554bb256-04c4-5e1b-a85c-a287423feebd'}, {'abstract': 'To help combat online piracy, the music industry’s global trade group has decided on Fridays as the official day to make new albums available.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/27/business/media/global-deal-to-release-new-albums-on-fridays.html', 'snippet': 'To help combat online piracy, the music industry’s global trade group has decided on Fridays as the official day to make new albums available.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For music fans in Britain, new albums come out on Mondays. In the United States, they typically hit stores on Tuesdays. In Japan, it’s Wednesdays.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Global Deal to Release New Albums on Fridays', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Global Deal to Release New Albums on Fridays', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audio Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of the Phonographic Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T01:05:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byli