bnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01tip/01mag-01tip.t_CA0-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/magazine/01tip/01mag-01tip.t_CA0-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Remember People’s Names', 'kicker': 'Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Remember People’s Names', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wollan, Malia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Personal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T11:58:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8eeefe3-9eec-5f0e-87f9-7db12cb81367', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8eeefe3-9eec-5f0e-87f9-7db12cb81367'}, {'abstract': 'What was once an authentic response to “thank you” has come to mean something more sinister.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/magazine/when-did-youre-welcome-become-a-gloat.html', 'snippet': 'What was once an authentic response to “thank you” has come to mean something more sinister.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1947, the Friendship Train left Los Angeles bound for Europe. The train crossed North America, picking up boxcars of Colorado wheat, Nebraska beans and Wisconsin condensed milk. By the time it reached New York, the Friendship Train had amassed $40 million in donations of food, clothing 