were too raw.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01OPENBOOK/1OPENBOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01OPENBOOK/1OPENBOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01OPENBOOK/1OPENBOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 770, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01OPENBOOK/1OPENBOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1503, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01OPENBOOK/01OPENBOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01OPENBOOK/01OPENBOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01OPENBOOK/01OPENBOOK-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Transcending War', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Transcending War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Green on Blue (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ackerman, Elliot', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T15:41:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bfef921-e4d3-5006-8a67-035e6532dc9c', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bfef921-e4d3-5006-8a67-035e6532dc9c'}, {'abstract': 'A young soldier from Afghanistan narrates this first novel by an American who served several tours of duty there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/books/review/elliot-ackermans-green-on-blue.html', 'snippet': 'A young soldier from Afghanistan narrates this first novel by an American who served several tours of duty there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1971, with the war in Vietnam ferociously stalemated, David Halberstam published a biography of North Vietnam’s leader, Ho Chi Minh, who had died two years earlier. Books about the enemy are typical in times of war. What was atypical about “Ho” was its fawning tone: “In his lifetime,” Halberstam wrote, “Ho had not only liberated his own country . . . he had touched the culture and soul of his enemy.” It’s a pretty lousy biography by any measure, every page a basket of freshly picked cherries, but for many historians on the right it’s still viewed as traitorous rot — as if Ernie Pyle had written, in 1943, “Goebbels: A Celebration.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01BISSELLSUB/0301-bks-Bissell-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'