{'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c03962e3-ae1d-581a-9828-e9b95ff0b7aa', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c03962e3-ae1d-581a-9828-e9b95ff0b7aa'}, {'abstract': 'Irvine Welsh’s tale of abduction and mutual obsession.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/books/review/irvine-welshs-the-sex-lives-of-siamese-twins.html', 'snippet': 'Irvine Welsh’s tale of abduction and mutual obsession.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Put down the novel and back away from the doughnuts. It is not advisable to read Irvine Welsh’s 13th book, “The Sex Lives of Siamese Twins,” and snack simultaneously. Because Welsh, known for his gleeful depictions of the depraved degenerates who populate Scotland’s seediest hollows, has turned his attention to one of his adopted homes, Miami Beach, that “sun-drenched refuge for strutting grotesques and desperate narcissists.” This is not Welsh’s first Rollerblade around Florida; “Crime” followed a coke-addled Edinburgh detective along the same bright sidewalks. It is, however, his first to be narrated by two Americans. And not just any two Americans. Female ones. This is a man who once successfully narrated a portion of a novel from the point of view of a tapeworm. The question is, in assuming the perspectives of women, has he bitten off more than he can chew?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01WELSH/0301-bks-WELSH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 584, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01WELSH/0301-bks-WELSH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 584}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01WELSH/0301-bks-WELSH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 996, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01WELSH/0301-bks-WELSH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1993, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01WELSH/0301-bks-WELSH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01WELSH/0301-bks-WELSH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01WELSH/0301-bks-WELSH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Irvine Welsh’s ‘The Sex Lives of Siamese Twins’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Miami Vise', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crosley, Sloane', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Welsh, Irvine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T15:50:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sloane Crosley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sloane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crosley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cf0f6bc-1a2c-56f3-9d79-3da95deaa84f', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cf0f6bc-1a2c-56f3-9d79-3da95deaa84f'}, {'abstract': 'A novel traces the changes brought by a baby’s arrival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/books/review/after-birth-by-elisa-albert.html', 'snippet': 'A novel traces the changes brought by a baby’s arrival.', 'lead_paragraph': '“A baby opens you up, is the problem,” explains Ari, who feels she didn’t actually give birth to her 1-year-old son because he was delivered via cesarean section. “There’s before, and there’s after,” she observes. “To live in your body before is one thing. To live in your body after is another.” Which might be the simplest way to communicate the impact of Elisa Albert’s brilliant new novel. “After Birth” cuts open the body of literature on mothering, birth, feminism, female friendship, female hateship — whether academic treatise or poem or novel — and wrenches out something so new we barely recognize it. Wet, red, slimy, alive: a truth baby.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/1TIERCE/1TIERCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/1TIERCE/1TIERCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/1TIERCE/1TIERCE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/1TIERCE/1TIERCE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/1TIERCE/1TIERCE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/1TIERCE/1TIERCE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/1TIERCE/1TIERCE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘After Birth,’ by Elisa Albert', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unexpecting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Albert, Elisa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'After Birth (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T15:50:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Merritt Tierce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Merritt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tierce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f6567cb-239c-579f-bc3c-9a8f022f3ff5', 'word_count': 660, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f6567cb-239c-579f-bc3c-9a8f022f3ff5'}, {'abstract': 'Laura van den Berg’s heroine picks her way across an American wasteland, searching for the mother who abandoned her.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/books/review/laura-van-den-bergs-find-me.html', 'snippet': 'Laura van den Berg’s heroine picks her way across an American wasteland, searching for the mother who abandoned her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joy Jones, the narrator of “Find Me,” Laura van den Berg’s pleasingly strange first novel, has spent her whole life waiting to be found. Born in the winter of 1995, she was left on the steps of a Boston hospital. The nurses gave her a name. Her birthday is only a guess. She grew up in a series of foster and group homes, feeling perpetually unwanted.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01CIURARU/01CIURARU-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01CIURARU/01CIURARU-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01CIURARU/01CIURARU-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 960, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01CIURARU/01CIURARU-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 960, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01CIURARU/01CIURARU-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01CIURARU/01CIURARU-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01CIURARU/01CIURARU-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Laura van den Berg’s ‘Find Me’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Forget Me Not', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ciuraru, Carmela', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van den Berg, Laura', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T15:50:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carmela Ciuraru', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carmela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ciuraru', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/09803989-bc83-5b63-8ef0-28f9469bf7ed', 'word_count': 1224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09803989-bc83-5b63-8ef0-28f9469bf7ed'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Rhodes offers an anecdotal — and technological — history of the Spanish Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/books/review/hell-and-good-company-by-richard-rhodes.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Rhodes offers an anecdotal — and technological — history of the Spanish Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is it possible to speak intelligently about supremely ideological political events from a standpoint that airily dismisses the supremely ideological politics? Richard Rhodes has put this question to a test by writing an aggressively anti-ideological history of one of the most extravagantly ideological events that has ever occurred, the Spanish Civil War of 1936-39. The Spanish war broke out because, on one side, the radically leftist Socialist Party pushed the Republican government ever further to the left, and large parts of the labor movement pushed still more sharply leftward, unto the zones of collectivist and libertarian revolution — and because, on the \xadopposite side, the national army and most of the Catholic Church and other parts of society veered sharply rightward, unto the nether regions of the fascist extreme. And, well, the two sides could not get along. Hitler, Mussolini and Stalin made their own specialized contributions. Three years of this and Generalissimo Francisco Franco became the caudillo of Spain, and wartime atrocities gave way to peacetime atrocities.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01RHODES/01RHODES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01RHODES/01RHODES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01RHODES/01RHODES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01RHODES/01RHODES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1357, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01RHODES/01RHODES-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01RHODES/01RHODES-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/books/review/01RHODES/01RHODES-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Hell and Good Company,’ by Richard Rhodes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Madrid Was Burning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rhodes, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spanish Civil War (1936-39)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T15:51:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Berman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/07c8a24c-a227-5b5b-a1ac-1ae378a3357a', 'word_count': 1196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07c8a24c-a227-5b5b-a1ac-1ae378a3357a'}, {'abstract': 'Senator Marco Rubio was well-received by the CPAC crowd Friday describing his own upbringing as an immigrant success story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2015/02/27/marco-rubio-at-cpac-an-immigrant-success-story/', 'snippet': 'Senator Marco Rubio was well-received by the CPAC crowd Friday describing his own upbringing as an immigrant success story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Best Applause Line:\xa0President Obama has treated “the ayatollah of Iran with more respect than the prime minister of Israel.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/us/politics/27firstdraft-rubio/27firstdraft-rubio-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/27/us/politics/27firstdraft-rubio/27firstdraft-rubio-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/us/politics/27firstdraft-rubio/27firstdraft-rubio-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/us/politics/27firstdraft-rubio/27firstdraft-rubio-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1211, 'width': 1816, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/us/politics/27firstdraft-rubio/27firstdraft-rubio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/02/27/us/politics/27firstdraft-rubio/27firstdraft-rubio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/us/politics/27firstdraft-rubio/27firstdraft-rubio-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Marco Rubio at CPAC: An Immigrant Success Story', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rubio, Marco', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T15:51:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maggie Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/452dc78f-e924-5cb6-b529-b0e2ac2ce8d7', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/452dc78f-e924-5cb6-b529-b0e2ac2ce8d7'}, {'abstract': 'Helen Macdonald, whose memoir “H Is for Hawk” is No. 8 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has become something of a sensation in her native England.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/08/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Helen Macdonald, whose memoir “H Is for Hawk” is No. 8 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has become something of a sensation in her native England.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Massive Wobble: “H Is for Hawk,” Helen Macdonald’s moving memoir about falconry and her father’s death, enters the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 8. Macdonald is hardly the first best-selling memoirist to combine nature and grief: In 2012, Roger Rosenblatt spent a couple of weeks on the extended list with the elegiac “Kayak Morning,” in which he paddles around a Long Island creek and reflects on the death of his 38-year-old daughter, while Cheryl Strayed’s “Wild,” about hiking the Pacific Crest Trail after her mother died, has logged more than two and a half years on the hardcover and paperback lists. (It’s currently No. 3 in paperback, after 100 weeks.) Still, Macdonald was unprepared for the kind of success her book has achieved — particularly in her native England, where it was published last year and has become something of a sensation. “It’s freaking me out a bit!” she told The Belfast Telegraph recently. “There was a moment about two weeks ago when I went to do a book talk. People were queuing up to get books signed, and some were incredibly nervous and had shaking hands. I was horrified! They didn’t see me as a person — they saw me as a place holder for being a ‘writer’ or ‘author’ or some figure that wasn’t really me. That threw me into a massive wobble!” Her experience, she said, “taught me that this relationship with what is technically very much a wild animal is a way of getting in touch with all sorts of elemental things. . . . I was in a really dark place, and the hawk helped me through it.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/08/books/review/08list-macdonald/08list-macdonald-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/08/books/review/08list-macdonald/08list-macdonald-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/08/books/review/08list-macdonald/08list-macdonald-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/08/books/review/08list-macdonald/08list-macdonald-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/08/books/review/08list-macdonald/08list-macdonald-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T15:51:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9535324-d172-5b52-96a5-cde657c5dce2', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9535324-d172-5b52-96a5-cde657c5dce2'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THIEVES OF STATE: Why Corruption Threatens Global Security,  by Sarah Chayes. (Norton, $26.95.) This important book makes the case that acute economic and political corruption causes both social breakdown and violent extremism.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T15:52:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/06291641-dc71-5f8d-a05b-69c80a15f366', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06291641-dc71-5f8d-a05b-69c80a15f366'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE SIGNAL AND THE NOISE: Why So Many Predictions Fail — but Some Don’t,  by Nate Silver. (Penguin, $17.) Silver has developed groundbreaking methods for predicting the performance of baseball players and the outcome of political races; here he assumes the role of public statistician as he investigates the world of forecasting, from finance to weather to epidemiology. Arguing for a sharper recognition of “the difference between what we know and what we think we know,” he proposes strategies for distinguishing true signals from a universe of noisy data.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-27T15:52:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff53ce87-ac94-5d4f-8b98-371bd757fa52', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff53ce87-ac94-5d4f-8b98-371bd757fa52'}, {'abstract': 'Johnson, a hard-throwing pitcher who showed flashes of greatness in 1947, struggled with injuries and off-the-field troubles, finishing his major league career with only 27 wins.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/27/sports/baseball/don-johnson-a-yankees-sensation-who-fizzled-out-dies-at-88.html', 'snippet': 'Johnson, a hard-throwing pitcher who showed flashes of greatness in 1947, struggled with injuries and off-the-field troubles, finishing his major league career with only 27 wins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not many baseball fans — not even the die-hard Yankee variety — will remember the right-handed pitcher Don Johnson, but he was the Yankees’ most promising rookie 68 spring trainings ago. That was 1947.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit'