stname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Higgins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/389c128f-aa83-5a49-8609-b75ff024dbda', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/389c128f-aa83-5a49-8609-b75ff024dbda'}, {'abstract': 'Rick Santorum urged CPAC attendees to support a presidential candidate with deep experience and a stiff backbone on foreign policy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2015/02/27/rick-santorum-talking-tough-says-u-s-needs-wartime-president/', 'snippet': 'Rick Santorum urged CPAC attendees to support a presidential candidate with deep experience and a stiff backbone on foreign policy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Best Applause Line: The former Pennsylvania senator received the best response for his tough talk on foreign policy: “We don’t need a weatherman in chief, we need a commander in chief to run this government.”Crickets: He attempted a joke about President Obama’s place of birth, joking that the Kenyan government had discovered that Mr. Obama was “actually born in America.” Nope.Biggest Pander: In a bit of country-when-country-wasn’t-cool, Mr. Santorum noted that he was against Common Core when he ran for president in 2012. “And today I’m still not for Common Core,” he said.Toughest Question: Not a one.Mood in the Room: Mr. Santorum had the unfortunate luck of following Senator Rand Paul, which meant that dozens of Mr. Paul’s libertarian-leaning followers exited the room during the opening minutes of his speech.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/27/us/politics/27firstdraft-santorum-selfie/27firstdraft-santorum-selfie-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/27/us/politics/27firstdraft-