{}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Different Destinies in One Place’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Staten Island Ferry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-02-28T02:57:14+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/7a171ac6-27fe-5db6-90ca-acf5933c58ad', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/7a171ac6-27fe-5db6-90ca-acf5933c58ad'}, {'abstract': 'Federal action is needed to ensure that people with criminal convictions are not walled off from the job market.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/28/opinion/remove-unfair-barriers-to-employment.html', 'snippet': 'Federal action is needed to ensure that people with criminal convictions are not walled off from the job market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About 70 million Americans have criminal histories that may prevent them from ever finding a job, even for arrests or minor convictions that occurred in the distant past. Fourteen states and about 100 cities and counties have tried to help by barring public agencies — and in some instances, private businesses — from asking job candidates about criminal convictions until later in the application process, when they have had a chance to prove their qualifications. These measures help, but federal action is needed to ensure that people with criminal convictions are not unfairly walled off from the job market.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/02/28/opinion/28sat3web/28sat3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/02/28/opinion/28sat3we