f73-aada-cd7e675b315e'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition at the Samuel Dorsky Museum of Art looks back on Videofreex, a 10-member collective that lived and worked together from 1969 to 1978 and pushed the boundaries of a new artistic medium.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/01/nyregion/videofreex-the-art-of-guerrilla-television-at-the-samuel-dorsky-museum-of-art.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition at the Samuel Dorsky Museum of Art looks back on Videofreex, a 10-member collective that lived and worked together from 1969 to 1978 and pushed the boundaries of a new artistic medium.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, smartphone users take for granted that they can record what they choose and circulate the results on a host of social media platforms. Rewind half a century and the public’s ability to produce and disseminate video was nearly nonexistent. In the late 1960s, television was controlled by three commercial networks that broadcast a prescribed selection of content.\xa0', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE1/01ARTSWE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE1/01ARTSWE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE1/01ARTSWE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE1/01ARTSWE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1315, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit'